2 CONC ding in Yreka, Siskiyou Co. CA, in the Shasta Valley. It's unclear why Levi made the move west, but the Shasta Valley area had boomed in the wake of the discovery of gold and there were more stage lines out of Yreka than any other
2 CONC  community in the state. Levi's occupation in 1860 continued to be farming, but he and Eliza shared their dwelling with Peter Batterton, a former Bloomington merchant, and Peter's son, W. D. Batterton was also in Yreka as a mercha
2 CONC nt. Whether the Battertons' presence is coincidental to Levi Burch's move west is unknown. By 1867, Levi was on the voter's rolls of Sonoma Valley, CA and was employed as a laborer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Levi's movements between 1867 and 1880 are not known, but it would appear his western venture was not a profitable one. By 1880, Levi had returned to Indiana and was working for his brother George as a farmhand. He was a widower b
