1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A051865 Ancestor #: A051865] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Robert Harris, of Brown’s Cove
2 CONT * Birth:	March 08, 1741, St. Martin Parish, Hanover County, Province of Virginia
2 CONT * Death:	November 1809 (68) , Surry County, North Carolina, United States
2 CONT * Burial: unknown 
2 CONT * Parents: Major Robert Harris, of Albemarle County and Mourning Glenn
2 CONT * Spouse:  Lucretia Brown (d. 1806) - married 1762.  Daughter of Benjamin Brown & Sarah Thompson.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Curator Note:''' Identity verified as '''Robert Harris''' via primary evidence, including his 1806 Surry County Will signed as "Robert Harris" and his '''DAR Patriot record (#A051865)'''. The middle name "Overton" is a later g
2 CONC enealogical insertion intended to link him to his grandmother, Temperance Overton, but is unsupported by 18th-century documentation. To maintain archival integrity and prevent the spread of unverified names, this profile retains t
2 CONC he name recorded in contemporary records, noting "Overton" only as a common but unproven family tradition.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Child of (unknown partner) and Lucretia Brown 
2 CONT # Bezaleel Brown (b. 1760, Albemarle Co., VA); d. c. 1778; (Deceased prior to 1806 Will).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children of Robert Harris and Lucretia Brown ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elijah Harris (b. 1762, Albemarle Co., VA); m. Kary Forkner; (Executor; Inherited farm and house).
2 CONT # Nancy Harris (b. 1763, Albemarle Co., VA); m. William Thompson (Boon?); (1806 Will Bequest).
2 CONT # Mourning Harris (b. 1764, Albemarle Co., VA); m. William Henry Burch; (1806 Will Bequest; named "Mourning Birch").
2 CONT # Tyree Rhodes Harris (b. 1765, Albemarle Co., VA); m. Mary Ann Hunt; (Previously endowed 100 VA pounds).
2 CONT # Sarah Harris (b. c. 1767, Albemarle Co., VA); m. (Unknown) Blackwell; (Deceased; Heirs mentioned in 1806).
2 CONT # Bernice Harris (b. c. 1769, Albemarle Co., VA); m. Henrietta Rodes; (Previously endowed 100 VA pounds).
2 CONT # Lucretia Harris (b. c. 1771, Albemarle Co., VA); m. William Blackwell; (Previously endowed 100 VA pounds).
2 CONT # Lucy Harris (b. c. 1775, Albemarle Co., VA); m. William Dabney; (1806 Will Bequest).
2 CONT # Robert Harris, Jr. (b. 1776, Albemarle Co., VA); d. bef. 1806; (Deceased; Heirs mentioned in 1806).
2 CONT # Patsy Harris (b. c. 1778, Albemarle Co., VA); m. (Unknown) Tanner; (Deceased; Heirs mentioned in 1806).
2 CONT # Bezaleel Brown Harris (b. 1779, Albemarle Co., VA); m. Elizabeth Thompson; (Executor; Previously endowed).
2 CONT
2 CONT Extra children found attached to Robert & Lucretia have been moved to https://www.geni.com/people/Placeholder-Harris/6000000225109754839.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Identity & Research Notes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Surname Convention: This list restores the maiden name "Harris" for the daughters while noting their married names (Burch/Birch, Blackwell, Tanner, etc.) as confirmed by the bequests and heirs listed in the 1806 will.
2 CONT * Mourning's Identification: In the 1806 document, she is written as "Mourning Birch," confirming her marriage to the Burch family while identifying her as a biological daughter of Robert and Lucretia.
2 CONT * The Two Bezaleels: The distinction between the 1760 Brown son and the 1779 Harris son is preserved to resolve common genealogical conflations in the Brown's Cove lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Disambiguation ===
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Robert Harris of Reedy Creek vs. Robert Harris of Brown's Cove''': This Robert Harris (1741–1806) is frequently conflated with Robert Harris of Reedy Creek (1738–1803), a signer of the Mecklenburg Declaration and a prominent f
2 CONC igure in the Rocky River area of Mecklenburg/ Cabarrus County. While contemporaries, they belong to distinct lineages.
2 CONT *'''Burial Misidentification''': The burial location at Rocky River Baptist Church Cemetery in Mecklenburg County is often attached to this profile in error. That cemetery serves the Mecklenburg Harris branch. This Robert Harris a
2 CONC nd his wife, Lucretia, were established residents of Surry County, approximately 70 miles to the north.
2 CONT *'''Geographical Conflict''': Primary records, including the 1806 Surry County wills for both Robert and Lucretia, confirm they lived and died in the Surry/Salisbury region. It is highly improbable that they were transported to Me
2 CONC cklenburg for burial. They are most likely interred in an unmarked family plot or a local community cemetery in Surry County.
2 CONT .
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Capt. Robert Harris, Jr.''' (Maj Robert Harris & Mourning Glenn) b Mar 8 1741  Albermarle Co VA d NOV 1809 Surrey Co NC md Lucretia Brown BET 1772/76 dau/o Benjamin Brown/Sarah Thompson b 1746 Albermarle CoVA d 1806 Surrey Co N
2 CONC C 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Served in VA. State Militia, was Capt. in Revolutionary War. Moved to Surrey Co NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Harris, son of Mourning [Glenn] and Major Robert Harris (1696-1765), was born on 8 March 1741 in Hanover County, Virginia. He married Lucretia Brown on 1762 in Hanover County, Virginia. Lucretia had been born in 1746 in Alb
2 CONC emarle County, Virginia. She was a daughter of Sarah and Benjamin Brown, Sr. of Albemarle County, Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children: Elijah, Tyree Rodes, Bezeleel Brown, Bernice, Lucretia, Mourning, Nancy, Lucy, Robert, Patsey and Sarah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1777 Robert Harris was a Captain of the Virginia State Militia during the Revolutionary War. After the War, the Robert Harris family moved to Surry County, North Carolina. in 1779. In 1790 William Cooke and Robert Harris were a
2 CONC ppointed Commissioners to select a site for the Surry County, North Carolina, court house. Later Lucretia [Brown] Harris died in 1802 and Robert Harris died in 1809. Robert Harris wrote a Will on 10 July 1806 which was recorded du
2 CONC ring the May Term in 1809. An Inventory of the Robert Harris Estate was made 11 November 1809. He had 1700 acres of land, owned 10 Negro slaves, horses, cattle, hogs and sheep.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Extracted From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Harris-1939
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Harris Jr. was born on March 8, 1741 in St. Martin's Parish, which at the time was in Hanover County, in the English Province of Virginia (North America). The Parish was assigned to Louisa County in 1742 and became part of 
2 CONC newly-formed Albemarle County in 1744. Robert was the 8th child and 3rd son of Robert  Harris Sr..p, a Virginia planter who was three times elected to the Virginia House of Burgesses (therefore, a wealthy and notable family) in th
2 CONC e 1730-40s. His mother was Mourning (Glenn) Harris.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1750, Robert Harris Sr., moved his family from St. Martin's Parish, Alblemarle County. to rural Brown's cove in the Shenandoah Valley, halfway between Stanardsville and Waynesboro. This land was also located in Albemarle County
2 CONC , Virginia. The Brown's Cove settlement was founded by Welsh-born pioneer lawyer, Benjamin Brown, who became Robert Harris Jr.'s father-in-law in 1762, the same year that he passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762, Robert Harris Jr. married 16-year-old widow [sic]  Lucretia "Lucy" (Brown) Brown, b: 1846, in Albemarle County, Virginia. The couple lived at Brown's Cove and raised 12 children there, including Lucy's son by her first hu
2 CONC sband, Bezaleel Brown Sr., [no proof & that’s her brother’s name] whom she had married when she was just 14; he had died within 18 months. Robert Harris Jr. raised orphan Bezaleel Jr. as his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a frontiersman and patriot, Robert Harris Jr. joined the Virginia state militia to fight rebellious Indians; when the American Revolution began, he quickly rallied to the American cause and rose to become a Captain in the Virgi
2 CONC nia militia during the 1776-1778 campaigns.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1780, when released from the Virginia militia, Robert and Lucretia (Brown) Harris moved from Brown's Cove, Virginia, to newly-formed [1770] Surry county in North Carolina's Piedmont country. The 1790 first US Federal census sho
2 CONC ws him living there with one son over age 16; one son under 16; four women, including his wife, and 11 slaves. In July 1806, at 65 years old and just after the death of his wife, Robert Harris drew up his last will and testament, 
2 CONC transcribed below. It was proven three years later, showing that he died in Surry County, North Carolina, in November 1809.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his 1806 Will in Surry Co., NC, Robert Harris states that he had previously given 100 Virginia dollars to his daughter, Lucretia & three sons: Bernice, Bezaleel B., and Tyree Harris, presumably when they married or reached 21 y
2 CONC ears old. His children Patsy, Robert & Sarah had already passed away, leaving Lucy, Mourning, Nancy and Elijah. Elijah, his oldest son, inherited his plantation, as was the norm in Virginia, stemming from the English practice of p
2 CONC rimogeniture.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Harris: 
2 CONT 1806 - Will -Surry County, North Carolina 
2 CONT Date of Will, July 10, 1806 - Probated November Term 1809 [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT * "I, Robert Harris of the said county and state, being perfect senses and memory calling to mind the mortality of mankind and as it is appointed once for all men to die, I therefore ordain this to be my last will and testament an
2 CONC d after paying all my just debts, what estate it hath pleased God to bless me with, I give and bequeath in manner and form following. "
2 CONT * "First I give my soul to God who gave it and my body to the earth from whence it came, to be buried in such manner as my executors hereafter named shall think fit."
2 CONT * Item - I give to my son '''Elijah Harris''' the land whereon I now live it being the balance of the land that I have a deed for from Robert Lanier, which I give to him and his heirs forever."
2 CONT * "Item - Whereas my son '''Tyree''', son '''Bezaleel''', my son '''Bernice''' and daughter '''Lucretia''' have received one hundred pound Virginia Money out of my estate and the rest of my children has not, namely '''Mourning Bir
2 CONC ch,''' '''Nancy Boon''', the heirs of son '''Robert''', '''Lucy''' and ['''illegible'''], the heirs of '''Patsy Tanner''', and heirs of '''Sarah Blackwell''', my will is that there be out of my estate one hundred pounds Virginia m
2 CONC oney a piece raised for them, except the heirs of Sarah Blackwell who [will receive] only fifty pounds, allowing the rest heirs above mentioned. To be equal to one legatee which legatees I give to them and their heirs forever."
2 CONT * "Item- I give to my '''granddaughter Milly Harris''' one horse and saddle, bed and furniture, to her heirs forever the rest of my estate be it of what denomination so ever to be sold whether real or personal and equally divided 
2 CONC between the above named legatees, except Elijah, which takes no share but the land [I] gave him. "
2 CONT * "I constitute and appoint my sons Bezebel [Bezaleel] and Elijah to execute this my last will and testament revoking all other wills heretofore made. Signed sealed and acknowledged in presence of this tenth day of July in the yea
2 CONC r of our Lord one thousand eighteen hundred and six." 
2 CONT * (Signed) Robert Harris
2 CONT * Test______before signed, Joseph Forkner, Jesse? McKinney, Elijah Davis. [note: Robert Harris signed his will.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT By this last will and testament, Robert O. Harris Jr. reveals several things about his later life: His son Robert Jr., and daughters Sarah (m. Blackwell) and Patsy (m. Tanner) had already died before 10 July 1806. Earlier, he had 
2 CONC distributed 100 VA pounds to daughter Lucretia & sons Tyree, Bernice and Bezaleel Brown Harris; now he added Mourning Birch, Nancy, Lucy & the heirs of Robert, Sarah and Patsy to receive a VA money bequest. Oldest son Elijah Harri
2 CONC s received the family farm & house, considered more valuable than the rest, so he got nothing else. Sons Elijah Harris and Bezaleel Brown Harris were trusted to be executors. Robert's wife, Lucretia (Brown) Harris, had already die
2 CONC d in June 1806, in Surry County, NC, so she was not mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT J.T. McAllister. Virginia Militia in the Revolutionary War, 1913, p. 176/ Virginia Military Records. Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1983, pp.457,475. Hamilton J. Eckenrode. Virginia Soldiers of the American Revolution, vo
2 CONC l. 1, p. 204. John H. Gwathmey. Historical Register of Virginians in the Revolution: 1775-1783, 1987, p. 353. Edgar Woods. Albemarle County in Virginia, p. 221. DAR Supplemental on Robert Harris approved by NSDAR HQS 2 November 19
2 CONC 94. Supplemental Ancestor Certificate issued to Donald A. Wise, descendant of Robert Harris, approved by NSSAR on 30 August 1995.
2 CONT
2 CONT xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed abstracts concerning Robert Harris (the younger) and his wife Lucretia Brown, daughter of Benjamin Brown, a prominent citizen of Albemarle Co. and previously of Louisa Co. (quite wealthy, as he gave many of his children deeds
2 CONC  of gift, some rather substantial).
2 CONT
2 CONT Indenture...03 April 1762...Benjamin Brown,Fredericksville Parish,Albemarle Co.Va. to his daughter Lucretia Harris...for natural love and affection.. 400 acres on the branches of Doyles River and at the foot of the Blue Ridge Moun
2 CONC tains..being land where Lucretia lived...bounded by John Statham's corner..near Benjamin Thurmond's line. Witnesses: James Harris; Christopher Harris; Wm.Brown and Benj. Thurmond. John Nicholas, Clerk Albemarle Court of May 26,176
2 CONC 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Indenture...10 May 1770...Robert harris and Lucretia, his wife of Albemarle Co. Va . to William Cox of Orange Co. Va.90 pounds for 377 acres on the Doyles River near the Great Mountains. No witnesses. T. Woodson, Clerk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain in Virginia State Militia during Revolutionary War Robert and Lucretia Brown Harris moved to Surry County, NC from Albemarle County Va. in abt 1770's. Robert's brother Tyree had already moved to Orange County where he was 
2 CONC sheriff at the time of the Regulators.
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT HARRIS Will - Surry County, North Carolina Date of Will, July 10, 1806 Probated November Term 1809 Deed Book 3, page 93 Location - North Carolina State Archives, Raleigh, North Carolina State of North Carolina, Surry County
2 CONT
