1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children of Robert Harris and Lucretia Brown ==
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  Tyree Rhodes Harris (b. 1765, Albemarle Co., VA); m. Mary Ann Hunt; (Previously endowed 100 VA pounds).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Tyree Harris: 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will, made 1 Sep 1786 and recorded in Caswell Co., NC will book  B-162, Jan 1787 Tyree named as executors his son Tyree and his son Simpson as well as his son-in-law, Cornelius Dabney.+ His children were Nancy Abercrombie; 
2 CONC Frances, wife of Cornelius Dabney; sons Tyree, Simpson,vRobert (to receivevland in Rockingham Co. on Little Troublesome Cr*.) Other daughters werevEde Harris (wife of Duke Williams), Liddy Harris. He named a grandson William Chapm
2 CONC an Abercromby. According to this will in Caswell Co., his son Robert was to receive land in Rockingham Co. Whether Robert migrated there, it cannot be said definitively. It also appears from the court records of Caswell Co. that s
2 CONC everal of these children, probably by Tyree's second wife, the former Mary Ann Simpson, were underage.
2 CONT
2 CONT > Major Robert Harris had only four sons, according to his will:
2 CONT > Concerning Robert Harris [the father of Tyree]: His four sons were Robert, Jr., William, Tyre(e), and Christopher, as named in will made in Albemarle Co., VA. He had a son-in-law named James Harris, which confuses the genealogy.
2 CONC  It seems he had another son-in-law Joel Crawford, as a deed of gift appears in Amherst Co. DB B-51, dated 20 May 1765, in which Robert Harris of Fredericksville Parish, Albemarle Co., gives to his son-in-law Joel Crawford two sla
2 CONC ves: Lewis, a boy, and Juliany, a girl.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Robert Harris's will, as recorded in Albemarle Co. appears in the archives of rootsweb.com. It was posted in early March 2003. His will is also recorded in Louisa Co., probably because he owned land in both jur
2 CONC isdictions, and those probates were in two jurisdictions.
2 CONT > 18 June 1765. I August 1765. Son Christopher to have 40 acres of land  in Albemarle County, known as "Bears Cornfield." After decease of wife, son William to have all land I now hold in Albemarle County. Sons, Robert, Tyree and 
2 CONC William Harris to have slaves. Wife Mowrning (Mourning) to have use of all lands during her life. Son William [who was underage] to be under direction of  son-in-law John Rodes. Exrs: sons-in-law John Rodes and William Shelton.
2 CONT > Wit: Daniel Maupin, John Mullins, Jr, William Maupin and Connerly Mullins. ( J. Estelle Stewart King, ABSTRACTS of WILLS, INVENTORIES, AND ADMINISTRATION ACCOUNTS OF ALBEMARLE COUNTY, VA, 1748-1800, p. 16.)
2 CONT >
2 CONT I hope this information leads others to seek the documents pertaining to this family. (*from Rootsweb.com archives)
