1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Sarah-Gregory/6000000083374247859, wife of https://www.geni.com/people/James-Gregory/6000000017144962570?through=6000000004827750515
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sarah Thompson''' was born on January 17, 1724, in Louisa County, Virginia, the daughter of Robert Thompson  and Jane Hescott.  Her family was established in Louisa County at the time of her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1743, Sarah married Benjamin Brown, Sr. (d. 1762), becoming his second wife. The couple resided at "Trinidad" and "Walnut Level" in Brown's Cove, Albemarle County. Benjamin Brown died at his home on November 9, 1762, leavin
2 CONC g Sarah with several young children, including their youngest, Brightberry, who was then an infant. Sarah survived her husband by over fifty years; her will was written in 1803 and proved on December 4, 1815, in Albemarle County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Identity & Conflation Notes
2 CONT * Not Sarah (Thompson) Gregory: Sarah Thompson Brown (d. 1815) is frequently confused with Sarah (Thompson) Gregory (1722–1799). Archival records confirm they were separate individuals; Sarah Thompson Brown remained the widow of B
2 CONC enjamin Brown until her death.
2 CONT * The Dabney Misidentification: Genealogical confusion often attributes the surname "Dabney" to Sarah. This stems from a 19th-century inheritance claim by her grandson, Dr. Charles Brown. The Dabney connection originated from his 
2 CONC maternal line (Elizabeth Dabney), not through Sarah Thompson.
2 CONT * First Wife Correction: The name "Anne Elizabeth Hescott" for Benjamin's first wife is a digital phantom. Primary records indicate his first wife was a **Miss Wilson**.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT * Robert Thompson** (d. c. 1738; Hanover Co., VA) and Jane Hescott (d. aft. 1738; Louisa Co., VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT * Benjamin Brown, Sr.** (d. 09 Nov 1762; Albemarle Co., VA) m. c. 1743 in Louisa Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Benjamin Brown and Sarah Thompson:
2 CONT # Elizabeth Brown (1744–bef. 1762); born in Hanover Co.; m. John Price. (Older 1/2 sister Elizabeth d.y.) 
2 CONT # Lucretia "Lucy" Brown (1746–1806); born in Hanover Co.; married  Robert Harris (1762).
2 CONT # William Brown (1747–1836); born in Albemarle Co. (Older 1/2 brother William “Billy.”) 
2 CONT # Barzillai Brown (1747–c. 1813); born in Louisa Co.
2 CONT # Benajah Brown (1748–1814); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.; m. Mary Jarman.
2 CONT # Bernard Brown (1750–1800); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.; m. Elizabeth Jennings Dabney.
2 CONT # Dabney Brown (1751–1773); born in Albemarle Co.
2 CONT # Bernis Brown (1752–1814); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.; m. Henrietta Rodes. He was a son of Benjamin Brown and Sarah Thompson.
2 CONT # Bezaleel Brown (1754–1829); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.; m. Mary "Polly" Thompson.
2 CONT # Brightberry Brown (1762–1846); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.; m. Susan Deer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Re: the Hescott/Thompson/Dabney mystery
2 CONT
2 CONT We know of two marriages for Benjamin Brown, first to “Unknown” Hescott (about 1734) and second to Sarah Thompson (about 1745). Miss Hescott was born about 1710 and died in 1744, at the age of only 32. Sarah was just 21 years of a
2 CONC ge when she married the then 50 year old Benjamin Brown. Benjamin Brown died at his home, Trinidad, in Brown’s Cove, Albemarle County, Virginia, on November 9, 1762, at the age of 67. His last child, Brightberry Brown, was only 9 
2 CONC months of age when the elder Benjamin Brown passed away. Sarah Thompson lived on until 1794 and passed away at the age of 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Thompson was the daughter of Robert Thompson and Jane Hescott, who lived in Louisa County, Virginia, at the time of Sarah’s marriage to Benjamin Brown. Therefore, it is conceivable that our Miss Hescott and Sarah Thompson co
2 CONC uld have been first cousins – the daughters of a brother and sister – or, since Sarah Thompson was 14 years younger than our Miss Hescott, Sarah’s mother could have been the older sister of our Miss Hescott. Whichever is the case,
2 CONC  we now know that all eleven of Benjamin Brown, Sr.’s children are also descendants of the Hescott family of Kent and Louisa Counties in Virginia at the turn of the 18th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT But what about Sarah Dabney and Dr. Charles Brown's claim to the Dabney fortune in England? Well - that mystery is also now solved. Many of the “B” Brown children and grandchildren married back into the Dabney, Thompson, Jarman, a
2 CONC nd Hescott families. One of the grandchildren, Dr. Charles Brown, maintained that he was entitled to a portion of the vast English Dabney estate – and he also made several references to his grandmother having been Sarah Dabney. So
2 CONC mehow, many of the “B” Brown descendants came to believe that Dr. Brown’s grandfather, Benjamin Brown, was married to Sarah Dabney instead of Sarah Thompson. However, very few folks take the time to realize that Dr. Brown’s parent
2 CONC s were Bernard Brown and Elizabeth Dabney. Sarah Dabney was Dr. Brown’s MATERNAL grandmother – and it was through his mother, Elizabeth Dabney Brown, that Dr. Charles Brown was basing his claim to the Dabney fortune.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Albemarle County, Virginia: Will Book 6, p. 132 (Will of Sarah Brown, proved Dec 1815).
2 CONT * Albemarle County, Virginia: Will Book 2, p. 125 (Will of Benjamin Brown, Sr., 1762).  (Probate records confirming Sarah as widow).
2 CONT * Woods, Edgar. Albemarle County in Virginia, 1901.
2 CONT * Primary Family Bible and administrative registers for the Brown's Cove settlement.
2 CONT * Albemarle County, Virginia: Will of Benjamin Brown, Sr., dated 1762
2 CONT * Louisa County, Virginia: Vital records and deeds of Robert Thompson and Jane Hescott.
2 CONT * Family Records: Documentation of the "Trinidad" and "Walnut Level" estates in Brown’s Cove.
2 CONT * Historical Analysis: Resolution of the Dr. Charles Brown "Dabney Fortune" claim, clarifying the maternal origin of the Dabney surname.
2 CONT * Woods, Edgar. Albemarle County in Virginia, 1901.
