1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Benjamin Brown''' (c. 1695 – 1762) was a prominent 18th-century landowner and early settler of Albemarle County, Virginia. Born in New Kent County, he was the son of John Brown, an immigrant from England. While some family trad
2 CONC itions cite a Welsh origin near Mount Snowdon, primary records such as family Bibles and land patents document his rise through the Virginia planter class, beginning in Hanover County before he established the family seat at Brown
2 CONC 's Cove.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Early Life and Land Holdings ===
2 CONT Benjamin established himself as a significant landholder in Hanover County during the early 1720s. Between March 24, 1725, and September 12, 1733, he acquired 4,200 acres through eleven separate purchases. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1747, Brown began shifting his interests to the frontier of Albemarle County. His initial purchase on October 1, 1747, included two lots (37 and 190 acres). By June 1, 1750, he secured an additional 740 acres for himself and 10
2 CONC 0 acres for his son, Benjamin Jr. This estate, located in the mountains of the Blue Ridge, became known as '''Walnut Level''' and his specific home was named '''Trinidad'''. Legend suggests the move was prompted by a desire to esc
2 CONC ape the "Malaria section" of the low country.
2 CONT
2 CONT === The "B" Browns ===
2 CONT The family gained historical notoriety as the '''"B" Browns''' due to Benjamin's practice of naming all of his sons with the letter "B." This onomastic tradition was maintained by several subsequent generations, eventually necessi
2 CONC tating the use of descriptive prefixes or double names to distinguish between the many family members sharing identical names in the same region.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death and Estate ===
2 CONT Benjamin Brown executed his will on April 15, 1762, and died shortly thereafter. His will was proved on November 11, 1762 (Albemarle County Will Book 2, Page 130). His elder sons, Benjamin Jr. and William, acted as executors and r
2 CONC eturned to Hanover to manage the family's original holdings there.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children of Benjamin Brown ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by First Wife (Miss Wilson):
2 CONT
2 CONT # Benjamin Brown, Jr. (c. 1737 - bef. 1781); born in Hanover Co. Served as executor of his father's estate; returned to Hanover County.
2 CONT # William "Billy" Brown (1735 - 1780); born in Hanover Co. Served as executor; migrated to Brier’s Creek, South Carolina in 1769.
2 CONT # Bartlett Brown, Sr.; born in Hanover Co.
2 CONT # Elizabeth Brown (c. 1740 - bef. 1744); born in Hanover Co. Died young before the birth of her half-sister of the same name.
2 CONT # Agnes Brown; born in Hanover Co.
2 CONT # Lucinda Brown; born in Hanover Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Second Wife (Sarah Thompson):
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elizabeth Brown (1744 - bef. 1762); born in Hanover Co. Married John Price; died before the 1762 settlement of her father's estate.
2 CONT # Lucretia "Lucy" Brown (1746 - 1806); born in Hanover Co. Married Robert Harris, Jr. (1762). Died in Surry County, North Carolina.
2 CONT # William Brown (1747 - 1836); born in Albemarle Co.
2 CONT # Barzillai Brown (1747 - c. 1813); born in Louisa Co. (twin).
2 CONT # Benajah Brown (1748 - 1814); born at Walnut Level, Albemarle Co.
2 CONT # Bernard Brown (1750 - 1800); born at Walnut Level; married Elizabeth Dabney.
2 CONT # Dabney Brown (1751 - 1773); born in Albemarle Co.
2 CONT # Bernis Brown (1752 - 1814); born at Walnut Level.
2 CONT # Bezaleel Brown (1754 - 1829); born at Walnut Level; married Mary "Polly" Thompson (twin).
2 CONT # Brightberry Brown (1762 - 1846); born at Walnut Level.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == History of the Brown Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT . . . a bit of history of the Brown family who settled in Albemarle as early as 1747.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Browns who settled in Brown's Cove came from Hanover County, where they had considerable holdings prior to making purchases of land in Albemarle County. Between the 24th of March 1725 and September 12th, 1733, Benjamin Brown h
2 CONC ad purchased in Hanover County 4200 acres of land, comprising two purchases of 300 acres each and nine purchases of 400 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Brown's original purchase in Albemarle County was October 1, 1747 for two lots of land, one lot of 37 acres and the second lot of 190 acres. Then on June 1, 1750, two additional purchases were made, one of 100 acres for B
2 CONC enjamin Brown, Jr., and 740 acres for himself, for which he paid three pounds and fifteen shillings or approximately $20 of today's money. This grant specifies as do all of the early grants to be of good and lawful money.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of Browns were referred to as the "B" Browns because all of the boys names began with the letter "B". Benjamin Brown of Hanover Co., was of Welsh descent and his people came from Wales in 1600, possibly toward the close
2 CONC  of the century. Their home in Wales was near Mount Snowdon, and in leaving the low country of Hanover and seeking the mountains, some have said it was their desire to be in a country like their native Wales, but others claim the 
2 CONC move was prompted by the desire to be out of the Malaria section of the state, which latter reason the writer feels was one of the basic reasons for leaving Hanover.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fear of Malaria did not affect the two oldest boys, Benjamin, Jr. and William (Billy), who served as executors of Benjamin Sr's., estate and returned to Hanover to claim their father's holdings there.
2 CONT
2 CONT ( this information came from rootsweb.ancestry.com--EB 10-17-2015)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Re: the Hescott/Thompson/Dabney mystery
2 CONT
2 CONT We know of two marriages for Benjamin Brown, first to “Unknown” Hescott (about 1734) and second to Sarah Thompson (about 1745). Miss Hescott was born about 1710 and died in 1744, at the age of only 32. Sarah was just 21 years of a
2 CONC ge when she married the then 50 year old Benjamin Brown. Benjamin Brown died at his home, Trinidad, in Brown’s Cove, Albemarle County, Virginia, on November 9, 1762, at the age of 67. His last child, Brightberry Brown, was only 9 
2 CONC months of age when the elder Benjamin Brown passed away. Sarah Thompson lived on until 1794 and passed away at the age of 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Thompson was the daughter of Robert Thompson and Jane Hescott, who lived in Louisa County, Virginia, at the time of Sarah’s marriage to Benjamin Brown. Therefore, it is conceivable that our Miss Hescott and Sarah Thompson co
2 CONC uld have been first cousins – the daughters of a brother and sister – or, since Sarah Thompson was 14 years younger than our Miss Hescott, Sarah’s mother could have been the older sister of our Miss Hescott. Whichever is the case,
2 CONC  we now know that all eleven of Benjamin Brown, Sr.’s children are also descendants of the Hescott family of Kent and Louisa Counties in Virginia at the turn of the 18th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT But what about Sarah Dabney and Dr. Charles Brown's claim to the Dabney fortune in England? Well - that mystery is also now solved. Many of the “B” Brown children and grandchildren married back into the Dabney, Thompson, Jarman, a
2 CONC nd Hescott families. One of the grandchildren, Dr. Charles Brown, maintained that he was entitled to a portion of the vast English Dabney estate – and he also made several references to his grandmother having been Sarah Dabney. So
2 CONC mehow, many of the “B” Brown descendants came to believe that Dr. Brown’s grandfather, Benjamin Brown, was married to Sarah Dabney instead of Sarah Thompson. However, very few folks take the time to realize that Dr. Brown’s parent
2 CONC s were Bernard Brown and Elizabeth Dabney. Sarah Dabney was Dr. Brown’s MATERNAL grandmother – and it was through his mother, Elizabeth Dabney Brown, that Dr. Charles Brown was basing his claim to the Dabney fortune.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Primary Records:'''
2 CONT ** Albemarle County, Virginia, Will Book 2, Page 130. Will of Benjamin Brown: Written 15 April 1762; Proved 11 November 1762.
2 CONT ** Hanover County and Albemarle County Land Patents and Grants (1725–1750).
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Family Bibles and Manuscripts:'''
2 CONT ** Brown Family Bible (Hanover and Albemarle Counties, VA). Records maintained by Reuben Dabney Brown (grandson of Benjamin Brown). Transcribed by Eleanor Williams Bradley, ''Virginia Genealogist'', Vol. 13, 1969.
2 CONT ** Hirsch, Jean. "The Family of Joseph Alston and Caroline Green Hatcher." WorldConnect Project, RootsWeb (5 May 2013).
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Published Genealogies:'''
2 CONT ** Early, Ruth Hairston. ''The Family of Early: Which Settled Upon the Eastern Shore of Virginia and Its Connection with Other Families.'' Lynchburg, VA: Brown-Morrison, 1920. Page 32.
2 CONT ** Pettibon, Bessie Z. "Records of Early Settlers: The William 'Billy' Brown Family of Brown’s Cove, Virginia." USGenWeb Project, 2009.
2 CONT ** WikiTree contributors, "Benjamin Brown Sr. (abt.1695-1762)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Brown-5190 : accessed 25 April 2026).
2 CONT * '''Digital Repositories:'''
2 CONT ** RootsWeb. "Brown Family History settled in Albemarle 1747." Accession date: 17 October 2015 via rootsweb.ancestry.com.
2 CONT ** South Carolina Genealogical Records. "The William Brown Family of Brier’s Creek." Accessed 15 December 2014.
