3 TEXT page 202 it has some famliy info The first owner, Mr. Light, was a Mennonite in religion, who opened his house for religious services, and it was so employed once a month for a long period.  This community must have abounded with 
4 CONC Mennonites in that early day, as quite a congregation is said to have assembled here.  But the house was also built as a fortification against the Indian savages.  It is provided with a deep, arched cellar, into which subterranean
4 CONC  refuge a flight of stone steps still leads the way from the inside.  Hither the neighbors were often compelled to repair, to find shelter from their enraged, insatiable foes.  Sixty families took refuge in it at one time.  Becaus
4 CONC e of these retreats for safety, it received the name it has borne ever since, that of "Old Fort."  We recently visited the building, but found the older part of the house rifled and deserted by all except a flock of English sparro
4 CONC ws, a saucy-looking goat, and a family of colored folks, the last of whom occupy a few dreary-looking rooms.  The writer has strong suspicious that during the best days of canal navigation this building was used as a warehouse.
