1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''The Hugh Howell who died in New Jersey in 1745 could have come through Barbados, but he could not have married Margaret Middleton.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Many descendants of Hugh Howell (b. 1659 Wales, d. 1745 Baptistown, New Jersey) assume that he was married to Margaret Middleton.There is a record of a marriage between Hugh Howell and Margaret Middleton in 1698 in St. James Paris
2 CONC h, Barbados (Barbados Records, St. James Parish Register, RL1/46, 1698 , p 13, p392 of typed manuscript), and this record has been incorporated into many a family tree of the descendants of Hugh Howell in Canada and the United Sta
2 CONC tes.However, the Hugh Howell who married Margaret Middleton in Barbados in 1698 could not be the same man who died in New Jersey in 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here are the rest of the records of Hugh Howell in Barbados. 1.       Hugh Howell was counted in the militia regiment of Col. Timothy Thornhill (Omitted Chapters from Hotten's Original Lists.Barbados.1679.p148). 2.       Hugh Howe
2 CONC ll married Margaret Middleton in 1698 (see reference above). 3.       Margaret died in 1704; listed as “Margaret, wife of Hugh Esq,” she was buried on 17 Jun 1704 in St. James Parish (RL1/46, p.27) 4.       Hugh Howell Esq married
2 CONC  Elizabeth Alexander, a widow of St. Thomas Parish, on January 1, 1714 (Barbados Records, St. James Parish, Page 44, RLI/46, 1714 (page 398 of typed manuscript) 5.       In 1715, Hugh Howell, Esq, was counted in the census conduct
2 CONC ed that year in Barbados (Census of 1715.Reprinted in Barbados and America by David L. Kent,p.231).Hugh Howell Esq is listed in St. James parish, with a household of2 men; 1 aged 23 and one aged 40, and one woman aged 23.[Hugh is 
2 CONC likely aged 40, Elizabeth is likely the one woman aged 23 and Thomas Williams is likely the 23-y-o male.] 6.       Hugh Howell Esq wrote his own will in 1716 (English Settlers in Barbados 1637-1800, Wills and Administrations, Vol 
2 CONC III p 178, RB6/35, p. 563) and died and was buried later that year in Barbados (Notebook in Library of Barbados Museum & Historical Society, reference 46/47).[The parish data was viewed personally on microfilm 23 Jul 2010.] Althou
2 CONC gh no date for the burial is recorded, the dates on subsequent entries suggest a burial date that is consistent with his will and the proving thereof in May 1716.As to Hugh's age, if the 1715 census was correct, then he was approx
2 CONC imately 41-42 when he died in May of 1716.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the above citations, the only one who could be the Hugh who died in New Jersey in 1745 is the first one.Born in 1659, he would have been 20 years old in 1679.If he had made his way to Barbados like many other people in the late
2 CONC  1600s (voluntarily signing up as an indentured servant to a plantation owner, being arrested and sent to Barbados from jail, being swept up by an unscrupulous sea captain to be sold in Barbados), he would have beena prime candida
2 CONC te to be offered by a master to be counted in the militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1705, the New Jersey Hugh Howell was recorded in New Jersey in John Holcombe’s will (will of John Holcombe, reported in A Collection of Papers Read Before the Bucks County Historical Society , V, pg. 599).In 1714, he served on 
2 CONC a jury in Hunterdon County, NJ (Minutes of Courts of Quarter Sessions and Common Pleas (no case number; p 13).In 1715, he served on a grand jury and was granted a liquor license (Minutes of Courts of Quarter Sessions and Common Pl
2 CONC eas (no case number; pg 19) and Minutes of Courts of Quarter Sessions and Common Pleas (case #97, pg 20)).
2 CONT
2 CONT In summary, the Hugh Howell who died in New Jersey in 1745 could have come through Barbados, but he could not have married Margaret Middleton.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/howell/8822/
2 CONT
