1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===William Larison
2 CONT * Birth:	1672 - Newtown, Long Island, New York
2 CONT * Death:	Apr 7 1749 - Hopewell, Hunterdon, New Jersey
2 CONT * Parents:	John Larison, Abigail 
2 CONT * Wife:Anna Pashuns, Catharine  Manley
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT William Larison (1672-1749) married 1) Anna Pashuns 2) Catherine Manley
2 CONT
2 CONT children 
2 CONT #  James Larrison b: ABT 1695 in Long Island
2 CONT # Elizabeth Larrison b: ABT 1705 in Hopewell, NJ
2 CONT # John Larrison
2 CONT # William Larrison
2 CONT # George Larrison
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Larrison-93
2 CONT
2 CONT William L Larrison, dated Apr 7 1749 probated May 30 1749 in New Brunswick, New Jersey. The will names his children and his surname is spelled Larrison.
2 CONT
2 CONT * James
2 CONT * William
2 CONT * Thomas
2 CONT * John
2 CONT * George
2 CONT * Elizabeth m David Stout
2 CONT * Martha m Hide
2 CONT * Mary m Higgins
2 CONT * Rachel m Brittain
2 CONT * Deborah m Shippey
2 CONT * Keziah m Vantilborrow[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/67_100/94jan1996/94vanschoonhoven.html Excerpts from the Journal of Emma Larison Colwell, 1857 - 1950.]  Transcribed and annotated by Derick Van Schoonhoven,  History from George H. 
2 CONC Larison, Lambertville, N. J.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Larisons are of Danish descent. Their ancestor John Larison, in the war between the King and Nobles in 1665, had his property confiscated, and, leaving the country, went to the seashore disguised as a peasant, where he escaped
2 CONC  to Scotland and soon afterwards came to America. Landing on Long Island, he purchased a large tract of land upon which he settled.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had six sons, two killed by the Indians. Four survived whose name[s] were Roger, James, William and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT James settled on Stoney Brook, Hopewell township, now in Mercer County, New Jersey, where he bought an estate of 250 acres of land and reared a family of six sons. He died there in 1792 and was buried on his farm. His 6 sons were 
2 CONC John, Andrew, Roger, William, Elijah, and David. His daughters [were] Achsa, Rachel, and Catherine.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Pioneers of Old Hopewell" by Ralph Ege:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Larison probably resided at Hopewell until after the death of Mrs. Larison, or near the close of life, when he removed to New Brunswick and probably resided with one of his children. In 1751 his son James owned the tract e
2 CONC ast of the road along the old driftway as shown by the deed from Joshua Anderson to Hon. John Hart and his brother Daniel on October 4 of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of William Larison dated at New Brunswick, N. J., April 7, and probated May 30, 1749, is recorded in Libre 6, Folio 70, in the office of the Secretary of State at Trenton and was copied in part by the writer many years ag
2 CONC o. This will is remarkable for the deep religious feeling manifested in the introductory sentences, which, although in accordance with the custom of the times, is extended far beyond all precedent, and contains the whole scheme of
2 CONC  redemption as revealed in the New Testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Following from: BELL, NIXON, BARROWS, McKINLEY, COOPER FAMILIES, RootsWeb.com, judiepc@charter.net
2 CONT
2 CONT "A Chronicle of Everyday People" States that William is known to have been with an emigration from Newtown and the Inventory of the estate of Theophilius Phillips of Maidenhead, NJ has the item of L131-11s-2d due from William Lari
2 CONC son March 19th, 1708/09. 1708/10 he settled near what now is Hopewell, NJ. He was on the tax list of Hunterdon Co., in 1722.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Pioneers of Old Hopewell"do make and Constitute my beloved son John Larison and son-in-law David Stout to be my executors of this my will and do Empower them to Collect in my debts and dispose of my Estate to the best advantage a
2 CONC t discretion of my Executors and after paying my Just Debts Funeral Charges and my aforesaid Legacy to Divide the remainder as aforesaid among my aforesaid sons William Thomas John and George and I do hereby revoke and Disannul al
2 CONC l other Wills, Whatsoever heretofore by me made Ratifying and Conforming this and only this to be my Last Will and Testament. "In witness Whereof I the said William Larison have hereunto set my hand & seal this seventh day of Apri
2 CONC l in the year of our Lord Christ one thousand seven hundred and forty nine. (David Stout was husband of daughter Elizabeth) William Larison "Witnesses Jediah Higgins-Samuel Wilson and Robert Rolfe"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed William Larison "Witnesses Jediah Higgins-Samuel Wilson and Robert Rolfe"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000186669790837&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=uQR_vjf2_EEC&pg=PA225&lpg=PA225&dq=old+John+the+Dane+Larison&source=bl&ots=e49zYhK3PW&sig=oauIYDrB16AKjvP7raa1kpZR9lk&hl=en&sa=X&ei=DutpUt-OI6nXygHcioH4DQ&ved=0CCsQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=old%20John%
2 CONC 20the%20Dane%20Larison&f=false Pioneers of Old Hopewell:] With Sketches of Her Revolutionary Heroes (Google eBook) by Ralph Ege.  Race & Savidge, 1908 - Hopewell (N.J.) - 289 pages.  Page 225.  
2 CONT *The Early Germans of New Jersey; their History, churches and Genealogies. Dover, NJ: Dover Printing Co., 1895. "LARASON: WILLIAM, whose nameoccurs on tax list of Hopewell twp., 1722, as assessed for 11 cattle and horses, 9 sheep 
2 CONC and 160 acres. His will, dated April 7 and prob. May 30, 1749. (Trenton, Lib 6,Vol. 70), mentions five children: James, William, John, George, and the wife of David Stout."
