1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://minerdescent.com/2010/05/19/roger-parkes-jr/
2 CONT
2 CONT Keziah’s husband James Larison was born 1695 in Middlesex, New Jersey. His parents were William Larison (1672 – 1749) and Anna Pashuns Radolff (1673 – 1749) . James died in 1792 in Hunterdon, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT #  John LARISON b: 1737 in Hopewell, Hunterdon County, New Jersey
2 CONT #  Andrew LARISON b: 2 FEB 1738/39
2 CONT #  William LARISON b: 24 JAN 1740/41
2 CONT #  Anne LARISON b: 11 FEB 1742/43
2 CONT #  Roger LARISON b: 1745
2 CONT #  Catherine LARISON b: 1750
2 CONT #  Achsah LARISON b: 1752
2 CONT #  Elijah LARISON b: 1754
2 CONT #  David LARISON b: 8 MAR 1757
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [maitino3-11.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT The author of "Pioneers of Old Hopewell" does not list any primary sources for Elizabeth LARISON's name, but he does list her genealogical background. He starts with John LARISON (or Jon LARSEN) according to the fireside tradition
2 CONC s  of the older Larisons was a Danish nobleman who was compelled to flee from Denmark and lose his estates by confiscation in 1660 because of taxes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He fled first to Scotland and hearing that a large reward had been offered for his capture, he went over to Ireland for a time and finally emigrated to America and purchased a large tract about 1700 acres near Brooklyn, Long Islan
2 CONC d.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had six sons, one of whom, William came to Hopewell, NJ. William's son was James LARISON m. Kesiah PARKE, daughter of Doctor Roger PARKE.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's daughter was Elizabeth LARISON who m. David STOUT in 1726. The author mentions the entire family of children of James LARISON and Kesiah PARKE and their spouses, but mentions only that David STOUT and Elizabeth LARISON h
2 CONC ad four sons and five daughters. I do have a list of these children from another source however, in case you don't have them. David STOUT who m. Elizabeth, sister of James LARISON, bought the PARKE claims (they were looking for si
2 CONC lver) about 1740. The quit claim deed was not obtained by David STOUT until 1765, and on 10 June of the same year he sold the plantation of 243 acres to James LARISON for the sum of twelve hundred pounds ($3,000.00). Both of these
2 CONC  old parchment deeds are in the writer's possession. (This book was originally printed in 1908.)
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Pioneers of Old Hopewell" by Ralph Ege
2 CONT
2 CONT James, the oldest son of William and grandson of John the Dane, was born in 1695, doubtless on Long Island and died at Hopewell in 1792, aged 97 years. He was buried in the Parke- Larison family plot on the farm where the writer s
2 CONC pent sixty years of his life, and his grave, marked by a rough unlettered sand stone, was often pointed out to him by his grandfather, and was carefully protected for 60 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a tradition in the family that Kesia’s father Roger Parke found a nugget of silver on the farm while digging a post hole, had it examined and pronounced genuine silver ore. He was very desirous that James Larison should p
2 CONC urchase the farm and would not reveal his secret to any one else, and as Mr. Larison did not secure his title of the heirs of Dr. Coxe until after the death of Roger Parke, his secret died with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The old fence was taken up and a trench some six feet in width and several hundred feet in length was dug in order to discover the treasure, and this trench is well remembered by the writer. Along this trench a shaft was sunk and 
2 CONC when a depth of ninety feet was reached caved in while the men were at dinner, burying all their tools. They were so discouraged at what they were pleased to consider this ill omen that it was never reopened. Several other shafts 
2 CONC were sunk on different portions of the farm, some of them to a considerable depth, but were abandoned on account of the vast accumulation of water which had to be kept out by hand power and necessitated working day and night. Jame
2 CONC s Larison sunk a comfortable fortune in his mining enterprises without realizing any profit whatever on the investment.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a tradition in the family that the old log schoolhouse that stood on a part of James Larison’s north tract, and at the cross roads near Wm. F. Golden’s, was built by James Larison, and that he was the first teacher. It is
2 CONC  quite probable that he taught there when a young man, but it doubtless stood there many years before he purchased the Parke tract, and was no doubt built by Doctor Roger Parke, or his son John, who resided on that part of the tra
2 CONC ct.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Larison, the father of this large family, was the executor of his own will, which he made in the form of a deed to his two youngest sons, Elijah and David. The old patriarch was then 94 years of age, and his four eldest sons
2 CONC  were well advanced in life. He had doubtless given them their portion many years before, and they were well established in business. It only remained for him to deed the homestead farm of 243 acres to the two youngest, and make s
2 CONC ome provision for the three remaining daughters who had doubtless been given a start in life at the time of their marriage many years before.
2 CONT
2 CONT This deed is dated February 14, 1791, and is a very lengthy document. It states that in consideration of his natural love and affection for his two sons, Elijah and David, and also for and in consideration of the sum of two hundre
2 CONC d and thirteen pounds to be paid by the said Elijah and David Larison unto his daughters and their children, and names them in their order as given above, not forgetting the young grandson, John Humphrey, Jr., son of his deceased 
2 CONC daughter Achsah. The boundaries of the tract are then given in detail, commencing in the middle of Stony Brook, and bounded by lands of Charles Sexton, deceased, on the west and north, James Hunt on the east, and lands of Ralph Hu
2 CONC nt and the several courses of Stony Brook on the southeast and south.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of James Larison, Elijah and David divided the tract as equally as possible and each gave to the other a quit claim deed. All these deeds and also a map of the tract as divided, made by Wilson Stout December 7, 179
2 CONC 6, are in possession of the writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of James’ youngest son David Larison the administrators sold their tract to Samuel Ege, who divided it between two of his sons, John and George. (George was Keziah Maple's father not James)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT In the Pioneers of old Hopewell with sketches of her revolutionalry heros (NJ) - it states that Kesiah, youngest dau of Dr Roger Parke, born about 1700 married James, oldest son of William Larison of Hopewell.
2 CONT
