1 NOTE Jonathan Stout, the Hopewell pioneer, died in 1723, and in his will proved March 25, 1723, he bequeathed to his two youngest sons, Samuel and David, (who were at that time aged 14 and 17 respectively) the tract on which Jonathan r
2 CONC esided at the time of his death. This was the tract lying south of the Col. Joseph Stout farm, now George E. Weart's, and extended south about one mile along the Province line, and west to the mountain road. This tract now include
2 CONC s the farms of P. W. Sheppard, Harry Van Dyke, David Moore, Miss Sarah Stout, Peter O. Voorhees, and the northern half at least of the David Stout and Amos Bond farms. The will states that these two sons were left "one full equal 
2 CONC undivided sixteenth part of a proprietary," and one standing on the hill near Mr. George E. Weart's dwelling and viewing this whole tract spread out like a panorama at his feet can but exclaim in admiration, "What a magnificent in
2 CONC heritance was theirs!" 
2 CONT Name: Jonathan STOUT
2 CONT Birth: 1660 in Lewis, Kings Co., Long Island, New York Colony 1 Birth: 1658 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., NJ
2 CONT Death: 24 NOV 1722 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey Colony
2 CONT Burial: NOV 1724 Hopewell, Hunterdon County, NJ 
2 CONT Father: Richard STOUT  b: 1615 in Burton Joyce Parish, Nottinghamshire, England  Mother: Penelope PRINCE  b: ABT 1622 in Amsterdam, Netherlands 
2 CONT
2 CONT
