1 NOTE Was considered the Pioneer of Hopewell Township, NJ. On March 20, 1664 King Charles II, King of Great Britain, granted his brother James, the Duke of York territory of land which is mostly NJ today. The Duke gave this land to Sir 
2 CONC George Carteret and Lord Berkeley. The township of Hopewell was bounded on the East by the "Old Province Line", on the North by Amwell Township, in Hunterdon County, on the West by the Delaware River and on the South by the Assunp
2 CONC ink Creek. Daniel Cox was the Governor of West Jersey (1687 - 1690) He obtained the deed from the Indians for the tract of land which included the whole of Hopewell Township. The deed was dated 3/30/1688 and was signed by 11 India
2 CONC n chiefs proving it was not stolen. 
2 CONT The Stouts arrived in Hopewell in 1708. They came from Middleton, NJ and helped organize the First Babtist Church in Hopewell in 1715. The meetings were held in private homes until the church was built in 1747. Revered Isaac Eaton
2 CONC  was the minister in 1748 and established a school in 1756. His father-in-law Decon David Stout (of Amwell) build him the school. This was the First Baptist Academy in America. The school was set up to educate young men for the mi
2 CONC nistry. The success of the Baptist Academy created a demand among Baptists of the Atlantic Seaboard for a college, thus Brown University was founded. The first President of Brown had been a pupil at the academy. 
2 CONT The local tribe of the Delaware Tribe called the Lenni-Lenape. The young men would gather up and spend the summer at the beaches. One of these groups of Indians met up with Jonathan Stout's family who had settled at Middleton, Mon
2 CONC mouth county. This group took interest in Jonathan because he was a bold, fearless, powerful man. The indians invited him to visit during the fall. Jonathan accepted and returned with them. He succeeded in winning their esteem and
2 CONC  they urged him to move his family near by their village and promised him a large tract of land as a gift. In 1686 Jonathan came to Hunterdon County (at the time called Burlington County, today called Mercer County). He was the fi
2 CONC rst white settler to set foot on the soil soon to be named Hopewell. When he was ready to return that fall, a heavy snow had fallen in the niht. The Indians made him snowshoes and gave him a matchen stick (a stick displaying a sym
2 CONC bol that would identify him as a friend). This stick was used to present himself to different natives he might meet along the way home to Middleton. He was treated with great kindness when he did present it to the Indians at Princ
2 CONC eton and Cranbury. In 1706 his whole family settled on the land he had chosen. It was high on the hill within sound on the Minnepenason Indian Village. Soon he became their Idol and their Judge. 
2 CONT All of this information can be found in the Hopewell Valley Heritage book, by Alice Blackwell Lewis and can be found at The Hopewell Museum.
1 NOTE Jonathan was the 3rd son of Richart I. After he married he moved to Hopewell,
2 CONT Hunterdon Co., N J. They had 6 sons & 3 daughters.(The History of the Stout
2 CONT Family p 4).  Susan Tucker says Jonathan b. est.1639-1672 In Middletown,
2 CONT N.J. & md. AnnaThrockmorton Bollen, b. est 1642 in Middletown, NJ.
1 NOTE Jonathan was the 3rd son of Richard I. After he married he moved to Hopewell, Hunterdon Co., N J. They had 6 sons & 3 daughters.(The History of the Stout Family p 4).  He was Militia Capt. Lived near Baptist Meeting House at Hopew
2 CONC ell.  Organized Baptist Church at Colombia Village.  In 1681, President of County court. 1688 received patent of 30 acres from father at Middletown & Conesconk. Slaveholder & in 1722 personal estate $2,500.  His will proved 25 Mar
2 CONC ch 1723 (Pioneers of Old Hopewell p 262).
1 NOTE John was a very prominent citizen of the adjoining County of Somerset, living near Skillman Station. He was justice from 1810-25 & one of the judges of the Court of Common Pleas, 1820-25.
1 NOTE See Historical Document.
