1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Lewis Chamberlain and Lucretia Woolsey LDs birth record
2 CONT
2 CONT reported Killed at Battle of Long Island Ref: Snyder County annals: a collection of all kinds of historical ..., Volume 1 By Clara R. Winey 1919 .p.19 {Pennsylvania}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT John Chamberlin was the son of Lewis and Lucretia (Woolsey or Woolley) Chamberlin of East Amwell, Hunterdon Co., NJ. He and his wife Mary (Stout) had 12 children.  Contrary to some sources, HE WAS NOT KILLED AT THE BATTLE OF LONG 
2 CONC ISLAND during the Revolutionary War and I have much evidence to this effect (which I can share with other Chamberlin genealogists).
2 CONT
2 CONT John and wife Mary are buried on land once owned by John and donated to the Bethlehem Presbyterian Church for a cemetery. His executors sold the remaining parcel of land after his death. This explains why these two Baptists are bu
2 CONC ried in a Presbyterian church cemetery! Note that John originally received this land in his father's will in 1772. Lewis Chamberlin mentioned in his will that the land had previously belonged to Francis Quick, deceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://old.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=88788474 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 9 2018, 6:33:32 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Chamberlin BIRTH	27 May 1739 Hunterdon County, New Jersey, USA DEATH	26 Dec 1821 (aged 82) Hunterdon County, New Jersey, USA BURIAL	 Bethlehem Cemetery Union, Hunterdon County, New Jersey
2 CONT
