1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of Ensign.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A028252  DAR Ancestor # A028252]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crow's Will - In the name of God, Amen, I, William Crow, of the county of Lincoln and the State of Kentucky, do make this my last will and testament in manner and form following: My will and desire is that all my just debt
2 CONC s shall be paid out of the first moneys that may come into the hands of my Executors hereinafter to be named, whither from debts due to me or the proceeds of either real or personal property belonging to my estate. FIRST:
2 CONT  I give to my wife, Patience, one hundred and eighteen acres of land in Garrard County, the same which she claimed under her former husband's will, together with the following Negroes, to-wit: Sal, Adam and Dice, one brood mare an
2 CONC d suckling colt and the choice of all of my cows, feather bed and furniture, together iwth all the beds and furniture which she, my said wife, was possessed of at the time of our inter-marriage, also one young horse which is known
2 CONC  by the name of Colonel. I also give to my wife any quantity of cupboard and kitchen furniture not exceeding one-half that she may secause to take, all the property above mentioned as being given to my wife it is my will and desir
2 CONC e that she and her heirs enjoy it forever. In addition to the above I give to my wife, Patience, my negroe boy, Lewis, during her life and at her death he, the said Lewis, is to be sold and the proceeds divided between my daughter
2 CONC s or their heirs. My will and desire is that so much of my stock, stills, wagon and farming utesils together with plantation whereon I now live also the whole of the Negroes (or so much of the above property as my wife may think p
2 CONC roper to retain in her hands, shall remain with her for two years after my death or until a comfortable house of brick, stone or frame can be built on the 118 acres of land in Garrard County as above mentioned).  SECONDLY, 
2 CONT  I give to my sons, Walter Crow and James Crow, the balance of my preemption (not sold and lying on the south of my settlement supposed to be three hundred and eighty-two acres, also one hundred and eighteen acres to be taken off 
2 CONC the South of mysettlement to extend the full length of the settlement line and run parallel then to the land to be equally divided between my sons, Walter and James and Walter to have contract exist, between the said Walter and Ja
2 CONC mes for eighty acres of the said land so willed to James Crow which contract was made with my knowledge and approbation, that out of James Crow’s part of the land so willed, Walter Crow is to have the eighty acres, having heretofo
2 CONC re made advance to my sons, Walter and James together with the lands willed them. I conceive to be a just and full proposition of my estate and they nor neither of their heirs are entitled to any further interest in my estate. Hav
2 CONC ing made advance to my son, James, over and above what I have made to my other children to the amount of two thousand dollars and upward for which I hold his paper—Walter Crow also having made advance and paid sundry debts for the
2 CONC  said James my will is that the title of those lands so willed to James shall not be competed in him until those two debts be paid and that two years be allowed by said son, James, after my death to discharge those debts, if not d
2 CONC one in that time when the land so allotted him to be sold by my Executors on the following credit, one fourth in one year, the balance in annual installments, and the proceeds applied towards the discharge of those two debts, the 
2 CONC over plus, if any, to be paid over to my said son, James.
2 CONT  THIRDLY,  I give to my son, Benjamin Crow, all the land lying north of the following boundary to-wit: Beginning on the western boundary line of my entire bound, which divides my lands from the land now held by Joshua Barbee and w
2 CONC here my orchard fence joins the said land now held at the southwest corner of the orchard of in-closure running due east so as to include the whole of the orchard passing Benjamin House about eight poles thence a north-easterly co
2 CONC urse crossing the spring branch so far that a line running due east until it strikes the line that divides my lands and the lands of Robert Bryan will leave my barn at least forty feet to the south, also a Negro man named Ben, tog
2 CONC ether with all other property heretofore given to him which I conceive to be his full proportion of my estate. The residue of my estate, both real and personal, I give to my daughters and their heirs forever, in equal proportions 
2 CONC having theretofore made advances to each of them as followeth:  TO ELIZABETH PIERCE: Thirteen hundred dollars. TO RACHEL HAMILTON: Thirteen hundred dollars. TO ANN GIVINGS: Seven hundred dollars. TO MARY MOORE: One thousand dollar
2 CONC s. TO SARAH COOK: Six hundred dollars. TO MARGARET HOPPER: Six hundred dollars. TO MARIAH BLEDSOE: Six hundred dollars. TO MATILDA LETCHER: Six hundred dollars. The several sums heretofore advanced to my daughters are to be taken 
2 CONC into consideration and those having received a lesser sum must first be made equal to those that have received the larger sums and this is to be done out of the first money arising from my estate after my debts are paid, and then 
2 CONC an equal dividend to take place between my said daughters or their heirs. My will and desire is that so soon as the first article of this will as relates to my wife is complied with that the whole of my personal property and the N
2 CONC egroes shall be sold and on a credit of at least one a year and the balance of the tracts of land that I now live on and not willed to my sons shall also be sold by my Executors at public sale at four equal annual payments, my out
2 CONC  lands also to be sold and credit of at least two years.
2 CONT  My will is that my negroe woman, Horace, from and after my death, be free and enjoy all the liberty and privilege of free people of color in this State and my Executors are directed to retain in their hands much of my estate as w
2 CONC ill be sufficient to support said woman, Horace, or at least to prevent her from becoming a charge on the county. LASTLY, I do appoint George Givins, Samuel Moore, William Cook, Thomas Hambleton, Joseph Hopper, Willis Bledsoe, and
2 CONC  Stephen Letcher, Executors to this my last will and testament, hereby revoking all others. In testimony whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal this 16th day of September, on thousand eight hundred and twenty.. (SEAL) WILLIA
2 CONC M CROW. Witnesses: JOSHUA BARBEE, EPHRAIM McDOWELL.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Patriot of the American Revolutionary War; Lincoln Co., Kentucky Militia
