1 NOTE {geni:about_me} More evidence needed about Mourning Winn's parentage. She is NOT the Mourning Winn (m. James GUNN), daughter of Joseph & Elizabeth Bacon Winn with whom often confused. She may or may not have been the daughter of S
2 CONC amuel Winn and his wife Sarah Bannister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mourning Glenn's (1678-1756) ancestry is very uncertain. Multiple stories and theories abound, but very little if any documentation exists. She has often been conflated with the Mourning Winn (who lived a century later) who marrie
2 CONC d a man named James GUNN (not Glenn) on 4 Dec. 1789 in Lunenburg Co., Virginia (Lunenburg Co. Virginia Marriage Bonds, Bond #229). The bondsman was Samuel Winn. This Mourning Winn Gunn was the daughter of Joseph Winn & Elizabeth W
2 CONC inn of Lunenburg Co. Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Familysearch.com positions her as a daughter of John Wynne (1627-1694) and Elizabeth Minor (1628-1690) who married c. 1653 in Westmoreland County, VA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikitree positions her as the daughter of Samuel Joseph Winn (1640-1700) and Sarah Elizabeth (Bannister) Winn (1640-1705) married about 1661 in Richmond County, Colony of Virginia. Their narrative says that this Sarah Elizabeth ma
2 CONC y have been called Elizabeth Minor since it was her mother's maiden name (???). Note that Elizabeth Minor is the name of the presumed mother in the familysearch.com tree, but the father has a different name and origin. John Wynne 
2 CONC is said to have been born in London, while Samuel Joseph Winn is said to have been born in in Saint Ninian's, Stirlingshire, Scotland (which also happens to be where the Glenn family is supposed to have originated).
2 CONT
2 CONT Neither set of parents is supported enough by evidence or documentation to be convincing
2 CONT
2 CONT =---------------------------=
2 CONT Re: Mourning Winn posted by stephwm29 on 28 Feb 2015 on the Ancestry Genealogy Forum:
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is what I have on this family: In Accomack County, Virginia, there was a family of children born to James Glenn and his first wife, said to be Elizabeth Mitchell. Their children are said to include a son James who married Mou
2 CONC rning Winn. After the death of 1st wife Elizabeth, James Glenn married 2nd Catherine, who is sometimes shown as Catherine “Winn.” This is just my opinion, but Catherine Winn may have been a widow with a daughter Mourning Winn when
2 CONC  she married James Glenn as his 2nd wife. The Accomack Co. VA estate record for James Glenn mentions his widow Catherine: GLENN, JAMES – Adm. His est. to Catherine Glenn, wid. of sd. James – Henry Toles & John Morris sec. – 15 Sep
2 CONC t. 1696 – p. 199 (Wills and Administrations, Accomack County, Virginia, 1663-1800, p. 22).
2 CONT
2 CONT I have been unable to find a marriage record for James Glenn and Mourning Winn.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a different marriage recorded between Morning Winn and James Gunn on 4 Dec. 1789 in Lunenburg Co., Virginia (Lunenburg Co. Virginia Marriage Bonds, Bond #229). The bondsman was Samuel Winn. This Mourning Winn was the daug
2 CONC hter of Joseph Winn & Elizabeth Winn of Lunenburg Co. Virginia. The groom was Reverend James R. Gunn. The above marriage of Mourning Winn & James Gunn is often confused as being the marriage record for Mourning Winn & James Glenn,
2 CONC  even though the counties are different & the time periods are one hundred years apart. I hope this will help to clarify that the woman named Mourning Winn in Lunenburg Co. VA in the late 1700s is not to be confused with a woman n
2 CONC amed Mourning Winn of Accomack Co. VA in the late 1600s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted additionally: 13 Sep 2016 
2 CONT
2 CONT I'm posting a followup to the message I posted in 2015 on the topic of Mourning Winn.
2 CONT
2 CONT I've been researching for over a year, and I've been unable to find any documentation for Catherine wife of James Glenn of Accomack Co. VA, beyond the fact that her first name was Catherine or Katherine. There is no proof yet foun
2 CONC d that she was a "Winn." James Glenn is said to have had a son James, later found in records of New Kent Co. VA. This younger James Glenn is presumed to be the son of James Glenn of Accomack County, due to similarity of names and 
2 CONC compatibility of dates.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is claimed that James Glenn, the son, married Mourning Winn. The source is Landon C. Bell's compilation, "Cumberland Parish, Lunenburg County, Virginia, 1746-1816 Vestry Books 1746-1816." On p. 249 of that book Bell wrote, "Jam
2 CONC es Glenn m. Mourning Winn (M. B. Dec. 4, 1789)." Unfortunately, Bell's book contained a transcription or typographical error which resulted in "Gunn" being incorrectly published as "Glenn." It was James Gunn who married Mourning W
2 CONC inn, not James Glenn.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his Winn research years between 2003 and 2009, Warner Martin Winn examined the original Lunenburg marriage bond index at Library of Virginia and transcribed Winn marriages for his 2nd Edition publication of Daniel Winn and 
2 CONC His Nine Sons, 2009. Warner Martin Winn transcribed bond #229 for the marriage of "James Gunn and Morning Winn, Dec. 4, 1789." It may be noted here that Morning Winn, also called Mourning Winn, was the daughter of Joseph Winn and 
2 CONC Elizabeth of Lunenburg County, Virginia, and she was the granddaughter of Daniel Winn (1715-1789) of Lunenburg. James Gunn was the Rev. James R. Gunn (1772-1849). The James R. Gunn Bible, in possession of the family of Minor Thoma
2 CONC s Gunn, includes an 1807 death record for James's wife "Mourning Winn Gunn."
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, there are other errors to watch out for. Some family trees show a marriage between James Glenn and Mourning Winn on 4 Dec. 1689, Accomack Co. Virginia. This is an example of a double set of genealogical errors through
2 CONC  use of the incorrect surname ("Glenn" instead of "Gunn") but with the 1789 marriage date in Lunenburg changed to the previous century and a different county (1789 becomes 1689 and Lunenburg becomes Accomack).
2 CONT
2 CONT For those who would like to do their own fact-checking, the source of the Morning Winn/James Gunn marriage is Lunenburg County Virginia Index to Marriage Bonds 1746-1850, Reel 41, Library of Virginia. There are many family trees t
2 CONC hat need to be updated to reflect the corrected marriage information.
2 CONT
2 CONT Update through personal correspondence with Curator Pam Wilson in Jan 2020:
2 CONT
2 CONT I know there are many people with family trees who report their ancestors as James Glenn and Mourning Winn. Though it's certain James Glenn had a wife and had children with her, I cannot find proof of marriage for James Glenn whic
2 CONC h lists his wife by name. Her name may have been Mourning, but I cannot find any direct proof of her maiden name. The one record often cited for such proof is a 1789 Lunenburg County Virginia marriage bond for the marriage between
2 CONC  Joseph Winn's daughter Mourning Winn and James Gunn, spelled "G-u-n-n" and not "G-l-e-n-n." The man who paid surety on this 1789 marriage bond was Samuel Winn. Samuel was Mourning Winn's kinsman, no doubt, but it is certain that 
2 CONC Mourning Winn [Gunn] was the daughter of Joseph Winn. Joseph Winn died in 1800, Lunenburg, and his probated will specifically identified his daughter Mourning Gunn as one of his heirs. Unfortunately, this particular marriage recor
2 CONC d between Mourning Winn and James Gunn in Lunenburg has been repeated over and over as proof of marriage for James Glenn. It's unfortunate for people tracing their family trees to be misled this way. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To make matters even more complicated, there were three people named Mourning Winn in Lunenburg County in the 1700s, two females and one male. There are many Lunenburg records to support the family connections between each of thes
2 CONC e persons and their respective parents and spouses. They were as follows: (1) Joseph Winn and Elizabeth's daughter Mourning Winn (married James Gunn, 1789, Lunenburg Co. VA) (2) John Winn Sr. & Anne Stone's son Mourning Winn (marr
2 CONC ied Sarah Jones, 1786, Lunenburg Co. VA) (3) Thomas Winn Sr. & Elizabeth's daughter Mourning Winn (married John Hix before 1769). I've been active in Winn genealogy for many years and particularly the Colonial Era Winn lineage gro
2 CONC ups in Lunenburg, Amelia, and Hanover Counties. I have confidence in the information I've provided, but I do not know much about Glenn-family genealogy - except for the problem with the marriage often cited for James Glenn and his
2 CONC  wife Mourning. 
2 CONT =----------------------=
2 CONT Note:  Information unverified.  From "The Glenns's" by Leta Kay Glenn Cornett.
