1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James Glen, who in 1717-1719 owned land adjacent to the North Anna River in St. Paul's Parish, New Kent County. He is listed in the Vestry Book of St. Paul's Parish from 1719-1724, in connection with John Glenn. In
2 CONC  1725-1731 he owned land further upstream along the same (North Anna) river, in Hanover County. He is probably the same James of St. Martin's Parish, Hanover County, who purchased land in 1739 on Great Guinea Creek, St. James Pari
2 CONC sh, Goochland County (southeast of Hanover), an area that later became Cumberland County. He left a will in 1762 in Hanover County naming wife Hannah (maiden name probably Thompson) and children: GIDEON, NEHEMIAH, NATHAN, JAMES, G
2 CONC EMIMA GLEN SYMES, HANNAH GLEN AUSTIN, MARY GLEN HOPKINS, ELIZABETH GLEN BYARS, ANNA GLEN, THOMPSON GLEN, KEZIAH GLEN HARRIS, and SARAH GLENN DABNEY. Also named were grandchildren Frances Harris, Judy Harris, Anna Glen Harris, Moll
2 CONC ey Glen Harris, Peter Harris and Tyre Harris.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===The Will of James Glenn, Hanover County Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT written 11 June 1762, proved 3 Feb 1763[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, amen, the eleventh day of June 1762, I, James Glen of St. Martin's Parish, in the County of Hanover, Being praised be to God, of a sound mind and memory, do make this my Last Will and Testament, in manner and f
2 CONC orm following, that is to say first and principally I commend my soul to God, who gave it and my body to be buried at the discretion of my executors-hereafter named, and as touching such worldly estate as it hath pleased God to be
2 CONC stow on me, my desire is that the same be divided as is hereafter directed.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Item, I give to my son Gideon Glen all that part of my estate, as is present in his possession to him and his heirs forever,
2 CONT *Item: I give to my son Nehemiah Glen 300 acres of land on the upper side of Great Guinea Creek, including the plantation whereon he now lives and bounded as by a plan drawn by Robert Harris together with one half of the hogs and 
2 CONC cattle the said Nathan Glen received on the plantation, and three-fourth of the increase of the said hogs and cattle, and all the rest of my estate that is in his possession except a negro man named Pompey and a negro girl named N
2 CONC an, to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: I give to my son James Glen Three Hundred Fifty acres of land in Cumberland County on the upper side of Great Guinea Creek, bounded as by a plan drawn up by Robert Harris, and the sum of thirty five pounds current money and
2 CONC  that part of my stock of hogs and cattle that is in the hands of my son Nathan Glen, to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item, I give to my daughter, Jemima Symes all that part of my estate as is in her possession to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: I give to my grand daughter Frances Harris one negro boy named Daniel when she shall arrive at the age of eighteen years or marrys to her and her heirs forever. Item, I give to my granddaughter Jude Harris one negro boy nam
2 CONC ed Charles when she shall arrive at the age of eighteen years or marrys to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: I give to my granddaughter Anna Glen Harris one negro girl named Nan, when she shall arrive at the age of eighteen or marrys to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item, I give to my granddaughter Molley Glen Harris one negro boy named Dick when she shall arrive at the age of eighteen or marrys to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: I give to my grandson, Peter Harris one negro girl named Jenny to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: I give to my grandson Tyre Harris one negro boy named Harry to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item: my will and desire is that if either of my grandchildren, that is to say Frances Harris, Jude Harris, Anna Glen Harris, Molley Glen Harris, Peter Harris, and Tyre Harris should before they arrive at lawful age or Marrys hav
2 CONC e the misfortune to lose their negro given as before, the Loss to be made up out of the increase of Bess, if any, the mother of said negroes but if there should be no loss then the said negro woman Bess and her future increase sho
2 CONC uld be sold, and the money arising by such a sale to be equally divided between my six grandchildren that is to say Frances Harris, Judy Harris Anna Glen Harris Molley Glen Harris, Peter Harris and Tyre Harris or their heirs forev
2 CONC er.
2 CONT *Item I give to my daughter Mary Hopkins, all that part of my fore estate she has allready received to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item, I give to my daughter Elizabeth Byars forty pounds current money to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item I give to my daughter Anna Glen my best feather bed and furniture one good cow, and calf, and one pound ten shillings current money to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *Item, I lend to my loving wife Hannah Glen after the payment of my just debts and the Legacies in this will given, the plantation whereon I now live and four negroes, named Pompey, John, Nan, and Sarah and all my personal estate 
2 CONC not before given during her natural life and after her decease my will is that my daughter Anna Glen should have one negro woman named Sarah to her and her heirs forever, then the rest of my estate together with the land and plant
2 CONC ation where on I now live to the same of what kind whatsoever to descent to my son Thompson Glen and the heirs of his Body lawfully begotten forever.
2 CONT *Item my will and desire is that if my son Thompson Glen should die without lawful issue that then the estate lent to my loving wife and and given to him both real and personal shall be sold by my executors and the money arising f
2 CONC rom such sale to be equally divided between all my children except my daughters Keziah Glen and Sarah Dabney, who have already had their part of my estate.
2 CONT *Lastly I nominate constitute and appoint my sons Gideon Glen, Nehemiah Glen and Nathan Glen to be the Executors of this my Last Will and Testament hereby making void all other wills by me heretofore made. In witness whereof I hav
2 CONC e hereunto set my hand and seal the day and year before written.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed sealed and published in presence of Mary Gilbert Anna Gilbert his (seal) mark Henry Gilbert
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Court held for Hanover County on Thursday the 3rd day of February 1763, This Last Will and Testament of James Glenn decd. was offered to proof by Gideon Glenn one of the Exors. therein named and defered for want of witnesses 
2 CONC thereto. And at a Court helf or the said County on Thursday the 3rd of March following, the said will was proved by the oath of Henry Gilbert Gent, Mary Gilbert and Anna Gilbert, the said witnesses and admitted to record. And at a
2 CONC  court held for the said county on Thursday the 4th day of August in the year aforesaid, the said will was proved by the Oath of Gideon Glen and Nehemiah Glen two of the Executors thereto. Test April 1, 1822 William Pollard D.C.H.
2 CONC C. a copy teste Theo Pollad D.C. H. C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Hanover County to wit, I William Pollard Clerk of the Court of the said County, do hereby certify that the foregoing manuscript is a true copy from the records of the said County of Hanover. In witness where of I set my h
2 CONC and and applied the seal of the said County the first day of April One Thousand Eight Hundred and Twenty Two. William Pollard
2 CONT
2 CONT A presiding Justice of Hanover County State of Virginia, I do hereby certify that William Pollard who has signed and sealed the above certificate as Clerk of the said County of Hanover - is duly qualified as such, and to all his o
2 CONC fficial acts as clerk, full faith and credit is due and ought to be given. In witness whereof I have herto set my hand and seal this eighth day of April 1822. Jno Tinsely Recd $2.50 for this copy and for affixing the County Seal t
2 CONC hereto.
2 CONT
2 CONT The lst two certifications of WIlliam Pollard, Clerk of the Court, and Jno Tinsley, Justice, were affixed when a copy of the will was sent to Abbeville County SC in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT *http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/HARRIS-COLONIALVA/2003-06/1056910744 
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Glenn-80
2 CONT *[http://lva-linux-temp.hosted.exlibrisgroup.com:8991/F/VKLC8V2TK387SC1EJGJER2CTLUQK9VFFGVV4BC2GL6P242LL11-28375?func=full-set-set&set_number=004139&set_entry=000001&format=999 Virginia Library LVA Catalogue] Transcription of the 
2 CONC will, 11 June 1862, of James Glen (d. ca. 1762) of Hanover County, Virginia. The will was presented to the Hanover County Court for probate on 3 February 1763 and was proved by witnesses on 3 March 1763. This transcription is from
2 CONC  a copy made in 1822 which had been sent to Abbeville, South Carolina, as an exhibit in an equity suit. The Hanover County will book containing this instrument is no longer extant.
2 CONT --------------------
