1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT He was a clergyman and also a doctor.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to GENLOGY OF PENNSYLVANIA FAMILIES, p. 402-403 (see below), John was the son of Alexander Glen of Linlithgow, Scotland.  Yet his children and grandchildren have many of the same names as the family of Jeremiah GLENN (Lu
2 CONC cy, William, and the unusual names Warham and Tyree Glenn).  I believe that he was actually the son of James GLENN and Mourning WINN, of Virginia.  Rev. Dr. John Glenn's son Warham Glenn was deputy sheriff in Orange Co., NC at the
2 CONC  same time that Tyree Harris was sheriff. Tyree Harris is known to be son of Robert HARRIS and Mourning GLENN (daughter of James GLENN and Mourning WINN).
2 CONT
2 CONT GENLOGY OF PENNSYLVANIA FAMILIES, p. 402-403:
2 CONT John Glen, son of Alexander Glen of Lenlithgow, died before 1722 went to Orange Co., NC, married Sarah Jones, daughter of Robert Jones and Mrs. Ann (Duke) Christmas*, widow of Capt. Christmas and daughter of William Duke. Robert J
2 CONC ones was a son of Edward Jones of Shocco Creek and Abigail Sugan of King and Queen Co., VA Children of John Glen and Sarah Jones: Warham, Thomas, William, Duke, Dr. John, and Edward Glenn (in 1762 all young men, with residence in 
2 CONC Orange Co., NC)....this means that they all would have been born 1741 or earlier, because they were all "of age" by 1762.
2 CONT
2 CONT *the facts do not support that Sarah JONES was the daughter of Robert JONES and Mrs. Ann (DUKE) CHRISTMAS, because Ann DUKE married John CHRISTMAS about 1772 in Bute Co., NC, and after Captain CHRISTMAS' death she married Robert J
2 CONC ONES 18 Aug 1779 in Wake Co., NC.  Therefore Robert & Ann couldn't possibly have been the grandparents of the GLENN children who were born before 1741.  Robert & Ann were born LATER than their alleged grandchildren: Anne DUKE, dau
2 CONC ghter of William DUKE and Mary GREEN, was born about 1752, and Robert JONES, son of Edward JONES Jr. and Abigail SUGARS, was born 17 Mar 1752 in Granville Co., NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name started out as Nase or de Ness when the family left France, and stayed that way until it left Shrewsbury for Scotland. Then, however....
2 CONT
2 CONT As the story goes, Richard de la Glen of Bar arrived in Scotland (as a Nase or de Ness) in the mid-13th century under the broad arm of Edward I. His son is said to have commanded troops for the bishop of Glasgow against Wallace. H
2 CONC is grandson is said to have married a conveniently illegitimate daughter of Robert the Bruce. Fast forward to 1564. James Glen, his children, brother, kin, and friends were made answerable to the queen only, that is, exempt from a
2 CONC ny other law. This James commanded troops for the queen at Langside and, because he did, had had his estates forfeited in 1568, but restored five years later by the treaty of Perth. James's daughter Mary was said to have been one 
2 CONC of the four ladies in waiting of the queen (who apparently liked to have only those with the name Mary in that position). So much for the fancies of family history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Glenn family papers; Bulloch, A History of the Glenn Family
