2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.geni.com/people/Tery-Kempf-T510179-M510844-A789505-G040058/6000000012318135902 3 June 2019:
2 CONT
2 CONT >“William Harris and Temperance Overton only had three sons. Christopher Harris I, Overton Harris (married Anne Nelson) and Major Robert Harris II (married Mourning Glenn)” [https://s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/6c/d2/c9/6b
2 CONC /5344484c222519c3/f4ac9e4a-fd6e-41c4-92c9-6adec6b93d09_original.jpg link]
2 CONT
2 CONT == William Harris of Hanover County, Virginia==
2 CONT husband of Temperance Overton
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Capt William Harris''' first known to have been living in 'The Forks' area of Hanover Co. (St. Paul's Parish, New Kent Co. at that time) in 1704 where he owned 100 acres that year. He resided at ''''Cedar Hill'''' plantation lo
2 CONC cated on the North Anna River, in the forks of the Pamunkey River, St.Martin's Parish, Hanover Co. near Doswell, VA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had 2222 acres of land in various parts of Hanover Co., VA at various dates 1713, 1717, 1723, 1725. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a vestryman for St. Paul's Parish 1716 and later for St.Martin's Parish in 1724.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris married '''Temperance Overton''', a daughter of a wealthy tobacco grower, William Overton, and Mary Walters, his wife. The said William Overton was a son of Colonel (???) Overton, who commanded a Brigade of Ironside
2 CONC s under Oliver Cromwell. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris became also a tobacconist, raising and dealing in that weed, which was at that time a medium of exchange, and became fairly well off in this world's goods. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [PW, Curator: Who is the following referring to?] After immigrating from Wales he became a wealthy tobacco farmer. His home was in Brown's Cove, Albemarle County, Virginia. He was buried in an old Colonial Church at Weyanoke on th
2 CONC e James River. His bronze tablet was moved to Norfolk, Virginia and placed in the walls of St. Paul's old church.
2 CONT
2 CONT from Jesse Lawrence's family tree on World Connect: (http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jmljr&id=I97488)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT *(1) Lived on the land between the North Anna and Little Rivers, on the east side of what is now Route 188, near Doswell in Hanover County VA, close to the North Anna. Their place was to be called "Cedar Hill."
2 CONT *(1f) Served as Justice of Hanover County Virginia 
2 CONT *(1b) 1726, 21 Oct: William Harris and James Overton were replaced on the vestry of St. Paul's Parish.
2 CONT *(1b) 1731, Feb: His son William Jr. was present at the processioning of his land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 
2 CONT *(1g) 1 Feb 1733 his son Benjamin deeded land left by "the last will and testament of his deceased father, William Harris."
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Tobler Email: tobler@omniglobal.net 10/22/2001 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources include:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Harris Family of Virginia by Thomas H. Harris. Anderson Family Records, p. 141. 
2 CONT * Braxton (WVA) Hist. Soc. Journal 17 (1) of Mar 1989. 
2 CONT * The Virginia Genealogist 22:6 (1978). 
2 CONT * Tidewater Virginia Families by V. Davis (1989) Note: this has very good review of this family.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Correspondence from Bernice White of Houma, LA.  
2 CONT * Boddie, John B. Virginia Historical Genealogies, Vol. V; pg 128-129.  
2 CONT * World Family Tree, Vol. 1 Pedigree #336. GenServ  
2 CONT * Forinash, Rena V. 26611 Balerna Dr., Saugus, CA 91350 
2 CONT
2 CONT More About WILLIAM CLAIBORNE HARRIS, CAPT: Burial: Probably in the old Haris graveyard at Glen Cairn at Cedar Hill? which is near Doswell County, VA5 Lived location: Cedar Hill, St. Martin's Parish, Hanover County, VA; land was lo
2 CONC cated on the North Anna River near Doswell, VA5 Occupation: 1706, Key River, Surveyor; Vestryman, St. Paul's & later St Martin's6
2 CONT
