2 CONT from Jesse Lawrence's family tree on World Connect: (http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jmljr&id=I97488)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cedar Hill Cemetery is an ancient cemetery in good condition and is being maintained. It is believed to be the oldest cemetery in Hanover County, since it contains the oldest known grave in the county, the grave of Temperance 
2 CONC Overton Harris.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cemetery is located in a mature forest with huge hardwoods. The grave of Thomas Harris is enclosed in a wrought iron fence. This ancient cemetery is believed to be the largest private family cemetery in the State of Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cedar Hill - This was a very early home to the Harris family. William Harris and his wife Temperance Overton lived at Cedar Hill. This house was in good shape until a few years ago when Kings Dominion amusement park bought the lan
2 CONC d and destroyed the house. It was a 1 1/2 story house, said to have been log cabin with frame on the outside.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cedar Hill house was destroyed several years ago by the property owner. Time frame in 1980's. House set on fire and burned for fire department to practice firefighting. Development pressure strikes another blow to Harris heritage 
2 CONC and history.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Bobby Wood, Residing on the banks of the South Anna River in Historic Hanover County, VA) 
2 CONT =<nowiki>------------------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT from Jesse Lawrence's family tree on World Connect: (http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jmljr&id=I97488)
2 CONT
2 CONT There seems to be a quite a variation in information relative to Captain Thomas HARRIS, his wives and his children, part of which is caused by the similarity of several William HARRIS' existing at about the same time and in nearly
2 CONC  the same place.
2 CONT
2 CONT This has been addressed by Malcolm Hart Harris of West Point, Virginia in his three articles in Volume 22 of The Virginia Genealogist, starting on pages 3, 99, and 187. The first page of Hart's works entitled THREE WILLIAM HARRISE
2 CONC S IN HANOVER COUNTY states the following: "A good many years ago, the finding of three grants made on 24 March 1725 to three patentees named William Harris prompted a study in some detail of the three men, their families and their
2 CONC  lands in the counties of Hanover and Louisa...The first of these three, recorded as Capt. William Harris and William Harris, Gent., was certainly the well known Capt. William Harris of Hanover County who resided in the Forks of t
2 CONC he Pamunkey River at his plantation called Cedar Hill...
2 CONT
2 CONT The second William Harris was designated as Junior in the patent, to identify him as a son or a younger man. It seems reasonably certain that he was a son of Capt. William Harris as the records prove.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third William Harris was without rank or any identifying connection, except his association with George Alves in the records of St. Paul's Parish and patents of adjoining lands in Hanover County. The three tracts of land which
2 CONC  were granted on 24 March 1725 lay in widely separated areas of the county of Hanover, which in 1725 included Louisa County. The two tracts granted Capt. William Harris and William Harris Gent. lay along Little River and below the
2 CONC  North Anna River. That granted William Harris, Junior, was also on Little River but about twenty miles to the west. The tract granted the third William Harris lay along Cub Creek which lies wholly within the county of Louisa and 
2 CONC is about six miles from the nearest tract on upper Little River."
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart goes on to identify Captain William Harris as marrying Temperance, the daughter of William Overton and Elizabeth Waters, his wife, who also lived in the Forks.
2 CONT
2 CONT This seems to identify the Harris/Overton line as living on the Forks of the Pamunkey River at a place called Cedar Hill. There does not seem to be any reference to the parents of this first Captain William Harris in Hart's works.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart in his second article covers The Second William Harris of Hanover, again as part of the Harris/Overton line and living in the Little River area.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his third article Hart tells about The Third William Harris of Hanover and identifies him as having land on Cub Creek in Hanover County. This William Harris is called Major William Harris and is associated with George Alves. Ge
2 CONC orge Alves later marries the widow of this Major William Harris. This association is also covered by William R. Taylor writing also in Volume 22 of The Virginia Genealogist starting on page 261.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Adventurers of Purse and Person, VIRGINIA, 1607-1625, compiled and edited by Annie Lash Jester, second edition 1964, it is stated p. 202-205, 383-384 that Thomas Harris repatented 700 acres in Henrico, 12 July 1637, "called by 
2 CONC the name of the Longfield with swamp and marshes". It continues on telling about Captain Thomas Harris' daughter Mary Ligon conveying 200 acres in Curles, Henrico County "being part of a greater divident granted unto Captain Thoma
2 CONC s Harris deceased and given by will of said Thomas Harris unto his daughter Mary Ligon. This article goes on to state that William Harris (Major)(son of Captain Thomas Harris) fell heir to "Longfield" later known as Curles. He mar
2 CONC ried (1)_____ (later identified as Lucy Stewart) and (2) Alice_____ whose (2) husband George Alvis,...is named as having married "the relict of Major William Harris deceased".
2 CONT
2 CONT The above article lists on pages 384 and 385 the wills of Major William Harris and his brother Thomas Harris, both indicating association with the Ligons. These are found in Henrico County Records (Deeds & Wills) 1677-1692 on p. 6
2 CONC 8 and 90,91.
2 CONT
2 CONT No where in these articles is named Robert Harris, who married Mary Claiborne Rice,as the son of Captain Thomas Harris. This seems to be brought up by Ligon writing in The Ligon Family and Connections, 1947, on p. 844.
2 CONT
2 CONT In conclusion it appears that the articles named rather clearly identify two different families of William Harris, one which is the Harris/Overton line and does not seem to come from Captain Thomas Harris, and the other line which
2 CONC  continues down through Major William Harris and is further identified as associaed with George Alves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Searching for the grave site and year William Harris died. I have seen Temperance (Overton) Harris grave. I don't believe he is buried beside her.
2 CONT ========================== 
2 CONT Disambiguation: The following Information is about William Claiborne Harris, 1669-1725, or another William Harris who died 1687--these are likely NOT the same as the William Harris, 1652-1733, who married Temperance Overton.
2 CONT
2 CONT THIS PERTAINS TO THIS WM HARRIS BUT NOT THE ONE WHO MARRIED T. OVERTON WHO IS A DIFFERENT WM HARRIS BORN ABOUT THE SAME TIME. (PRT) <nowiki>----</nowiki> He was originally buried in a Colonial Church at Weyanoke on the James River
2 CONC . After the church went to ruin, on July 1, 1875, his bronze tablet was moved to Norfolk and placed in the walls of St. Paul's old church.
2 CONT
2 CONT On it is this engraving:
2 CONT *"Here lyeth ye body of
2 CONT *William Harris
2 CONT *who departed this life ye 8th day of March, 1687
2 CONT *aged 35 years"
2 CONT
2 CONT Source on Col Harris: THE BOONE FAMILY by Hazel Spraker. Barbara Brown <BBrown6575@aol.com>@worldconnect 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He died and was originally buried in a Colonial Church at Weyanoke on the James River. After the church went to ruin, on July 1, 1875, his bronze tablet was moved to Norfolk and placed in the walls of St. Paul's old church. Burges
2 CONC s for Hanover and Henrico counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT William HARRIS outfitted his son, Benjamin's, regiment for the Battle of Yorktown (1781)--which William?
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- http://www.juch.org/gedpages/fam/fam04911.htm -------------------- William was known as William of Cedar Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel William Claiborne /Harris/ II He was born about 1665, Hanover County, Colony of Virginia 1669, Hanover Co, Colony of Virginia 1672, Henrico, Virginia, United States 1672, Hanover Co, Colony of Virginia 1675, New Kent, Virg
2 CONC inia, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1695 at St. Peters Church, Hanover, New Kent, Virginia, British North America 1675, Weyanoke, Virginia, British North America 
2 CONT
2 CONT His children were  
2 CONT * Elizabeth Harris 
2 CONT * Major Robert Overton Harris 
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Harris died  1720, Norfolk, Norfolk, Virginia, United States, age 48 1720, Cedarhill, Hanover, Virginia Colony 1730, Cedarhill, Hanover, Virginia, USA
2 CONT
