1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Temperance Overton
2 CONT *Born 2 Mar 1679 in The Forks, New Kent County, Colony of Virginia
2 CONT *Died 19 Feb 1710 or 1716  in Cedar Hill, Hanover  County, Colony of Virginia. In 1934, The family put new writing on the back of the original stone with an incorrect death date of 1710.
2 CONT * Burial: Cedar Hill Cemetery Hanover County, Virginia, USA.  Hers may be the earliest known grave site in Hanover County.
2 CONT * Daughter of Elizabeth Waters  and William Overton. 
2 CONT *Married 1695 Pamunkey River St Peter Parish New Kent Co VA William Harris. 
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris (son of Robert Harris) had 6 children With Temperance Overton (daughter of William Overton)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I114938&tree=tree1 cites 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virginia Genealogist Volume 22, 1978, 3. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No family Bible record is known to exist and the will of Capt. William Harris was lost in the fire which destroyed the Hanover County records. The following list of children of Capt. William Harris has been derived from both court
2 CONC  and family records:
2 CONT * (1) Major Robert Harris of Albemarle County
2 CONT * (2) Benjamin Harris of Hanover County
2 CONT * (3) William Harris, Junior, of St. Paul's Parish
2 CONT * (4) John Harris of Hanover County
2 CONT * (5) James Harris of Hanover County
2 CONT * (6) Jemima Harris who married her cousin William Overton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.geni.com/people/Tery-Kempf-T510179-M510844-A789505-G040058/6000000012318135902 3 June 2019:
2 CONT
2 CONT >“William Harris and Temperance Overton only had three sons. Christopher Harris I, Overton Harris (married Anne Nelson) and Major Robert Harris II (married Mourning Glenn)” [https://s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/6c/d2/c9/6b
2 CONC /5344484c222519c3/f4ac9e4a-fd6e-41c4-92c9-6adec6b93d09_original.jpg link]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===William HARRIS RESIDENCES: 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris was living in New Kent county in 1704, landowner in St. Paul's Parish in New Kent in 1707; between 1713 - 1725 he patented over 2000 acres in what was to become Hanover County. His land lay between the North Anna an
2 CONC d Little Rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT His home was called "Cedar Hill" in Hanover County, Virginia. It was a 1-1/2 story log cabin, covered with  clapboard with at least one chimney on one end. An early photograph does not give a view which shows the  other end of the
2 CONC  house. The house had a doorway in the center of the front elevation and one window on  either side of the front door. There was an extension, shed type, off the rear. When King's Dominion, a  modern day amusement park, purchased 
2 CONC the property, they removed the house, still in good condition at the  time of the sale.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris' initials are on his wife's tombstone in the family graveyard, located on the northeast perimeter  of the above-mentioned amusement park, outside the park fence.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Temperance OVERTON ISSUE: 5 children with William Harris, 1st son died young and named second son same name, Robert.  1 child with Baker marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: Her death date is taken from her tombstone. There are some researchers who give the year as 1716, while others put it at 1710.  The 1710 date came about after Temperance's family restored her headstone, listing the date err
2 CONC oneously.  
2 CONT
2 CONT TOMBSTONE: Unusual for the time and for the isolated nature of their homeplace, William had a stone placed at her grave, which has survived because descendants later placed the stone in a large block of  granite to preserve it.
2 CONT
2 CONT "HERE LYES THE BODY OF TEMPERANCE HARRIS  DECEASED THE 19 OF FEBY1716 WH"
2 CONT
2 CONT GRAVEYARD: This graveyard is on land currently owned by King's Dominion, an amusement theme park, off Virginia Route 688, south of Virginia Route 30. The home "Cedar Hill" was removed by the amusement park.
2 CONT
2 CONT Directions: I95 north to Doswell-Kings Dominion exit = Route 30. Go East on Route 30, and cross the railroad tracks. Immediately turn to right on the road running parallel to tracks. Take the second left turn to sign Private Prope
2 CONC rty, winding road- to Kings Dominion water tank.  The cemetery is just on the north side of the water tank.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "History and Genealogies", by W.H. Miller, published by Press of Transylvania Co., Lexington KY, 1907:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Temperance Overton, (the wife of William Harris) came to this country with three brothers and settled in Virginia. She was a daughter of William Overton, and Mary Waters, William Overton was a Colonel under Oliver Cromwell, and c
2 CONC ommanded one wing of the army at the battle of Dunbar; for some cause he was cast into the Tower of London by Cromwell, and died there." 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT The eight children of William Overton and Elizabeth Waters, born at New Kent County, Virginia Colony, were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elizabeth b 28-6-1673-1735, m. Captain Robert Anderson
2 CONT # William 1675-d 18 Jun 1759; m. Margaret "Peggy" Garland.
2 CONT # Temperance b. 1678/79, New Kent, VA, d 19 Feb 1716, Hanover, VA. She md Capt. William Claiborne Harris abt 1695, son of Maj. Robert Harris and Elizabeth (Mary) Claiborne.
2 CONT # Margaret Overton, 1680-1704. no spouse
2 CONT # Samuel b: 14 AUG 1685 d 1759 Hanover, VA; m. Anne Carr 1710, Hanover, VA
2 CONT # James, Captain, b: 14 AUG 1688 in St. Martin's Parish, Hanover County, Virginia d 18 Jun 1749, Louisa, VA; md Elizabeth Garland.
2 CONT # Truhart Overton, 1689 - bef 1725. m. Elizabeth
2 CONT # Barbara b: 05 FEB 1690 -1766, m. William Winston
2 CONT
