1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A102836  DAR Ancestor # A102836]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shelton was born in 1731 in Albemarle Co., VA, Son of Thomas Shelton and Lucy (Austin) Shelton
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Lucy Harris in VA about 1754. Together they had 8 children - 4 sons and 4 daughters - 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Thomas; 
2 CONT #Mourning Harris (M-Archibald Woods); 
2 CONT #Lucy (M-Elliott Brown); 
2 CONT #William Harris; 
2 CONT #Sarah (M-Matthew Grubbs); 
2 CONT #Dabney; 
2 CONT #Agnes; and 
2 CONT #Weatherston 
2 CONT
2 CONT between 1755 and 1772.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 2) to Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/181105297/william-shelton
2 CONT * Son of Thomas Shelton and Lucy (Austin) Shelton
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Shelton-2128
2 CONT
2 CONT Married (1) Lucy Harris b. 12 Apr 1734, daughter of Robert Harris (2) Sarah
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT # William Jr., emigrated from Albemarle to Kentucky
2 CONT # Mourning m. Archibald Woods
2 CONT # Sarah, unmarried.
2 CONT # Dabney, sold out in 1817 to Francis McGee, living in Augusta County at the time.
2 CONT # Lucy m. Elliott Brown
2 CONT # Agnes, unmarried.
2 CONT # Weatherstone m. Elizabeth Harrison, moved to Mason County, Virginia.
2 CONT # Thomas, also sold out in 1817 to Francis McGee, living in Augusta County at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Executor of Robert Overton Harris' will (father of wife Lucy Harris).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Lucy [Harris], married William Shelton ; among their descend ants were Thomas Shelton Moberly of Kentucky, Rev. R. M. Dudley, once President of Georgetown College, and A. C. Caperton. editor of the Western Recorder."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT DNA Match with me
2 CONT
2 CONT Served in the American Revolutionary War, member of the Public Safety Committee, signer of the Albemarle Declaration of Independence, April 21, 1779.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen. I, William Shelton of the County of Albemarle,
2 CONT being weak in body, yet sound in mind and memory, do make this my last will
2 CONT and testament, revoking and disannulling all other wills by me heretofore
2 CONT made.
2 CONT Imprimis: I recommend my soul to God in sure and certain hope, that by
2 CONT the death and passion of my dear Redeemer Jesus Christ he will receive me
2 CONT into heaven there to join the blessed choir in singing glory to God and the
2 CONT Lamb forever world without end Amen. My worldly (word ommitted) that it has
2 CONT pleased God to help me with I give and bequeath in the following manner:
2 CONT (Viz.)-ITEM: I give to my daughter, Mourning Shelton Woods, one negro
2 CONT girl named Henrietta and her Increase, one feather bed and furniture and
2 CONT one side saddle to her and her heirs forever, said negro girl Henrietta has
2 CONT already several children. I mean by her increase those children that she
2 CONT now has, and those that she may hereafter have, said girl feather bed and
2 CONT sadddle are now in my daughter Mourning's possession.
2 CONT ITEM: I give to my daughter Sarah Shelton one negro boy named Tom, one
2 CONT feather bed and furniture , a sorrell colt now two years old and a side
2 CONT saddle. To her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT ITEM: I give to my daughter Agnes Shelton one negro boy named Barnett,
2 CONT one feather bed and furniture, a bay colt about two years old, and one side
2 CONT saddle, to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT ITEM: I give unto my last mentioned daughters viz. Sarah and Agnes all
2 CONT that part of the tract of land on which I now live on the North West side
2 CONT of Mitcham's River, during their natural lives, or during the time they
2 CONT remain unmarried, and in case one of my said dauthters should marry or die
2 CONT the other shall, that remains unmarried, or alive as the case my be possess
2 CONT the whole of the land then given during her life, or while she remain
2 CONT unmarried. In case both these my daughters should or shall marry, and in
2 CONT any event at their death it is my will and desire that this part of my land
2 CONT shall he possessed by my son Weatherton Shelton and I do hereby give it
2 CONT unto him, so soon as these my daughters shall marry or die. I do further
2 CONT lend unto my daughter Sarah Shelton, a negro girl named Sarah during her
2 CONT natural life, at her death it is my will that she and her increase be
2 CONT divided among the rest of my children, equally provided she may have
2 CONT increase, in any event the property of the girl is to be vested in the rest
2 CONT of my children.
2 CONT ITEM: I give to my son William Shelton one negro boy named Nelson,one
2 CONT feather bed and furniture, also a mare which property is now in my said
2 CONT son's possession. I think it is unnecessary to say anything about land, as
2 CONT I have given this my son a right to land in the state of Kentucky. The
2 CONT above negro, bed and furniture and horse I give to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT ITEM: I lend to my daughter Lucy Brown one feather bed and furniture and
2 CONT a saddle, one negro woman and all the children of said woman, which she now
2 CONT has, or may hereafter have, during her natural life said negro woman and
2 CONT children, feather bed and furniture and saddle are now in possession of my
2 CONT daughter. At the death of this my daughter Lucy Brown, it is my will and
2 CONT desire that the property thus lent to her during her natural life shall be
2 CONT equally diveded amongst her children at her death. I give it to her
2 CONT children and their heirs forever.
2 CONT ITEM: I give and bequeath unto my son Weatherton Shelton all that part of
2 CONT land in the County of Albemarle lying on the South East side of Mitcham's
2 CONT River to him and his heirs forever. Yet it is my will that my son
2 CONT Weatherton shall not become possessed of this land thus willed to him until
2 CONT the death of his Mother, my wife according to the provision which I
2 CONT hereafter make for her, should he die without lawful heirs, it is my will
2 CONT and desire that the land mentioned in the ITEM where I make provision for
2 CONT my daughters Sarah and Agnes shall be sold on a credit of two years and the
2 CONT money arising from the sale be equally divided among my children, or lawful
2 CONT representatives. I do further give my son Weatherton an negro womean named
2 CONT Bartlett, a feather bed and furnitre to him and his heirs forever, said
2 CONT negro woman is now in possession of this my son.
2 CONT ITEM: It is my will and desire (in case that a claim that I now have to a
2 CONT tract of land on Browning's Creek in the state of Kentucky should be
2 CONT established) that my three grandchildren, the children of my deceased son
2 CONT Thomas Shelton, viz; Lucy Shelton, Susannah Shelton and Elizabeth Shelton,
2 CONT shall become possessed of it. And in case my right is established I hereby
2 CONT give and bequeath it unto these my grand children to them and their heirs
2 CONT forever. To be equally divided among them agreeably to quantity and quality.
2 CONT ITEM: I lend to my beloved wife Lucy Shelton, the land whereon I now
2 CONT live, also the following (viz) David, Rose, Rosana, Sherod, Betty, Gardner,
2 CONT Ben, Billy, Jesse and Judy and her children and all her personal estate out
2 CONT of which all my just debts are to be paid during her natural life and after
2 CONT her death, my desire is that all the negroes and personal estate be equally
2 CONT divided among my children and their heirs, Viz. William Harris Shelton,
2 CONT Mourning Woods, Sarah Shelton, Lucy Brown during her life and at her death
2 CONT to go to her children, Agnes Shelton, and Weatherton Shelton, the children
2 CONT of my deceased son Thomas one child's part. On my wife's death I give to
2 CONT them and their heirs forever.
2 CONT I do hereby constitute and appoint my friends Joel Harris and John R.
2 CONT Kerr and my son Weatherton Executors of this my last will and testament. In
2 CONT witness whereof I have hereunto annexed my hand and seal this 13th day of
2 CONT July eighteen hundred and two.
2 CONT Signed and sealed in presence of:
2 CONT John Gillum
2 CONT William Fretwell
2 CONT William C.Maupin
2 CONT Austin Shelton
2 CONT Wm.Davenport
2 CONT at Albemarle December Court, 1803
2 CONT William Shelton
2 CONT
2 CONT (seal)
2 CONT
2 CONT Will Book IV, Page 136
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Served in the American Revolutionary War
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shelton was born in 1731 in Albemarle Co., VA. He married Lucy Harris in VA about 1754. Together they had 8 children - 4 sons and 4 daughters - Thomas; Mourning Harris (M-Archibald Woods); Lucy (M-Elliott Brown); William H
2 CONC arris; Sarah (M-Matthew Grubbs); Dabney; Agnes; and Weatherston between 1755 and 1772.
2 CONT
2 CONT As documented in the VA Early Census Records, William is in Christ Church Parish, Lancaster Co., VA in 1750. On May 9, 1772 he purchased 35 acres on the South Branch of the Potomac - Hanover Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the American Revolutionary War he resided at Albemarle Co., VA and assisted in establishing American Independence while acting in the capacity of Patriotic Service. William Furnished Supplies to the Army during the Revoluti
2 CONC onary War; Daughters of the American Revolutionary Patriot #A102836 - Abercrombie & Slatten VA Rev Publick Claims Vol 1 P 13. William was a signer of the Albemarle Declaration of Independence of Apr 21, 1779.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1780 he was living in Albemarle Co., VA. The census listed his family as having 1 free male above 21; 11 slaves: 26 Cattle; 7 Horses, mares, colts & mules; No wheels for Riding Carriags; No Billiard Tables. This is
2 CONT from U.S. Census Reconstructed Records, 1660-1820. In 1790 William and his family are living in Fredricksville Parish. Albemarle Co., VA ("Road" is written on the right side of his name). His family is listed as having 3 white mal
2 CONC es over 16 yr. old; 6 Blacks above 16; 2 Blacks between 12 & 16; 5 Horses; No Wheels; Ordinary licences. From U.S. Census Reconstructed Records, 1660-1820
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1800 William and his family are living in the District of Fredricksville Parish, Albmarle Co., VA; his household is listed as 2 White Males Over 16 Years of Age; 9 Blacks over 16 years of age; 1 Black between 12-16 years of age
2 CONC ; 9 Horses; 0 Stud Horses; 0 Ordinary Licenses; 0 Carriage Wheels; Retailing
2 CONT From U.S. Census Reconstructed Records, 1660-1820
2 CONT
2 CONT William will die Jul 13, 1803 in Albemarle Co., VA at the age of 72.
