1 OCCU Farmer, Lawyer, Politician, Judge, Sec. of War and Treasury (Madison and Monroe administrations), Sen. President pro tempore, US Ambassador to France
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Harris_Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT William Harris Crawford (February 24, 1772 – September 15, 1834) was an American politician and judge during the early 19th century. He served as United States Secretary of War from 1815 to 1816 and United States Secretary of the 
2 CONC Treasury from 1816 to 1825, and was a candidate for President of the United States in 1824.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political career
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1803, Crawford was elected to the Georgia House of Representatives as a member of the Democratic-Republican Party. In 1807, Crawford joined the 10th United States Congress mid-term as the junior U.S. Senator from Georgia when t
2 CONC he Georgia legislature elected him to replace George Jones, an appointee who had held the office for a few months after the death of Abraham Baldwin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford was elected President pro tempore in 1811. When Vice President George Clinton died on April 20, 1812, Crawford, as President pro tempore, became "Acting Vice President" until March 4, 1813.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1813, President James Madison appointed Crawford as the U.S. minister to France during the waning years of the First French Empire; Crawford held that ministerial post until 1815, shortly after the end of the War of 1812.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Crawford's return, Madison appointed him as Secretary of War. After slightly more than a year of satisfactory service in that post (and after disclaiming interest in the 1816 Democratic-Republican nomination for President), C
2 CONC rawford moved within the Cabinet to become Secretary of the Treasury. He remained in that position through the rest of Madison's term and Monroe's entire administration which ended in 1825.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford was again a leading candidate for the Democratic-Republican presidential nomination in 1824. However, Crawford was put out of the running because of a paralytic stroke he suffered in 1823 that was brought on by a prescrip
2 CONC tion given to him by his physician.[1] The Democratic-Republican Party split around this time and one of the splinter groups nominated Crawford. Despite Crawford's improved health (and the support of former presidents Madison and 
2 CONC Thomas Jefferson), he finished third in the electoral vote, behind John Quincy Adams and Andrew Jackson. He thus was still in the running when the Presidential election ended up in the House of Representatives, but his stroke made
2 CONC  him a non-factor there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Refusing Adams's request that he remain at the Treasury, Crawford then returned to Georgia, where he was appointed as a state superior court judge. Crawford remained an active judge until his death a decade later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Societies
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1820s, Crawford was a member of the prestigious society, Columbian Institute for the Promotion of Arts and Sciences, who counted among their members former presidents Andrew Jackson and John Quincy Adams and many promin
2 CONC ent men of the day, including well-known representatives of the military, government service, medical and other professions.[2] Crawford also served as a Vice President in the American Colonization Society from its formation in 18
2 CONC 17 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford was born in Amherst County, Virginia, but his family moved south to Appling County, Georgia, when he was a boy. As a young man, he worked as a farmer and a schoolteacher for about 10 years, then began to practice law in L
2 CONC exington, Georgia, in 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT His cousin George W. Crawford served as Secretary of War under President Zachary Taylor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford was buried in Crawford Cemetery in Crawford, Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT The following are named in honor of William H. Crawford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cities and Towns
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford, Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawfordville, Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawfordsville, Indiana
2 CONT
2 CONT Counties
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Arkansas
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Indiana
2 CONT
2 CONT  Crawford County, Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Michigan
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Missouri
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford County, Wisconsin
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford is buried in Crawford, Georgia. In 1875, Crawford appeared on the 50 cent bill. Find A Grave#9103117
