1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/William-Brown-of-Washington-County/6000000041046710203?through=6000000180167576823 who married https://www.geni.com/people/Christiana-Brown/6000000019177695334
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William "Billy" Brown (1735–1780); born in Hanover Co.; m. Sarah Jennings. He was a son of Benjamin Brown and Miss Wilson.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William "Billy" Brown''' (1737 – 1780) was a mid-18th-century planter and frontier settler who migrated from Virginia to South Carolina. Born in Hanover County, Virginia, he was a son of Benjamin Brown, Sr. and his first wife, 
2 CONC '''Wilson'''. Along with his brother Bartlett, he was a primary figure in the southern migration of the "B" Brown family.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Early Life and Virginia Residency ===
2 CONT While some modern records place his birth in Albemarle County, contemporary land records indicate the family did not settle in Brown's Cove until approximately 1747. Consequently, William was likely born in '''Hanover County'''. H
2 CONC e spent his early adulthood in the Albemarle region, where he served as an executor of his father's estate in 1762. Following the settlement of the estate, he and his brother Bartlett returned briefly to Hanover to manage their fa
2 CONC ther's original holdings before moving permanently south.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Migration to South Carolina ===
2 CONT In 1769, William relocated to South Carolina, settling at '''Brier’s Creek''', opposite Burton's Ferry on the Savannah River (in what became the Barnwell District). He established a plantation and became a figure of some standing 
2 CONC in the backcountry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Revolutionary War and Death ===
2 CONT William's life was cut short by the partisan violence of the American Revolution. In May 1780, he was killed at his home at Brier's Creek by a band of **Tories**. Family tradition, documented in the Reuben Dabney Brown records, no
2 CONC tes that one of his young sons was killed alongside him during the raid.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriages and Family ===
2 CONT William Brown is recorded as having married twice:
2 CONT * First to '''Sarah Jennings'''.
2 CONT * Second to '''Christiana Thompson'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children included:
2 CONT * '''Tarlton Brown''' (1757–1845) – A noted officer in the Revolutionary War who authored a well-known memoir of the conflict.
2 CONT * '''Bartlett Brown''' 
2 CONT * '''William Brown'''
2 CONT * '''James Brown'''
2 CONT * '''John Brown'''
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes ===
2 CONT Forensic auditing is required to distinguish William from his brother, Bartlett Brown. Because the two brothers migrated together and held neighboring lands in South Carolina, they are frequently conflated in digital genealogies a
2 CONC s "Bartlett William Brown." However, their father’s 1762 Albemarle County will explicitly names them as two separate individuals. Furthermore, care must be taken to distinguish this William Brown from his younger half-brothers bor
2 CONC n to Sarah Thompson back in Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT * '''Brown Family Bible:''' Transcribed by Eleanor Williams Bradley, ''Virginia Genealogist'', Vol. 13, 1969.
2 CONT * '''Albemarle County Will Book 2, Page 130:''' Will of Benjamin Brown, Sr. (1762).
2 CONT * '''Memoirs of Tarleton Brown:''' A Captain in the Revolutionary Army, written by himself (provides context on the family's migration and the death of William Brown).
2 CONT * '''Pettibon, Bessie Z.:''' "The William 'Billy' Brown Family of Brown’s Cove, Virginia who settled in South Carolina," USGenWeb Project (2009).
