1 NAME Tyree Harris /Bell/
2 GIVN Tyree Harris
2 SURN Bell
2 _MARNM Bell
1 NAME General Tyree Harris Bell, CSA
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 SEP 1815
2 PLAC Covington, Kentucky
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 AUG 1902
2 ADDR
3 CITY New Orleans
3 STAE Louisiana
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 6 SEP 1902
2 PLAC Fresno, California, USA
2 ADDR
1 OCCU farmer, stock raiser, and trader
1 TITL General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyree_H._Bell
2 CONT
2 CONT http://scvcalifornia.blogspot.com/2008/06/general-tyree-h-bell-camp-1804-fresno.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyree Harris Bell (5 September 1815 - 1 September 1902) was born in Covington, Kentucky. He grew up on his family's Tennessee plantation, and later began a plantation of his own. He attained the rank Brigadier General in 1865. In 
2 CONC 1864, he held the rank Colonel, and had distinguished himself as a cavalry commander under General Nathan Bedford Forrest. He came to be known as "Forrest's Right Arm". 
2 CONT
2 CONT  He was an ardent Confederate supporter, and recruited a company for the 12th Tennessee Infantry early in 1861. Elected captain of the company, he soon advanced to become lieutenant colonel of the 12th Tennessee. Bell led troops a
2 CONC t the Battles of Belmont (Missouri) and Shiloh (where he was wounded in the leg by the fall of his horse, one of two shot from under him during the battle). He then took part in the Battle of Richmond (Kentucky). Transferred to ca
2 CONC valry command, he participated in the Battles of Perryville and Stone's River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Major General Nathan Bedford Forrest recruited Bell when the General was organizing an independent command in West Tennessee and northern Mississippi in 1863. Bell served with Forrest until the end of the war. Initially, Bell serv
2 CONC ed as a recruiting and conscript officer. In April, 1864, Bell participated in the Battle of Fort Pillow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bell was eventually given command of a brigade, although he was not commissioned a Brigadier General until 28 February 1865. He received the commission as a result of his service in the Battle of Brice's Cross Roads, his work with
2 CONC  various Tennessee campaigns and in his leadership and combat in Mississippi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After being defeated around Selma, Alabama, Maj. Gen. Forrest's troops and officers, including Bell, were surrendered. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Bell moved to Fresno, California and became a farmer. He died in 1902 while traveling through New Orleans, Louisiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyree_H._Bell
