1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eddleman, Rebecca E. of Newton County was a member of the Sophomore Class of the Southern Masonic Female College in Covington, for the scholastic year ending June 27, 1855. 
2 CONT
2 CONT GRANDMA BAKER CELEBRATES NINETY-EIGHTH BIRTHDAY MONDAY
2 CONT
2 CONT First Came to Bryan in Horse and Buggy Days of 1873; Has Seen Disasters of Four Wars that America Has Fought
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mrs. R. A. Baker, better known as "Grandma Baker" moved to Bryan in 1873, this city was only a tiny village. There were no paved streets, modern brick buildings, car, beautiful home--only muddy trails, wooden shacks for store
2 CONC s, horses and buggies,, a few substantial home. Bryan was then a pioneer town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today at 98, Grandma Baker can say that she is one of the very few living who has seen Bryan grow and progress into the modern little city that it is today. She has seen the high=speed automobile replace the buggy and she has seen
2 CONC  the development and use of all modern conveniences that we of today enjoy. Celebrates Birthday Monday, Grandma Baker celebrated her ninety-eighth birthday--an event dear to her and her friends. the Philathea Class of the First Me
2 CONC thodist Church sent a beautiful two-tier cake with 98 candles on it, and that cake remained uncut until all of her friends had seen it. All Sunday and Monday members of the Philathea Class, the Methodist Church and a large number 
2 CONC of other friends called to wish her a happy birthday. No other day in the year could possibly be so happy for her, for on that day the people from Bryan and Brazos County remember her by visits and many lovely presents. Grandma Ba
2 CONC ker, the daughter of William and Permelia Eddelman, was born in Covington, Newton County, Georgia. She lived in that little town most of her early life, except her last school days which were spent at Southern Masonic Female Colle
2 CONC ge. Married in 1858 On March 2, 1853, Rebecca Eddelman and William Batts Baker were married. Mr. Baker was the son of the Hon. Lawrence Baker, who was for many years a member of the Georgia Legislature. Three years before the star
2 CONC t of the Civil War, the Bakers moved to the northern part of Georgia. When the North and South began fighting, Mr. Baker and two of Mrs. Baker's brothers joined the army. Mr. Baker was in the Eight Georgia Battalion, Gist's Brigad
2 CONC e, C.S.A., and first sergeant in Company A. It was during a battle in Virginia that youngest brother, who was in the troop under the leadership of General Stonewall Jackson was killed. Gen. Johnson received a wound in the same bat
2 CONC tle that finally resulted in his death. Not only the Civil War has Grandma Baker lived through. She has been touched by the horror and disaster of the Mexican War, the Spanish-American War and the World War. "I have lived through 
2 CONC four wars," she says, "and God grant that they will be the last." Since she has been in Bryan, Grandma Baker has been a very active member in the First Methodist Church. She became a Methodist at the age of 12, for as she says, "t
2 CONC hey just couldn't make anything but a Methodist out of me, I wouldn't be anything else." She is mother of the Philathea Class here and until a few years ago she went regularly to church and Sunday School. Spelling Her Specialty Sp
2 CONC elling was always "pie" to Grandma Baker. She could spell almost any word anyone would give her. Once there was a spelling bee in Abilene. A very large number of contestants were lined around two rooms. The person conducting the b
2 CONC ee gave out the word "au revoir." The word was passed from one to another on down the line, and not a person could spell it correctly. Then Grandma Baker, who was merely witnessing the bee, said, "I'll take just one chance at that
2 CONC --a-u-r-e-v-o-i-r." Once when she was 92, one of her grandsons took her out in the airplane field and began kidding her about taking a ride in the plane. She told him that she wanted very much to go up, but he thought she was mere
2 CONC ly joking. Not until she began to climb in the plane did he realize that she really meant it, so there was nothing for him to do but go too. Now Mrs. Baker ways she's just waiting until she is 100 years old until she goes up again
2 CONC . And she really means to go up again, too. Many Descendants One of Mrs. Baker's three living children resides in Bryan"--Mrs. L. M. Johnson. A son, E.H. Baker, lives in Abilene and a daughter, Mrs. Florence Andrews lives in Oklah
2 CONC oma City. There are now five generations in five different branches of the family. Mrs. Baker has three children, fourteen grandchildren, fourteen great grandchildren and eight great great grandchildren. Mr. Baker died in 1918 and
2 CONC  three of her six children are dead. Grandma Baker today at 98 is very active for her years and is anxiously waiting til the day when she can say that she has lived a century.
