1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This morning, after reaching the ripe age of 71 years, Gideon Light, residing at 825 Maple Street, Independent District, passed away peacefully over to that undiscovered country, after a long illness and well spent
2 CONC  life. The deceased came from a worthy and honored ancestry, (his parents being Mr. and Mrs. Jacob Light), that back in the last century settled in this section, and assisted so materially in making the lovely Lebanon valley bloom
2 CONC  in all its splendor. He grew to manhood on his father's farm, and many years ago moved to the home in which he died, where he raised a large family of children.  He was a devoted Christian gentleman and trustee and a zealous and 
2 CONC devoted worker of Salem United Brethren church, and in and out of season assisted in advancing the interests of his church, so that in Lebanon County there were few members of the faith who did not know him. He was of an amiable a
2 CONC nd retiring disposition and never turned a deaf ear to worthy charity, his heart ever going out to those in distress or needed his aid, and he has gone down to his grave honored and respected by all who knew his noble qualities of
2 CONC  heart and mind.  In the fall of 1872, he was nominated by the Democrats for the office of county recorder, but was unsuccessful, and in the year following, 1873, he was nominated by a fusion of Republicans and Democrats for count
2 CONC y treasurer, and was elected, discharging the duties in a highly successful manner. He was one of the organizers of the U. B. mutual aid society and for many years filled the treasureship of the society. In the death of the deceas
2 CONC ed the community has lost an honored citizen the church an exemplary member and the family a loving husband and affectionate father. The following children survive: B. W. Light Miss Emma L. Light, Jacob H. Light, Mrs. Geo. B. Trei
2 CONC st, S. P. Light, Esq., Mrs. Clinton. Boltz, Gideon Light, Miss Cora B. Light, and his wife also survives. [Lebanon Daily News, April 18, 1902, Page 1]
