1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Henry Gregg was 14 years old when he left Ireland and settled in Delaware. He was married to Elizabeth Cooke at Concord Friends Meeting. John was only 19 years old when his father died. His family continued to
2 CONC  live in the family home and keep possesion of  their land. John was a serious, proud and capable youth. He was always a faithful Friend and a busy person. He built a house and a gristmill, married and fathered seven children. His
2 CONC  remains are in The Friends Burial Ground, Center, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John came to Delaware colony with his parents, sister Anne and brothers George and Richard when he was 14.  Under the influence of constant Quaker discipline, he became a serious, proud and capable young man who, at age 19, took o
2 CONC n the responsibility of his home when his father died.  John was the administrator of his father's estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his brother George used the name Gregg or Grigg interchangeably in their business dealings.  They seated 400 acres of land on the Brandywine as old rent on 12/13/1691-2.  It was an entailed ground of James Logan to William 
2 CONC Gregg which would never run out, but a small lien inherited in perpetuity in a property returning an income to the proprietary family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed of gift to his brother George in 1719, John passed on some land that Richard had been given previously.  Based on the laws at that time, it is probable that his brother Richard had died and left no surviving heirs.  Greg
2 CONC gs were inherently land owners.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5/20/1685 - a warrant granted his father William for 200 acres was confirmed by patent to John Gregg on 2/18/1693 and later sold to Samuel Underwood, Sr. whose executor re-sold part of it back to John Gregg who sold it to Jonathan
2 CONC  Strange on 2/18/1733.  John Gregg paid the taxes for his father's estate 1693-1696.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1694, he built a 2 story stone house which sloped against the rocks on this land in Rocky Manor.  He was 26 at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8/17/1702 - John bought the first land sold out of "Letitia's Manor," 3 miles on the south side of the Brandywine; on 200 acres of it, he erected a great mill and then conveyed it to his son William on 4/10/1730.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9/8/1703 - John took up 300 acres in Chester County at Kennett, PA and lived on it for some years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 12/18/1717 - he patented 150 acres on the Brandywine for £22 10 s.  That same year he desired to purchase 2 parcels of land very uneven and rocky joyning the tract where he now dwells containing 100 acres each parcel.  It was form
2 CONC erly laid out to Richard Gregg who afterwards threw it up having never seated or improved on it.  It was agreed that John could have this land for £30 and one bushel of wheat quit rent on each 100 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his lifetime, John acquired about 5760 acres of fine land along the Brandywine reaching to Wilmington and extending over 3 miles west.  Deeds show all Gregg land belonged to the manor of Steining northwest of Wilmington spr
2 CONC eading over the state into Kennett and New Garden Twps.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a deed of gift (Deed Book X-I, p. 427) dated 12/2/1719, John passed to his brother George all title interest or demand whatsoever ... in a 150 acre tract of land formerly owned by their brother Richard.  This tract included the
2 CONC ir family home of Strand Millas.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a member of the building committee of the Center Friends Meeting House which was built near him in 1724.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an advisor and guardian of his sister's husband, William Dixson (Dickerson).  He was co-executor of his sister's 2nd husband's will (John Houghton).
2 CONT
2 CONT Later during the Revolution, the DuPonts bought some of the lands surrounding those of the Greggs.  They began making gunpowder.  Since the homes of the Greggs shook and cracked from the explosions, they eventually sold out to the
2 CONC  DuPonts!
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Gregg, of Brandywine[1, 2, 3] Male 1668 - 1738
2 CONT
