2 CONT * Parents:	Johannes Laurenson, Mary Pearcy?
2 CONT * Wives:	Abigail Pashuns, Jemima Woodhull (widow Halsey), Mary Herrick (widow Howell) 
2 CONT *AKA Larason, Lawrason, Laurenson 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=77480687 Find A Grave Memorial# 77480687]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr Cornelius Wilson Larison (1837-1910), Physician-Natural Scientist-Educator-Author, was the 4th great grandson of John Larason (1649-1693) (formerly Johannes Laurenson), who was born in Denmark and fled with his family to Scotla
2 CONC nd and later to Ireland before emigrating to Middle Village, L.I., NY in 1683.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/67_100/94jan1996/94vanschoonhoven.html Excerpts from the Journal of Emma Larison Colwell 1857 - 1950] Transcribed and annotated by Derick Van Schoonhoven.  History from George H. Lar
2 CONC ison, Lambertville, N. J.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Larisons are of Danish descent. Their ancestor John Larison, in the war between the King and Nobles in 1665, had his property confiscated, and, leaving the country, went to the seashore disguised as a peasant, where he escaped
2 CONC  to Scotland and soon afterwards came to America. Landing on Long Island, he purchased a large tract of land upon which he settled.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had six sons, two killed by the Indians. Four survived whose name[s] were Roger, James, William and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://books.google.com/books?id=uQR_vjf2_EEC&pg=PA225&lpg=PA225&dq=old+John+the+Dane+Larison&source=bl&ots=e49zYhK3PW&sig=oauIYDrB16AKjvP7raa1kpZR9lk&hl=en&sa=X&ei=DutpUt-OI6nXygHcioH4DQ&ved=0CCsQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=old%20Jo
2 CONC hn%20the%20Dane%20Larison&f=false Pioneers of Old Hopewell:] With Sketches of Her Revolutionary Heroes (Google eBook) by Ralph Ege.  Race & Savidge, 1908 - Hopewell (N.J.) - 289 pages.  Page 225.  
2 CONT
2 CONT NUMBER XXXIX.  John Larison (or Jon Larsen) according to the fireside traditions of the older Larisons was a Danish nobleman who was compelled to flee from Denmark and lose his estates by confiscation in 1660, because of taxes. He
2 CONC  fled first to Scotland and hearing that a large reward had been offered for his capture, he went over to Ireland for a time and finally emigrated to America and purchased a large tract, about 1700 acres, near Brooklyn, Long Islan
2 CONC d. His name is found on the rate list of Newtown, Long Island, in 1683. When John Larison came to America he left grown sons in Ireland, who tradition says married and settled there and who have the same tradition respecting their
2 CONC  ancestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 22, 1683, John Larison, (probably a son of John 1st) and Jemima Halsey were married at Newtown and on December 20, 1686, a John Larison also of Newtown married Mary Howell, a widow. She was probably the second wife of John 
2 CONC Larison, Senior, the Dane. The evidence in support of this theory is that the first husband of Mary Howell was doubtless a brother of Margaret Howell, who married Rev. John Moore. This was the only family of Howells at Newtown and
2 CONC  this marriage would make the Larisons a family connection of Nathaniel Moore, the first of the name in Hopewell, and connect the Larisons with a large circle of the English families who first settled in this region.
2 CONT
2 CONT This fact will account for Hopewell as the choice of location for William Larison, who came at the time of the great migration of Newtown families. Tradition says that of the six sons of John Larison the Dane, two were killed by t
2 CONC he Indians, Roger (the same as George) went to Pennsylvania, William to Hopewell and the others settled near Chester in Morris County, and that John the Dane also spent the closing years of his life there and that after a very eve
2 CONC ntful career his body found its last resting place at Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that John the Dane had a very superior education and that he gave his children the best educational advantages available at that time, and that in addition to being engaged in agriculture they were a race of school teac
2 CONC hers and business men as well. This made its impress on the generations following, among whom have been shrewd business men and educators and leaders in their respective communities.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Mary and Johannes LAURENSON is: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2. i. John 'Jan' LARASON was born ABT. 1647 in Southampton, Long Island, New York, and died 30 Nov 1693 in Southampton, Long Island, New York. He married Abigail ABT. 1668 in Newton, Long Island, New York. She was born ABT. 1650
2 CONC  in Newton, Long Island, New York, and died BEF.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1683 Rate List Newtown, Long Island, NY
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
