1 NOTE {geni:about_me} iv. Anne Larison b. 11 Feb 1743 Hunterdon, New Jersey; d. 1835; m1. 28 Mar 1768 to Judge Jared Sexton (b. 1737 Long Island, New York – d. 28 May 1785 – Hunterdon, NJ) who resided on an adjoining farm. Jared’s paren
2 CONC ts were Charles Sexton (1680 – 1752) and Sarah Jamison/Whitman (1688 – 1751). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna and Jared had seven children: (1) John, (2) Sarah, (3) Achsah, (4) Margaret, (5) William, (6) Elijah, and (7) Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT m2. 1788 to her cousin Benjamin Parke (b. 8 Jan 1743 in Hopewell, Hunterdon, New Jersey; d. 1778 in Hunterdon) His parents were William Parke and Sarah Jewell (See above) He first married Lucy [__?__] (b. 20 May 1736)
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Anne lived to the age of 92, and retained her faculties of mind and memory to a remarkable degree. She was eminently social, an entertaining conversationalist and as she was on terms of intimacy with the wives of many of the lea
2 CONC ding men of the revolutionary period she was considered authority on reminiscences of the war for fifty years after its close."
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://minerdescent.com/2010/05/19/roger-parkes-jr/ Dr. Cornelius W. Larison, in The Larisun Famili ... [sic] (1888), p. 384, states that "The bodies of all these individuals [i.e., Jared Sexton, his wife Anne Larison, and her s
2 CONC econd husband, Benjamin Parke] were buried in the ancient Parke-Larison Cemetery which was near the center of James Larison's homestead." Ralph Ege, in Pioneers of Old Hopewell (1908), p. 239, states that "Judge [Jared] Sexton ...
2 CONC  is buried in the family plot on the farm." It is not clear whether "the family plot" was the Parke-Larison burial ground, or possibly the nearby Sexton family burial ground; see GMNJ 27:14 (Jan. 1952). Note: See GMNJ 71:16 (1996)
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=145292646 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 7 2016, 2:50:00 UTC''
