1 NOTE {geni:about_me}      Source: Pioneers of Old Hopewell with Sketches of Her Revolutionary Heros by Ralph Ege
2 CONT     Published by Race & Savidge, 1908. Page 235
2 CONT
2 CONT     David, youngest child of James Larison, born March 8, 1757 , married September 15, 1780,
2 CONT     Jerusha, daughter of Ethan Smith, and sister of Temperance who married Hart Olden, and
2 CONT     became the mother of Charles Smith Olden, afterward governor of New Jersey. She also had
2 CONT     a brother Dr. Charles Smith, a wealthy and prominent citizen of New Brunswick. David
2 CONT     Larison settled on the farm adjoining the James Larison tract on the north, now the
2 CONT     property of Mr. E. S. Titus of the Borough. They had four children, viz: Jonathan, born
2 CONT     September 1, 1781; Amos, born May 7, 1784, resided in Ohio and had two daughters, died
2 CONT     in 1839; Charles, born July 24, 1793, resided in Cass Count y, Indiana, had twelve children
2 CONT     and died in 1841; Enoch, born November 2, 1796, removed to Cincinnati, Ohio, when it was
2 CONT     a small town, grew up with the place, and also had a family.
2 CONT
2 CONT     David Larison died November 29, 1800, aged 43, and his widow married second, Nathan Drake and removed to Lexington, Ky ., where he died, and his widow married third, David Stout , son of Jonathan Stout and Mary Leigh, of Hopew
2 CONC ell.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The will of David Larison, dated November 19, 1800, bequeaths the homestead farm to his widow and four sons, directing that his son Jonathan work the farm, and the children, all of whom are underage, be kept together. One item
2 CONC  reads a s follows: "At the expiration of my wife's widowhood the fa rm is to be sold, and the proceeds divided between my four sons, Jonathan, Amos, Charles and Enoch." He appoints his wife Jerusha, and his neighbor John Sexton, 
2 CONC executors.
