1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT ===Willem van Tilburgh
2 CONT *Birth:	Dec 11 1720 - Roxbury, Morris, NJ, USA
2 CONT *Death:	Dec 15 1804 - Kingston, Somerset Co., NJ
2 CONT *Parents:	Pieter Johannsen Van Tilburgh, Metje Van Tilburgh (born Bouwman)
2 CONT *Siblings:	Johannes Or John Van Tilburgh, Jorius Van Tilburgh, Hendrickus Van Tilburgh, Mary Van Tilburgh, Johanna Van Tilburgh
2 CONT *Wife:	Kesiah Van Tilburgh (born Larrison)
2 CONT *Wife:	Mrs. Rebecca Van Tilburgh
2 CONT *Children:	Henry Van Tilburg, William Jr. Van Tilburgh, Mary Berrien (born Van Tilburgh), Peter Van Tilburgh, Martha Van Tilburgh, John Van Tilburgh, Charlotte Van Tilburgh
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT William (Willem) was born 1720  in Port Richmond, Staten Island, New York. His father was Pieter Johannsen Van Tilburg and his mother was Metie Bouwman. He was Christened 11 Dec 1720 in the Dutch Reformed Church in Port Richmond, 
2 CONC New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem married Keziah Larison on Dec 20, 1745 in Somerset County, New Jersey. Her parents were William Larisaon and Anna Pashuns. After their marriage, they resided in Kingston, Somerset County, New Jersey. William’s occupation wa
2 CONC s a tavern owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were 
2 CONT *Johannes Van Tilburgh		1751 - 1826
2 CONT *Henry Van Tilburgh		1756 - 1842
2 CONT
2 CONT Early 18th century activity in Kingston centered around the numerous stage coaches that traveled by way of Kingston between the two largest cities during colonial times - Philadelphia and New York. Taverns provided a place for foo
2 CONC d and drink, for finding out the news, changing horses, making an overnight stop or shelter in poor weather. Van Tilburgh's inn, called "The Sign of the Mermaid," is perhaps the most remembered from this era.  Willem’s family belo
2 CONC nged to the Kingston Presbyterian Church, which was organized in 1723 and was initially located in a log building adjacent to the Millstone River. The old tombstones surround the site where the church once stood in the Kingston Ce
2 CONC metery. The Reverend Eleazer Wales was the first recorded minister of the church. The original church building in the cemetery burned down in 1791, and new building was erected in 1792 on the original foundation. The congregation 
2 CONC used this building until 1852, when the larger building on Main Street in Kingston was constructed on land purchased from Elijah Stout. In 1853, the church building in the cemetery was sold and was later dismantled.
2 CONT
2 CONT The church suffered through some difficult times during the later 1700s. During this period, Princeton University was built and the village of Princeton expanded, while the population of Kingston declined. Consequently, the church
2 CONC  lost many members to the "new" Presbyterian Church down the road in Princeton.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was also during this time that the Revolutionary War was fought in the area, and British officers were quartered in local homes. General George Washington's victory at the Battle of Princeton on January 3, 1777 turned the tide 
2 CONC of the Revolution. Washington led his weary troops along the King's Highway to Kingston where Washington held his famous "conference on horseback" with his officers to decide whether to attempt capturing British supplies in New Br
2 CONC unswick or to camp in Morristown for the winter. His troops were exhausted and freezing; Washington and his officers chose to march past Rocky Hill and Griggstown along the Millstone River and on to Morristown. Washington's troops
2 CONC  marched through Kingston again in June 1778 en route to the Battle of Monmouth. Later, after the Battle of Yorktown in October 1781, Colonel Dillon Lauzun's Legion camped in Kingston. After the war, the Continental Congress met i
2 CONC n Princeton during 1783 and George Washington arrived in Princeton in August and stayed at nearby Rockingham. During these months, many of America's most influential people visited this area as participants in the Congress or as g
2 CONC uests of General Washington. Gen. Washington also had his headquarters hardly a mile from the church while the Continental Congress sat in Nassau Hall. It is likely that Gen Washington or his troops visited the Sign of the Mermaid
2 CONC  Tavern owned by William Van Tilburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Record show that William married Rebecca about 1760 (b, 1739). There is no record to indicate Willem and Kesiah divorced, but records evidenced Kesiah died in 1791. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Rebecca’s five children were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # William Van Tilburgh 		1760 – 1843
2 CONT # Mary Ann Van Tilburgh	1763 – 1854
2 CONT # Peter Van Tilburgh		1764 – 1855
2 CONT # Martha Van Tilburgh		1766 – 1829
2 CONT # Charlotte Van Tilburgh		1770 - 1844
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem died 15 December 1804 Kingston, Somerset, New Jersey and is buried Kingston Presbyterian Church Cemetery, Kingston, Somerset, New Jersey, His monument below reads: In memory of William Vantilburgh who departed this life on 
2 CONC Dec 15, 1804 at age 84. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca Van Tilburgh died 12 Nov 1822 and was buried Kingston, Somerset Co., New Jersey
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * "New Jersey, Marriages, 1670-1980," database with images, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:Q283-QTBP : 29 November 2018), William Vantilbury and Keziah Lawrason, 20 Dec 1745; citing Marriage, New Jersey, Uni
2 CONC ted States, Division of Archives and Record Management, New Jersey Department of State, Trenton; FHL microfilm 007719783.
2 CONT * [https://www.kingstongreenways.org/history.html Kingston: Crossroads To History 1675 -] Kingston is a place like no other in the state of New Jersey. Although the community remains unincorporated today, residents share a common 
2 CONC heritage that goes back to the 17th century. What makes Kingston so unique, however, is that it is the only tri-county community in New Jersey. The former King's Highway and the Millstone River, which historically brought people t
2 CONC ogether to live here, are now the boundaries that politically divide this historic village. ... Early 18th century activity in Kingston centered around the numerous stage coaches that traveled by way of Kingston between the two la
2 CONC rgest cities during colonial times - Philadelphia and New York. Taverns provided a place for food and drink, for finding out the news, changing horses, making an overnight stop or shelter in poor weather. '''Van Tilburgh's inn, ca
2 CONC lled "The Sign of the Mermaid," is perhaps the most remembered from this era.'''
2 CONT * [http://www.towntopics.com/wordpress/2016/11/09/main-street-cafe-and-bakery-to-closewill-reopen-as-pjs-pancake-house/ Princeton Town Topics] ... According to George Luck, vice-president of the Kingston Historical Society, the Ma
2 CONC in Street building dates from the 1880s, when it replaced an earlier structure on the site, a tavern called The Sign of the Mermaid. “William Van Tilburg ran it during the Revolutionary War time, from 1750 till he passed away in 1
2 CONC 802,” Mr. Luck said. “It was on the same foundation of the current building. It was a tavern and an inn, with rooms. It was always kind of a stopping place. George Washington had some of his life guard put up there.”
