2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Henry Vantilburg, the son of Willem and Kesiah Van Tilburgh, was born on February 16, 1756 in Middlesex County, New Jersey. He married Jane Holman on July 14, 1773 in Kingston, Somerset, New Jersey. Jane was born in 1747, to Mr an
2 CONC d Mrs Holman.  
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Revolutionary War, Henry enlisted in Washington's army in New Jersey and was in the Battle of Trenton and Monmouth. He served in the artillery. His wife, Jane Holman, was shot by a Hessian soldier while standing in the 
2 CONC front door of her home with a child in her arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Jane Holman had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William H Vantilburg		1772 - 1862 Samuel Vantilburg		1773 - 1863 Polly Vantilburg		1776 - Deceased
2 CONT
2 CONT Polly may had been the child in Jane’s arms and may have also died in the incident with her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after his wife’s death, Henry married Jane Sunderland, born 1760, and had nine children. About 1782, the family moved to Virginia/West Virgina. Then about 1800, they moved to the "Territory Northwest of the Ohio River" on a s
2 CONC oldier’s land claim for the reason of being paid in continental money which the government could not redeem. Three of his brothers served in the Continental army and all came with them to Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Henry and Jane Sunderland:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Vantilburg			1779 - 1863 Daniel Vantilburg			1780 – 1866 John Vantilburg			1784 – 1871 Catherine Vantilburg Spitler	1786 – 1843 Kesiah Vantilburg Stoaks		1791 – 1849 Peter Vantilburg			1795 – 1874 Francis Vantilburg			1799 –
2 CONC  1861 Nancy Vantilburg Cooper		1800 – 1886 Jane Vantilburg Stoaks		1802 – 1873
2 CONT
2 CONT   Jane died in 1840 at the age of 80 and Henry Vantilburg died on October 22, 1842 in Port Homer, Jefferson County, Ohio at the age of 84 and is buried in the Sugar Grove Cemetery. Jane was probably buried in the same cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT REVOLUTIONARY WAR PENSION APPLICATION OF HENRY VANTILBURG
2 CONT The pension application of Henry Vantilburg is of interest because it names as fellow militiamen David Maple and William Maple. Henry Vantilburg was born Feb. 16, 1746, the son of William and Kesiah (Larrison) Vantilburg, and neph
2 CONC ew of George Larrison, father of Kesiah (Larrison) Maple, the first wife of William Maple Sr. Kesiah (Larrison) Maple was thus Henry Vantilburg's first cousin. William Maple and Henry Vantilburg were lifelong friends, and it is pr
2 CONC obably Henry who introduced William Maple to his future wife. Both settled near Port Homer in Jefferson Co., OH in 1797. (see Dr. Robert W. Schilling. Tales of Yellow Creek. Gold Seal Publications. Poland, Ohio. 1942. Page 94). At
2 CONC  the time of his application, Henry Vantilburg was in his 90th year. (the birth year given in the interrogatory part is an error of the clerk). His application was rejected by an unsympathetic young officer who made no allowance f
2 CONC or the inability of a person of such advanced age to recall all the details of service which had occurred sixty years before. William Maple had died in 1812, and Henry apparently had no living acquaintance who could testify to his
2 CONC  service.
2 CONT Text of the application was copied from a photostat of the Revolutionary War pension file of Henry Vantilburg, R 10 894. National Archives, Washington, D.C.
2 CONT State of Ohio Jefferson County SS.
2 CONT On the 16th day of October AD 1835 personally appeared before the Supreme Court of said County Henry Vantilburg a resident of said County, aged 90 years the 16th of February 1836, ho being first duly sworn according to law, doth o
2 CONC n his oath make the following declar­ation in order to obtain the benefit of the Act of Congress passed June 7th 1832. That he entered the service of the United States under the following named officers, & served as herein stated:
2 CONC  Sometime in the years of either 1775 or 1776 (he does not recollect particularly which,) he was drafted to serve in the Militia. This was in Princeton in the State of New Jersey, under the following officers, to wit, Captain Aaro
2 CONC n Longstreet, Lieutenant Samuel Meshon, Colonel of the Regiment William Scudder. They were drafted to go to Long Island in the State of New York, to disarm the tories, it was in the fall of the year when they were drafted, about f
2 CONC our days after they were drafted, the companies which were drafted to go to Long Island, of which there were several, met at Brunswick & rendesvoused, from Brunswick they marched to Woodbridge New Jersey, which was the residence o
2 CONC f Haird the General of their Brigade, at this place Haird took command of the Brigade & marched it to New York City, at New York they did not dare to cross over to the island on account of the Asia, a man of war, 75 guns laying br
2 CONC oadside to the city, she belonged to the British, the next morning they marched five miles up the river & crossed over to the island, & marched to Hampstead, at Hampsted they remained seven or eight days. While at Hempdsted the ge
2 CONC neral would send detachments of men to disarm the tories, said detachments were commanded by Sergeants, from Hampsted they were marched about ten miles to Jericho, remained there two days from Jericho they were marched back to New
2 CONC  Jersey, to Woodbridge & there they were all discharged. But said discharges were verbal. In this service about three weeks were occupied. But they were drafted to serve for no specified time. The company of which Deponent was a m
2 CONC ember, was divided into four classes. They were all obliged to be in readiness to serve whenever they should be called upon, the English were upon Staten Island opposite to Amboy, this ws in the following Spring, at which time thi
2 CONC s Deponent was drafted, & they were drafted this, the second time, to guard the shore, opposite to the English; on this second tour they were engaged a month, at ewhich their class was relieved, by another class of said company; o
2 CONC ne year after the second tour, and deponent thinks in the year 1776, the English marched from Brunswick to Somerset & burnt the court house Deponent was then called out again at this time they were out about two weeks; this was in
2 CONC  the latter part of the summer; The British continually made inroads, & Deponent with others was called out at least a dozen different times, sometimes a week or two & sometimes but two or three days.
2 CONT In the third tour, the Deponent was in an action, between the Americans & English, on Bargain Point in New Jersey, under the command of General Haird,-- He says that he served altogether six months H never received or was offered 
2 CONC a written discharge, he has no documentary evidence or knows of no person living who can testify to his services. The following are the names of some of the persons who were drafted at the same time deponent was, Henry Emmons, Wil
2 CONC liam Maple, David Maple Tunis Middleswarth; he knows of no clergyman in his neighborhood, who can testify that he is reputed & believed in his neighborhood to be a revolutionary Soldier, but names the following persons who are his
2 CONC  neighbors who will testify that he is reputed & believd in his neighborhood to have been a revolutionary soldier. to wit David Sloane, Alexcander Cooper, Samuel Watts.
2 CONT He hereby relinquishes every claim whatever to a pension or annuity except the present, & declares that his name is not on the Pension Roll of the Agency of any State. The following is the reason why he never made application soon
2 CONC er for a pension, to wit he thought that it was impossible to procure a pension without actual proof of service & there has been no person living (as far as he knows) who could testify to his services, they having all died previou
2 CONC s to the passage of the law of 1832.
2 CONT Sworn & subscribed before said
2 CONT Court the day & year aforesaid.
2 CONT S/ Henry Vantilburg
2 CONT S/ James Ross Wells Clk
2 CONT We the undersigned neighbors of the aforesaid applicant hereby certify that we are well acquainted with said applicant, who has sworn & subscribed to the above declaration; that we believe to be nearly ninety years of age;that he 
2 CONC is reputed & believed, in the neighborhood where he resides to be a soldier of the revolution & we concur in that opinion
2 CONT S/ D Sloane
2 CONT S/ Alex Cooper
2 CONT S/ Saml. Watt
2 CONT Sworn & subscribed the day & year aforesaid.
2 CONT S/ James Ross Wells
2 CONT Clerk
2 CONT Interrogatories propounded by said Court to said applicant & his answers thereto.
2 CONT 1. Where & in what year were you born. Ans. I was born in Middlesex Co, New Jersey AD 1756.
2 CONT 2. Have you any record of your age. Ans. I have. It is at my residence.
2 CONT 3. Where were you living when called into the service,
2 CONT where have you lived since the revolutionary war & where do you now live--Ans I lived when called into the service near Princeton N. J. Since ths time seven years in the State of Va. & for the last thirty eight years in Jefferson 
2 CONC Co. Ohio.
2 CONT 4. How were you called into the service Ans. I was drafted.
2 CONT 5. State the names of some of the Regular Officers who were with the troops, where you served; such continental & Militia Regiments as you can recolect & the general circumstances of your service. Ans. Captain Aaron Longstreet, Li
2 CONC eut Samuel Meshon, Col Wm Scudder, General Hairds. I served as a militia-man in New Jersey.
2 CONT 6. Did you ever receive a discharge. Ans I never did.
2 CONT 7. State the names of persons to whom you are known in your present neighborhood who can testify as to your character for truth & veracity & their belief of you service as a soldier of the Revolution. Ans. David Sloane, Alexander 
2 CONC Cooper, Samuel Watts.
2 CONT And the said Court do hereby declare their opinion, after the invest­igation of the matter & after putting the interrogatories prescribed by the War department that the above named applicant was a revolutionary Soldier & served as
2 CONC  he states. And the Court further certifies that it appears to them that David Sloane, Alexander Cooper & Samuel Watts, are residents in the township & neighborhood of said applicant & that their statement is entitled to credit.
2 CONT I, James Ross Wells Clerk of said
2 CONT Supreme Court of Jefferson
2 CONT County Ohio, do hereby certify
2 CONT (SEAL WITH RIBBONS) that the foregoing contains the
2 CONT original proceedings of said Court
2 CONT in the matter of the application
2 CONT of Henry Vantiburg for a pension. In testimony
2 CONT whereof I have hereunto set my hand & seal of
2 CONT office this 19th day of October 1835.
2 CONT S/ James Ross Wells
2 CONT Clerk
2 CONT Source: Telford Grant Maple, "Genealogical History of the Maple/ Maple Family in America", Penobscot Press, 1993.:
