1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Richard E. Stout ''' Emigrated in 1644 from Nottinghamshire, England to New Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Timeline =
2 CONT
2 CONT *"Ran away from home and joined or was pressed into the British Navy where he served for seven years. At New Amsterdam left his ship and through bearing arms, became a Netherlands subject (and eligible for the Huguenot Society.) 
2 CONT *1643 Owned plantation #18 at Gravesend. 
2 CONT *1664 With eleven others patented a large section of New (East) Jersey by Gov. Nichols in the vicinity of Monmouth. 
2 CONT *1667 Held lot #6 and upland country at Middletown. 
2 CONT *1669 Overseer. Illiterate. 
2 CONT *1675 Deeded 1800 acres to his heirs. 
2 CONT *1677 Received 745 acres by patent. 
2 CONT *Will proved 23 October 1705 at Perth Amboy, New Jersey. Lib. 1, p 120, N. J. wills."
2 CONT
2 CONT Had 9 recorded children:
2 CONT *1. John 1645-1724
2 CONT *2. Richard 1646-1717
2 CONT *3. James 1648
2 CONT *4. Mary 1650
2 CONT *5. Peter 1654-1703 *
2 CONT *6. Sarah 1656
2 CONT *7. Johnathan 1660-1722 (Jonathan)
2 CONT *8. David 1667
2 CONT *9 Benjamin 1669-1734
2 CONT
2 CONT Above information from (p. 1) Stout and Allied Families: Volume One, 1951, Harold F. Stout, The Eagle Press, Dover, Ohio
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice 1650 included in list of 10 children : http://www.solavirtusnobilitat.com/pedigree/individual.php?pid=I2621&ged=19%20jan%2008.ged
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Below from http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~brookefamily/stoutrichard.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Another story coming down through the family is that the original Richard Stout was born in England about 1615 and after a disagreement with his father, John, about the choice of a wife he signed on a sailing ship for seven years.
2 CONC  After serving his term he journeyed to the new world. There he met and married the incredible Penelope Van Princin. Her story of survival is recorded in New Jersey history books and has been documented as true. --- 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Will of Richard Stout
2 CONT
2 CONT Know all men by these presents that I, Richard Stout of Middletown, in the county of Monmouth, in East Jersey, being of Sound Mind and disposing memory, do make and ordain this to be my last will and testament which is as followet
2 CONC h:
2 CONT
2 CONT I will that all my just debts be paid; I give and devise unto my loving wife, during her natural life, all my orchard and that part or room of the house she now lives in, with the cellar, and all the land I now possess. I give and
2 CONC  bequeath unto my loving wife, all my horse kind, excepting one mare and colt. My son Benjamin is to have for keeping my cattle last year.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my sons John, Richard, James, Johnathan, David and Benjamin, one shilling each of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give unto my daughters, Mary, Alice and Sarah, each of them one shilling.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give to my daughter-in-law Mary Stout and her son John one shilling each of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give and bequeath unto my kinswoman Mary Stout, the daughter of formerly Peter Stout, one cow, to be paid within six days after my wife's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the remainder of my personal estate whatsoever, I give and bequeath unto my loving wife, and to this, my last will and testament, I make my son John and my son Johnathan my executors to.
2 CONT
2 CONT For this my will performed, in witness hereof I have hereunto put my hand and seal, June the ninth day, in the year one thousand seven hundred and three.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard X Stout
2 CONT
2 CONT Mark
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, sealed and published in the presence of us: 
2 CONT *Richard Hartshorne
2 CONT *John Weekham
2 CONT *Peter Vandervere
2 CONT
2 CONT Proved before Lord Cornbury, Governor, Captain General, & E tc. 23 October 1705 at Perth Amboy.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Recorded in LiberI, p 120, of Wills at Trenton, N.J.)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/htm/stout.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of the Stout Family: First Settling in Middletown, Monmouth County, New Jersey 
2 CONT
2 CONT By Nathan Stout, 1823. Second printing by H.G. McCarter, "Herald" Office, Hopewell, N.J., 1878  with additions by Mrs. Sarah Weart, Third printing in 1906 by Joab B. Stout, with additions and corrections. Fourth printing in 1929 b
2 CONC y George A. Chandler, Bethlehem, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of the Stout Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout, the first of the name in America was born in Notinghamshire, in Old England, and his father's name was John.  The said Richard, when quite young paid his addresses to a young woman that his father though below his r
2 CONC ank, upon which account some unpleasant conversation happened between the father and the son, on account of which, the said Richard left his father's house; and in a few days engaged on board a ship of war, where he served about s
2 CONC even years, after which time he got a discharge at New Amsterdam, now called New York, in America.  
2 CONT
2 CONT About the same time a ship from Amsterdam, in Holland, on her way to the said New Amsterdam, was driven on the shore that is now called Middletown, in Monmouth County, in the State of new Jersey, which ship was loaded with passeng
2 CONC ers, who with much difficulty got on shore.  But the Indians not long after fell upon them and butchered and killed the whole crew, as they thought, but soon after the Indians were gone, a certain Penelope Van Princes, whose husba
2 CONC nd the Indians had killed, found herself possessed of strength enough to creep to a hollow tree, where she remained some days.  
2 CONT
2 CONT An Indian happening to come that way, whose dog coming to the tree, occasioned him to examine the inside of the tree, where he found the said Penelope in a forlorn, distressed condition.  She was bruised very severely about the he
2 CONC ad, and her bowels protruded from a cut across her abdomen; she kept them in with her hand.  She had been in this fearful condition seven days when the Indian found her.  In his compassion he took her out of the tree and carried h
2 CONC er to his wigwam where he treated her kindly and healed her wounds, and in a short time conveyed her in his canoe to New Amsterdam, where he sold her to the Dutch, who then owned that city, now called New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT The man and woman, Richard & Penelope Van Princes,  from whom the whole race of Stouts descended, got into the city of New Amsterdam, where they became acquainted with each other and were married.  And, not withstanding, it may be
2 CONC  thought by some, that they conducted themselves with more fortitude than prudence, they immediately crossed the bay and settled in the above said Middletown, where the said Penelope had lost her first husband by the Indians and h
2 CONC ad been so severely wounded herself. There was at that time but six white families in the settlement, including their own, (which was in the year 1648), where they continued until they became rich in prosperity and rich in childre
2 CONC n.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They had together seven sons and three daughters, viz: John, Richard, Jonathan, Peter, James, Benjamin, David.  The daughters were - Deliverance, Sarah, Penelope.  All of which sons and daughters lived to raise large families.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout, one of the first settlers of Gravesend, New Netherlands in 1643, was born in 1615, died in 1705, the son of John Stout and his wife Elisabeth Bee, whom he married on November 13, 1609, the marriage recorded in the p
2 CONC arish register of Burton Joyce Parish, Nottinghamshire, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout married Penelope Kent (or Lent) Van Princen, who was born 1622 and died in 1732. Richard Stout ran away from home and joined, or was pressed into, the British Navy where he served seven years, left his ship at New Am
2 CONC sterdam and became a subject of the New Netherlands. He was allotted plantation-lot No. 18, in 1646, in Gravesend, according to town records, having resided there since about 1643. He also bought on April 5, 1661, plantation lot N
2 CONC o. 26, from Edward Griffen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With a number of his neighbors he left Gravesend and settled at Middletown, Monmouth County, New Jersey, where he was one of the patentees and original purchasers from the Indians. In 1664, he with 11 others patented a large secti
2 CONC on of East Jersey, the patents granted by Governor Nicholas in the vicinity of Monmouth. In 1667, he held lot No. 6, and upland country at Middletown. In 1669, he was an overseer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1675, he deeded 1,800 acres to his heirs. In 1677, he received 745 acres by patent. Richard Stout was a member of the first General Assembly composed of deputies and patentees and convened at Portland Poyot, New Jersey, in 1671
2 CONC , and he was also an Indian commissioner. Because of these offices held by Richard Stout, descent from him is a qualification for the Society of the Colonial Dames of New Jersey. Descent from Richard Stout is also a qualification 
2 CONC for membership in the Huguenot Society. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Richard Stout, Sr., dated 9 June 1703, in the office of the Secretary of State at Trenton, New Jersey, described him as of Middletown (Monmouth County), New Jersey. No wife is mentioned. Mentioned are his sons John, Ri
2 CONC chard, James, Jonathan, David, Benjamin; daughters Mary, Alicia, Sarah; daughter-in-law Mare Stout and her son John; and kinswoman Mary Stout, daughter of Peter Stout. He left real and personal property. Executors were his sons Jo
2 CONC hn and Jonathan. Witnesses were Richard Hartshorne, John Weekham and Peter Vandevandeter. It was proved 23 October 1705, records Lib. 1, p. 120, Monmouth Wills. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An inventory of personal estate was made 6 October 1705, by Obadiah Bowne and James Hubbard, and totaled 64 pounds, comprising mostly livestock.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~bdollar/p42.htm  :
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout was born in England about 1615 and after a disagreement with his father, John, about the choice of a wife he signed on a sailing ship for seven years. After serving his term he journeyed to the New World. There he me
2 CONC t and married the incredible Penelope Van Princin. Her story of survival is recorded in New Jersey history books and has been documented as true. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout, first of the name in America, was born in Nottinghamshire, England, son of John Stout, a gentleman of good position. Richard Stout paid his addresses to a young woman whom his father disapproved, whereupon he left h
2 CONC ome, enlisted in the British navy, served seven years on a man-of-war, receiving an honorable discharge in New Amsterdam, New York, where he remained until 1668. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He became one of the original proprietors of Middletown, Monmouth county, New Jersey. He continued to reside in Middletown until his decease at an advanced age. Longevity is characteristic of the Stout family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, at New York, a widow whose maiden name was Penelope Van Princess, born at Amsterdam, Holland, in 1602, died at Middletown, New Jersey, 1712, aged 110 years (hard to believe!  I have left her death at 1712, but who know
2 CONC s, she is such a legend). She crossed the ocean with her first husband on a vessel that was wrecked upon the Jersey coast near Sandy Hook. All reached the shore in safety and proceeded by land to New York. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband was overcome by illness and was left behind with his wife. They were discovered by the Indians and her husband was killed. She was left for dead, but recovered consciousness and concealed herself in a hollow log, where
2 CONC  for several days she subsisted in part upon the excresences that grew upon it, until discovered by an old Indian to whose kindness and crude knowledge she was indebted for the recovery of her health and restoration to her friends
2 CONC  in New Amsterdam. (See Smith's History of New Jersey, published 1765, Appendix VI [i.e., Samuel Smith's The History of the Colony of Nova-Caesaria, or New Jersey]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In New Amsterdam she became acquainted and married John Stout. They settled in Middletown, New Jersey, where their children were born. At her decease in 1712, she was said to have had five hundred and two descendants. Richard and 
2 CONC Penelope (Van Princess) Stout had nine children:
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Stouts bore several coats of arms.  Richard was granted arms by the French Hugenots (Protestants).  The German Stouts were granted several coats of arms, some of them indicating they were members of the Crusades.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard ran away from home while still a young boy and was "Shanghied" into the English Navy, where he served for seven years.  He finally jumped ship and landed in Amsterdam, Holland, where he met and married Penelope Van Princin
2 CONC .  She was born Penelope Kent in England in 1622.  Her parents fled to Holland with the Pilgrims and there she met and married a Hollander named VanPrincin.  She was a widow when she met and married Richard Stout.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just what year Richard and Penelope came to America isn't clear.  In 1644, with eleven others, he patented a large section of New (East) Jersey by Gov. Nichols in the vicinity of Manmouth.  He could not read nor write.  In 1675, h
2 CONC e deeded 1800 acres to his heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed and scalped by Indians in 1705 and his wife, Penelope was nearly killed.  She was left for dead, but managed to seek refuge in a hollow tree.  She was eventually rescued and lived to be 110 years of age, leaving 502 
2 CONC decendants.  She died in 1732.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard's will "was proved" October 23, 1705, at Perty, Amboy, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Richard and Penelope Stout:  John, Richard, James, Mary, Peter, Sarah, Jonathan, David, Benjamin
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard died at age 90.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The STOUT FAMILY OF DELAWARE states that "Richard Stout was the most prominent of the founders of the new colony at Middletown." The records show that Richard and his sons owned 780 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born about 1615 in Nottinghamshire, England. He emigrated in 1622 from Long Island, New York.  Richard Stout of Nottingham, England, (son of John Stout), settled in Long Island about 1622, in which year he was one of 39 ori
2 CONC ginal patentees of Gravesend. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Testified on 13 Oct 1643. October 13, 1643, Richard Aestin, Ambrose Love and Richard Stout made declaration that the crew of the Seven Stars and of the Privateer landed at the farm of Anthoney Jansen of Sallee (New Utrecht)
2 CONC  in the bay and took off 200 pumpkins and would have carried off a lot of hogs from Coney Island had they not learned they belonged to the Lady Moody. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He resided in 1648 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Lawsuit on 8 Oct 1663 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York.  In the Town Court, of Gravesend, October 8, 1663, "It is said Richard Stout declareth that Nathaniel Brittain had slandered him; that he had sold wine to the India
2 CONC ns. The said Nathaniel denieth it, but said that the Indians told his wife that they had bought wine of Stout." The Court, however, ordered Nathaniel to pay costs of prosecution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned Purchased tract of land from Indian Chief Papomore on 25 Jan 1664 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York.  In 1664, January 25th, Richard Stout, John Bowne, John Tilton, Jr. and others bought a tract of land of Papomore, Chi
2 CONC ef of Indians, the deed of which is recorded at Albany, also other tracts from other Indians, April 7th, 1665 and June 5th, 1665. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Original Patentee of New Jersey in 1665 in Monmouth Co., NJ.  Richard was one of the twelve patentees of New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Member of General Assembly in 1671 in Portland Point, New Jersey.  Richard Stout, member of first General Assembly (composed of Deputies and Patentees), convened at Portland Point, New Jersey, 1671. Indian Commissioner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He signed a will on 4 Jun 1703 in Middletown, Royal Colony of New Jersey. 1703 June 9. Stout, Richard, senior, of Middletown; will of. Wife ______. Sons - John, Richard, James, Jonathan, David, Benjamin; daughters - Mary, Alse, Sa
2 CONC rah; daughter-in-law Marey Stoute and her son John, kinswoman Mary Stoute, daughter of Peter Stout. Real and personal property. Executors - sons John & Jonathan. Witnesses - Richard Hartshorne, John Weepham and Peter VanDevandeter
2 CONC  (?). Proved Oct 23, 1705. Lib. I, p. 120 & Monmouth wills. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died about 1705 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey. He had an estate probated on 6 Oct 1705 in Middletown, Royal Colony of New Jersey.    
2 CONT
2 CONT 1705 Oct 6. Inventory of personal estate (64.8/0 pounds, mostly hogs, cattle, horses and sheep); made by Obadiah Bowne and James Hubbard. He resided in Squan, New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also known as Squan Dick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned Land purchase in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey.   In the early days after his removal to Middletown, Richard became one of the purchasers of the Monmouth Tract of land embraced between the Raritan and Sandy Point. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Richard Stout, the first name in America was born in Notinghamshire, in Old England, and his father's name was John. The said Richard, when quite young paid his addresses to a young woman that his father thought below his rank, u
2 CONC pon which account some unpleasant conversation happened between the father and the son, on account of which, the said Richard left his father's house, and in a few days engaged aboard a ship of war, where he served about seven yea
2 CONC rs, after which time he got a discharge at New Amsterdam, now called New York, in America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About the same time a ship from Amsterdam, in Holland, on her way to the said New Amsterdam, was driven on the shore that is now called Middletown, in Monmouth County, in the State of New Jersey, which ship was loaded with passeng
2 CONC ers, who with much difficulty got on shore. But the Indians not long after fell upon them and butchered and killed the whole crew, as they thought, but soon after the Indians were gone, a certain Penelope Van Princes, whose husban
2 CONC d the Indians had killed, found herself possessed of strength enough to creep to a hollow tree, where she remained some days. An Indian happening to come that way, whose dog coming to the tree, occasioned him to examine the inside
2 CONC  of the tree, where he found the said Penelope in a forlorn, distressed condition. She was bruised very severely about the head, and her bowels protruded from a cut across her abdomen, she kept them in with her hand. She had been 
2 CONC in this fearful condition seven days when the Indian found her. In his compassion he took her out of the tree and carried her to his wigwam where he treated her kindly and healed her wounds, and in a short time conveyed her in his
2 CONC  canoe to New Amsterdam, where he sold her to the Dutch, who then owned that city, now called New York.   
2 CONT
2 CONT The man and woman from whom the whole race of Stouts descended, got into the city of New Amsterdam, where they became acquainted with each other and were married. And not withstanding, it may be thought by some, that they conducte
2 CONC d themselves with more fortitude than prudence, they immediately crossed the bay and settled in the above said Middletown, where the said Penelope had lost her husband by the Indians and had been so severely wounded herself.   The
2 CONC re was at that time but six families in the settlement, including their own (which was in the year 1648), where they continued until they became rich in prosperity and rich in children.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Richard appears to have come to New Amsterdam about 1640. He became a  subject of the Netherlands & remained in the area untill the British came. He & a few others went to New Jersey & purchased land that was to becme the settlem
2 CONC ent of Monmouth. Upon his death he had become the largest landed proprieter in the area. In 1668 he & his wife helped to organize the first Baptist church of New Jersey. Richard's will was proved in October of 1705. He was married
2 CONC  to Penelope VAN PRINCISS about 1644 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Stilwell"s "Genealogy of the Stout Family", vol . # 4
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigrant: Stout , Richard
2 CONT *Name of Ship: ?
2 CONT *Arrival Date: abt. 1643
2 CONT *Origin of Immigrant: Nottinghamshire, England
2 CONT *Immigrant's Date & Place of Birth: 1615, Nottinghamshire, England
2 CONT *Immigrant's Date & Place of Death: Oct 1705, Middletown, Monmouth Co. 
2 CONT *NJ Immigrant's Spouse: Penelope VanPrincin
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigrant's Children:
2 CONT *1. John Stout, 1645 Gravesend L.I. New Netherlands, m. Elizabeth Crawford, d. 1724 NJ
2 CONT *2. Richard Stout, 1646 -- 1717, m Frances Heath, Mary Seymour
2 CONT *3. James Stout, 1648 - ? , m Elizabeth Truax
2 CONT *4. Mary Stout, 1650 - ? , m Judge James Bowne 1665
2 CONT *5. Alice Stout, 1652 - ? m John Throckmorton 1670
2 CONT *6. Peter Stout, 1654- 1703, m Mary Bollen, then Mary Bowne
2 CONT *7. Sarah Stout, 1656 - ? m John Pike
2 CONT *8. Johnathan Stout, 1660 - 1723, m Anne Bollen 1685
2 CONT
2 CONT All the above were born in Gravesend Long Island
2 CONT
2 CONT *9. David Stout, 1667 - ? b in Middletown NJ, m Rebecca Ashton 1688
2 CONT *10. Benjamin Stout, 1669-1734, m Mary Leonard, then Agnes Truax
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: Married in Gravesend, Long Island in 1644, a member of Lady Deborah Moody's anabaptist settlement. He accompanied her from Massachusetts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664 he moved his family from NY to NJ and was an original patentee of Middletown, Monmouth Co. NJ. (records in the NJ Archives)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:"Stout and Allied Families", by Herald F. Stout, vol. 1, 1951 edition, pg. xxii
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Doug Gordon's Family History: http://history.wdgordon.com/gedcom/p11.htm#i302
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout[1]
2 CONT *b. circa 1615, 
2 CONT *d. 1705
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout was born circa 1615 in Nottinghamshire, England.[2] He was the son of John Stout and Elizabeth Bee.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout married Penelope Kent in 1644.[1],[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He left England as a naval sailor before being discharged in New Amsterdam (N.Y.). He was one of the founders of Gravesend, on Long Island, N.Y., and was also an early settler in Monmouth Co., N.J. near Middletown.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout died in 1705 in Monmouth Co., New Jersey.[2]
2 CONT
