2 CONT * * * * * * * *  Excerpts from a STOUT-L posting by Linda Stout Deak: 
2 CONT
2 CONT I traveled today to Amsterdam and went to the Scheepsvaart (maritime or  Ship Navigation, esp. Atlantic) Museum. It is a splendid old granite  building on the water a fifteen minute walk from Amsterdam Central Station.  I was look
2 CONC ing for Penelope's name on a passenger list. I had to find the  ship upon which she sailed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 107.1 Kath  Hans Jelisz. (owner) Jacht (yacht or sailboat) WIC (West Indies Company)  1647  Nieuw Amsterdam voor 06-06-1647 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kreeg in Juni 1647 de opdracht tot kaapveren. November 1648 bij Sandy Hook  gestrand. Did not return 
2 CONT
2 CONT This has to be Penelope's ship. I scanned the doctoral thesis (in Dutch) of  a J.A. Jacobs from Leiden University on the ships sailing to the new world  from Holland between 1609-1675. The average was 3.75 ships per year, about  f
2 CONC ive ships per year in the period 1639-1648. It is very unlikely that a ship  other than the Kath was beached at Sandy Hook. 
2 CONT
