2 CONT
2 CONT Richard STOUT Sr. > was born in 1584 in Nottinghamshire, England.  He was born in 1604 in Nottinghamshire, England. "Richard Stout is said to have left home because of parental interference in an affair of love with a young woman 
2 CONC who was considered below him in social scale. He enlisted on a man-of-war where he served seven years receiving his discharge at New Amsterdam where his vessel happened to be when his term of enlistment expired." Note: If Richard 
2 CONC was born in 1604 and was in New Amsterdam in 1622 as record indicate, his alleged love affair must have taken place when he was eleven years old. His residence in New Amsterdam was taken up after seven years of enlistment on the m
2 CONC an-of -war, according to the foregoing statement by Mr. Streets. He was born about 1615 in Nottinghamshire, England. He emigrated in 1622 from Long Island, New York. Richard Stout of Nottingham, England, (son of John Stout), settl
2 CONC ed in Long Island about 1622, in which year he was one of 39 original patentees of Gravesend. He was Testified on 13 Oct 1643.October 13, 1643, Richard Aestin, Ambrose Love and Richard Stout made declaration that the crew of the S
2 CONC even Stars and of the Privateer landed at the farm of Anthoney Jansen of Sallee (New Utrecht) in the bay and took off 200 pumpkins and would have carried off a lot of hogs from Coney Island had they not learned they belonged to th
2 CONC e Lady Moody. He resided in 1648 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey. He was Lawsuit on 8 Oct 1663 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York. In the Town Court, of Gravesend, October 8, 1663, "It is said Richard Stout declareth that
2 CONC  Nathaniel Brittain had slandered him; that he had sold wine to the Indians. The said Nathaniel denieth it, but said that the Indians told his wife that they had bought wine of Stout." The Court, however, ordered Nathaniel to pay 
2 CONC costs of prosecution. He owned Purchased tract of land from Indian Chief Papomore on 25 Jan 1664 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York.In 1664, January 25th, Richard Stout, John Bowne, John Tilton, Jr. and others bought a tract of l
2 CONC and of Papomore, Chief of Indians, the deed of which is recorded at Albany, also other tracts from other Indians, April 7th, 1665 and June 5th, 1665. He was Original Patentee of New Jersey in 1665 in Monmouth Co., NJ.Richard was o
2 CONC ne of the twelve patentees of New Jersey. He was Member of General Assembly in 1671 in Portland Point, New Jersey.  "Richard Stout, member of first General Assembly (composed of Deputies and Patentees), convened at Portland Point,
2 CONC  New Jersey, 1671. Indian Commissioner. He signed a will on 4 Jun 1703 in Middletown, Royal Colony of New Jersey. 1703 June 9. Stout, Richard, senior, of Middletown; will of. Wife ______. Sons - John, Richard, James, Jonathan, Dav
2 CONC id, Benjamin; daughters - mary, Alse, Sarah; daughter-in-law Marey Stoute and her son John, kinswoman Mary Stoute, daughter of Peter Stout. Real and personal property. Executors - sons John & Jonathan. Witnesses - Richard Hartshor
2 CONC ne, John Weepham and Peter VanDevandeter (?). Proved Oct 23, 1705. Lib. I, p. 120 & Monmouth wills. He died about 1705 in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey. He had an estate probated on 6 Oct 1705 in Middletown, Royal Colony of
2 CONC  New Jersey. 1705 Oct 6. Inventory of personal estate (64.8/0 pounds, mostly hogs, cattle, horses and sheep); made by Obadiah Bowne and James Hubbard. He resided in Squan, New Jersey. He was also known as Squan Dick. He owned Land
2 CONC  purchase in Middletown, Monmouth Co., New Jersey.  In the early days after his removeal to Middletown, Richard became one of the purchasers of the Monmouth Tract of land embraced between the Raritan and Sandy Point. "Richard Stou
2 CONC t, the first name in America was born in Notinghamshire, in Old England, and his father's name was John. The said Richard, when quite young paid his addresses to a young woman that his father thought below his rank, upon which acc
2 CONC ount some unpleasant conversation happened between the father and the son, on account of which, the said Richard left his father's house, and in a few days engaged aboard a ship of war, where he served about seven years, after whi
2 CONC ch time he got a discharge at New Amsterdam, now called New York, in America. About the same time a ship from Amsterdam, in Holland, on her way to the said New Amsterdam, was driven on the shore that is now called Middletown, in M
2 CONC onmouth County, in the State of New Jersey, which ship was loaded with passengers, who with much difficulty got on shore. But the Indians not long after fell upon them and butchered and killed the whole crew, as they thought, but 
2 CONC soon after the Indians were gone, a certain Penelope Van Princes, whose husband the Indians had killed, found herself possessed of strength enough to creep of a hollow tree, where she remained some days. An Indian happening to com
2 CONC e that way, whose dog coming to the tree, occasioned him to examine the inside of the tree, where he found the said Penelope in a forlorn, distressed condition. She was bruised very severely about the head, and her bowels protrude
2 CONC d from a cut across her abdomen, she kept them in with her hand. She had been in this fearful condition seven days when the Indian found her. In his compassion he took her out of the tree and carried her to his wigwam where he tre
2 CONC ated her kindly and healed her wounds, and in a short time conveyed her in his canoe to New Amsterdam, where he sold her to the Dutch, who then owned that city, now called New York.   The man and woman from whom the whole race of 
2 CONC Stouts descended, got into the city of New Amsterdam, where they became acquainted with each other and were married. And not withstanding, it may be thought by some, that they conducted themselves with more fortitude than prudence
2 CONC , they immediately crossed the bay and settled in the above said Middletown, where the said Penelope had lost her husband by the Indians and had been so severely wounded herself.   There was at that time but six families in the se
2 CONC ttlement, including their own (which was in the year 1648), where they continued until they became rich in prosperity and rich in children."Richard appears to have come to New Amsterdam about 1640. He became a  subject of the Neth
2 CONC erlands & remained in the area untill the British came. He & a few others went to New Jersey & purchased land that was to becme the settlement of Monmouth. Upon his death he had become the largest landed proprieter in the area. In
2 CONC  1668 he & his wife helped to organize the first Baptist church of New Jersey. Richard's will was proved in October of 1705. He was married to Penelope VAN PRINCISS about 1644 in Gravesend, Long Island, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT More About RICHARD STOUT and PENELOPE VAN PRINCIS: Marriage: 1644, Gravesend, LI, NY  Children of RICHARD STOUT and PENELOPE VAN PRINCIS are: 
2 CONT 	i.		JOHN3 STOUT, b. 1645, Gravesend, Long Island, NY; d. 24 Nov 1724, Middletown, Monmouth Co., NJ.	
2 CONT 	ii.		RICHARD STOUT, b. 1646, Gravesend, LI, NY; d. 10 Jul 1717, Middletown, Monmouth, NJ.	
2 CONT 	iii.		JAMES STOUT, b. 1648, Gravesend, Long Island, NY; d. Aft. 1714, Amwell twp, Hunterdon Co., NJ.	
2 CONT 	iv.		MARY STOUT, b. 1650, Gravesend, Long Island, NY.	
2 CONT 	v.		ALICE STOUT, b. 1652, Middletown, Monmouth Co., NJ.	
2 CONT 	vi.		PETER STOUT, b. 1654, Gravesend, Kings County, NY; d. 14 Apr 1704, Middletown, Monmouth County, NJ.	
2 CONT 	vii.		SARAH ELIZABETH STOUT, b. 1656, Gravesend, LI, NY; d. 29 Dec 1714, Woodbridge, Middlesex Co., NJ.	
2 CONT 	viii.		JONATHAN STOUT, b. 1658, Gravesend, LI, NY; d. Bef. 25 Mar 1723, Hopewell, Hunterdon, NJ.	
2 CONT 	ix.		DAVID STOUT, b. 1667, Middletown, Monmouth, NJ; d. 1732, Amwell, Hunterdon, NJ.	
2 CONT 	x.		BENJAMIN STOUT, b. 1669, Middletown, Monmouth, NJ; d. 1734, Georges Creek, Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adapted from Lequear's "Traditions of Hunterdon." Originally published in The Hunterdon Republican 1869-70.   East Amwell is Rich in History 
2 CONT Richard and Penelope Stout had seven sons. They lived at Freehold, Monmouth county. Their son James was the first of the name who came to Amwell. He settled on the west side of Jacob Manners’ farm, where Abraham Runkle now lives. 
2 CONC His brother, David, came soon after and brought his family, settling about a mile north of James. Benjamin Stout, David’s youngest son, settled northwest of the Manners farm. David gave the land for the family burying ground, whic
2 CONC h was then on a part of his farm. The old David Stout dwelling house is still standing, about a mile northeast of Jacob Manners’ residence.
1 NOTE See Historical Document.
1 NOTE Richard may have been b. 1602/4. I guessed 1610 because his parents md. 13
2 CONT Nov 1609.
1 NOTE Richard was 1st English settler in N J in 1648, remaining for 4 yrs. when the English were driven out by the Dutch.  He returned to Middletown, Monmouth Co., in 1665.  On 30 Dec 1667, he had lot 6  in Middletown.  He was one of th
2 CONC e fonders of the Middletown Baptist Church.  His will proved 23 Oct or Dec 1705.
1 NOTE Family tradition says that he left England because of friction with his father, who interfered with his love affairs, which drove him to engage on a man-of-war for 7 years. He received his discharge at New Amsterdam. Emigrated in 
2 CONC 1640. He married Penelope and lived together at Middletown among other Dutch inhabitants. 
2 CONT He was one of the patentees of Gravesend, Long Island, in 1645 and in 1665 he was one of 12 men to whom the Monmouth patent was granted. So he was one of the original and permament settles in East Jersey. Richard and Penelope sett
2 CONC led on a farm about 3 miles west of the village of Middletown and are buried there. In his will dated June 9, 1703 mentioned his sons John, Richard, James Jonathan, David & Benjamin and his daughters Mary, Alice, Sarah, his daught
2 CONC er in law Mary Stout and her son John and his "kinswoman" Mary Stout, the daughter formerly of Peter Stout.  
2 CONT According to Geneology.com they put his death on 23 Oct 1705 Perth Amboy, NJ
