2 CONT     This has to be Penelope's ship. I scanned the doctoral thesis (in Dutch) of a J.A. Jacobs from Leiden University on th e ships sailing to the new world from Holland between 1609- 1675. The average was 3.75 ships per year, abou
2 CONC t five ships per year in the period 1639-1648. It is very unlikely that a ship other than the Kath was beached at Sandy Hook.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1643, Penelope and her husband took a ship from the Netherlands to New Amsterdam.  Their ship foundering, she and her husband and several others made land at Sandy Hook.  Her husband, often identified as Jan Van Princin, was no
2 CONC t able to travel and after the couple were abandoned.  There the couple fell prey to hostile Indians who, after killing her husband, left Penelope to die.  She was horribly cut and mangled, her skull fractured, her left shoulder h
2 CONC acked, and a great cut across the abdomen caused her bowels to protrude.  She took shelter in a hollow tree until seven days later she felt compelled to make herself known to two members of  the Navesink tribe of Leni Lenape.  Pen
2 CONC elope prayed that they might end her misery, and the younger Indian was willing to oblige, but the elder stopped him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Throwing her over his shoulder, the elder Indian carried her to his camp, where he sewed up her wounds with fish bone needles and vegetable fiber. Penelope lived with the aged chief until she recovered.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was well enough to travel she was perhaps sold to the Dutch at New Amsterdam.  There she married Richard Stout.  They had a large family (7 sons and 3 daughters) mostly born at Gravesend in the area of Coney Island, Brook
2 CONC lyn.  They moved to Middletown Township, New Jersey around 1665.  This was where the Leni Lenapi who had earlier helped her were living, and they were still living there when the Stouts arrived.  In some versions of the story, the
2 CONC  Indian who rescued Penelope many years earlier warned her of an Indian attack in the planning stages, and she was able to thwart it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The dates and surname for Penelope are quite variable in the several references. The Gravesend Town Records, as written by Englishmen at the time of a slander trial in Sept 1648 name the defendant as Penelope Prince  However, this
2 CONC  does not necessarily mean that she had not yet married Richard Stout, as married Dutch women in that time period traditionally used their maiden names.  In honor of her being a pioneer in Middletown, Penelope Lane off of Kings Hi
2 CONC ghway is named after her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The early Dutch settler Penelope Van Princis Kent Stout, dubbed the "Mother of Middletown", may also be New Jersey's most famous survivor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Circa 1622, Penelope Kent was born in Amsterdam, Holland, the Dutch Republic (now called the Netherlands). Some sources claim that Penelope was a daughter of Baron Van Princis (a.k.a. Van Prinzen). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Circa 1640 -- Penelope van Princis and John Kent married at England. They sailed for New Amsterdam (present day New York), but near the end of the journey their ship ran aground near what is now the Highlands, Monmouth County, New
2 CONC  Jersey. Although the other stranded passengers resumed their journey on foot, Penelope stayed behind in the Navesink woods with her husband who had become too ill to travel. There the couple were attacked by hostile Native Americ
2 CONC ans who, after killing her husband, left Penelope to die. Partially scalped and bleeding from an abdominal wound, she was rescued by friendly Native Americans and eventually recovered from her injuries. Penelope was trying to hide
2 CONC  and survive on her own when she was taken in by the Navesink tribe of Leni Lenapi who returned her to the Dutch at New Amsterdam.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Native Americans took her to New Amsterdam, Penelope married an English-born colonist, Richard Stout, with whom she had a large family. Around 1669 the Stouts settled at Middletown, Monmouth county, New Jersey where Pene
2 CONC lope lived to the ripe old age of 110.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several of the Stouts' colonial era descendants are interred in the Presbyterian Burial Ground off King's Highway in Middletown, and while the exact location of Penelope's grave is unknown, she, too, was buried in this Monmouth Co
2 CONC unty town. (bio by: Nikita Barlow) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Commemorative medallion depicting the rescue of the gravely wounded Penelope Van Princis Kent by a compassionate Lenni Lenape chief. Attacked by hostile Indians in the woods off the New Jersey shore, Penelope had been left for dea
2 CONC d & her first husband, John Kent, killed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Commemorative medallion depicting Penelope's return to New Jersey with her second husband, Richard Stout, one of their 10 children, & a welcoming Lenni Lenape chief. Dubbed the "Mother of Middletown" & the "First Lady of Monmouth 
2 CONC County", more than 10,000 Americans are among her descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1644 -- At New Amsterdam, widow Penelope (Kent) van Princis married Richard Stout and settled at Gravesend which is across the bay from Coney Island, Brooklyn, Kings County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT September 9, 1664 -- New Netherland Director-General Peter Stuyvesant signed the treaty surrendering New Netherland, including New Jersey, to British Commander Colonel Richard Nicholls. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Circa 1665 -- Richard and Penelope Stout moved from Gravesend (now Brooklyn, Kings County, New York) to what became Middletown Township, Monmouth County, New Jersey.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
