1 NOTE Notes for PENELOPE (KENT OR LENT) VAN PRINCIS: Newspaper article - Newspaper name and time of publication unknown, author was John T. Cunningham   ------------ THE STORY OF PENELOPE STOUT -----------  There is cause to dispute the
2 CONC  traditional claim that Penelope vanPrincis Stout of Monmouth County lived to a mature 110 years before she died in 1712, but no one can deny that for indomitable will to live and in number of descendants Penelope has had few equa
2 CONC ls.  Penelopes's story is obscured slightly by discrepanceies in the dates of her birth and other occurrences in her life, but consider first the narrative as it is usually told.  Born in Holland (in 1602 according to the usual ve
2 CONC rsion,) Penelope vanPrincis joined her young husband and other Dutch settlers headed for New Amsterdam in 1620. Violent storms caught their ship, drove it off course and finally wrecked it off Sandy Hook.  --- ON THE BEACH ---  Al
2 CONC l survived, and the passengers and crew set off for New Amsterdam on foot, leaving Penelope on the beach to nurse her desperately ill husband (whose name was never recorded by Penelope and all of the large brood she would later re
2 CONC ar.) Indians found the Dutch couple on the beach, killed the husband and left Penelope viciously hacked.  The young widow lay unconscious, her skull fractured, her left arm so mangled that it would never again be normal and her ab
2 CONC domen slashed open. Somehow she revived and crawled into a hollow tree, where two Indians found her several days later.  ---- SHE PRAYED ---  Penelope prayed that they might end her misery and the younger Indian was willing to obl
2 CONC ige. The older Indian dissented, carried her over his shoulder to camp, and there nursed her back to health. She stayed with the Indians, working, learning their language and their ways.  Some of her shipwrecked friends returned a
2 CONC fter a time and asked the Indians to give her up. Penelope's Indian benefactor said he would let the young woman decide for herself. Penelope decided to leave, "very much to the surprise of this good Indian," according to Frank St
2 CONC ocktons's version.  About two years later Penelope met Richard Stout who had left Nottingham, England, because of parental disapproval of his love affair with a girl they considered socially inferior. He enlisted in the navy, serv
2 CONC ed for seven years and left ship in New Amsterdam when his enlistment ended.  Penelope vanPrincis and Richard Stout were married in 1624 (according to tradition), when she was 22 and Richard was 40. Some time after, they moved to 
2 CONC Middletown, where through the years their family grew and prospered.  Several years after the Stouts came to Middletown, Penelope's old Indian benefactor called on her to warn of an impending attack by his tribe. Penelope and her 
2 CONC children fled in a canoe, but Richard Stout and his neighbors stood up to the Indians and argued them out of an attack. So the Stouts lived on into the 18th century.  Dr. Thomas Hale Streets questioned the time sequence in a study
2 CONC  he made of the Delaware branch of the Stout family in 1915.  He said that all dates in recorded accounts were about 20 years too early, thus making the date of the shipwreck about 1640 rather than 1620 and making the date of the 
2 CONC marriage to Richard Stout about 1644 rather than 1624. This logic seems sound.  For example, there was no New Amsterdam in 1620 and certainly there was no Middletown at the time when the Stout allegedly moved over. Advancing all d
2 CONC ates 20 years, however, makes New Amsterdam, Middletown and all else fall in line.  His most telling rebuttal hinged on the known birh date of Penelope's 10th and last child, David, born in 1669. That would have made Mrs. Stout a 
2 CONC mother at age 67 and Richard a father at 85. Speaking of the mother, Dr. Streets commented drily:  "No medical man, it is safe to say, ever knew of such a case."  Penelope vanPrincis Stout died in 1712, either at the age of 110, i
2 CONC f you believe traditional accounts, or at the age of 90 if Dr. Streets is correct.  Before dying, Mrs. Stout saw her seven sons and three daughters multiplied into 492 other descendants.  One son, Jonathan, bought a large tract of
2 CONC  land at Hopewell in 1706 and quickly the number of Stout descendants in and near Hopewell became almost as numerous as those in Monmouth. Today huge numbers of Stout descendants cherish a noble name; they recognize that without P
2 CONC enelope vanPrincis, a stout-hearted woman if ever there was one, they wouldn't be here at all.   * * * * * * * *  12 Sep 1648":Ambrose London plaintive agt:ye wife of Tho: Aplegate defent in an action of slander for saying his wif
2 CONC e did milke her Cowe"   "The defent saith yt shee said noe otherwise but as Penellopey Prince tould her yt Ambrose his wife did milke her Cowe"   "Rodger Scotte being deposed saith yt being in ye house of Tho: Aplegate hee did hea
2 CONC re Pennellopy Prince saye yt ye wife of Ambrose London did milke ye Cowe of Tho: Aplegate"   "Tho: Greedye being deposed saith yt Pennellope Prince being att his house hee did heare her saye yt shee and Aplegates Daughter must com
2 CONC  as witnesses agat: Ambrose his wife milking Aplegates Coew"   "Pennellope Prince being questationed adknowled her faulte in soe speaking and being sorrie her words she spake gave sattisfaction on both sides." source: Gravesend To
2 CONC wn Book, vol. 1, Sept 12, 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Story of Penelope VanPrincis Stout  Peter Young showed us a deed signed by John, James, Jacob and David Stout, conveying real estate of their father, James, to their brother Joseph Stout. This was dated 1746. This James was th
2 CONC e son of the original “Amwell Stout,” one of the very first settlers. If tradition tells the truth, he came to this plantation as early as 1699, for Hopewell was known by that name at that date. The history of the mother of James 
2 CONC and Jonathan Stout is one of the most remarkable ever written.  “She was born in Amsterdam, about the year 1602. Her father’s name was VanPrincis. She and her first husband, (whose name is unknown,) sailed for New York, (then New 
2 CONC Amsterdam,) about the year 1620; the vessel was stranded by Sandy Hook; the crew got ashore, and marched to the said New York; but Penelope’s (for that was her name) husband being hurt in the wreck, could not march with them; ther
2 CONC efore he and his wife tarried in the woods; they had not been long in the place, before the Indians killed them both, (as they thought) and stripped them to the skin; however, Penelope came to, though her skull was fractured, and 
2 CONC her left shoulder so hacked that she could never use that arm like the other; she was also cut across the abdomen so that her bowels appeared; these she kept in with her hand. She continued in this situation for seven days, taking
2 CONC  shelter in a hollow tree, and eating the excresence of it. The seventh day she saw a deer passing by with arrows sticking in it, and soon after two Indians appeared, whom she was glad to see, in hope they would put her out of her
2 CONC  misery. Accordingly, one made towards her to knock her on the head; but the other, who was an elderly man, prevented him; and throwing his matchcoat about her carried her to his wigwam, and cured her of her wounds and bruises. Af
2 CONC ter that he took her to New York and made a present of her to her countrymen, viz.: an Indian present, expecting ten times the value in return. It was in New York that one Richard Stout married her; she was now in her 22nd year an
2 CONC d he in his 40th. She bore him seven sons and three daughters, viz.: Jonathan, (founder of Hopewell,) John, Richard, James, Peter, David, Benjamin, Mary, Sarah, and Alice; the daughters married into the families of the Bounds, Pik
2 CONC es, Throckmortons, and Skeltons, and so lost the name of Stout; the sons married into the families of Bullen, Cramford, Ashton, Traux, &c., and had many children. The mother lived to the age of 110, and saw her offspring multiplie
2 CONC d into 502, in about 88 years.”---“Hist. Baptists.”
1 NOTE The Strong Brave Penelope
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=407d522f-a2da-40ca-b531-fe1f257c9b52&tid=8556782&pid=-882450216
1 NOTE Prince
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Stout was her 3rd husband
1 NOTE Penelope may have d. 1733. These dates must be wrong, because she would have
2 CONT only been 6 when her 1st child was b. & only 7 when she md.
1 NOTE See Historical Document.
1 NOTE Story goes that Penelope & her 1st husband (who's name is said to  have been Lent) sailed for NY; vessel stranded at Sandy Hook; her husband hurt real bad, so they  stayed in the woods; Indians killed him & wounded her real bad.  
2 CONC After about a week, Indians found her; took her & cured her of her wounds.  She later married Richard.
1 NOTE The Strong Brave Penelope
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=8fdab97f-7b70-4c69-a799-1903919f3859&tid=7524179&pid=-1028068612
1 NOTE The Strong Brave Penelope
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=407d522f-a2da-40ca-b531-fe1f257c9b52&tid=8556782&pid=-882450216
1 NOTE Penelope was formerly from Amsterdam. She and her husband (name unknown) were sailing for New Amsterdam (NY City), when the ship was grounded (wrecked) off Sandy Hook, NJ.  The crew and other occupants left Penelope and her husban
2 CONC d on or near the beach. They started a fire on the beach. It is not known if her husband was sick during the sailing or if he was wounded during the wreck. But they were left behind by the rest because the other were concerned abo
2 CONC ut the local Indians attacking. The others marched off towards New Amsterdam. Some Indians did come along, and they killed her husband (not Stout). The Indians thought they had killed her, they stripped them both and left her for 
2 CONC dead. She was not dead, she did have a fractured skull, her left shoulder was hacked so badly that she had no use of that arm, and she was cut across the abdomen, so that her bowels were exposed. She had to hold her arm across her
2 CONC  abdomen. She crawled to a nearby hollow tree, where she hid, eating berries and whatever else she could find. She would crawl to what was left of the fire on the beach for warmth. Seven days later two Indians came along. The youn
2 CONC ger one wanted to kill her and the older one stopped him. They took her back to their village and took care of her wounds. When she was fully recovered the Indian took her to New Amsterdam to trade her to the white man.  He said s
2 CONC he was a present and he expected one in return. She became close to this Indian who saved her. Later in her life, when Indians from the same tribe were going to kill every white man in their territory. This same Indian came to her
2 CONC  to tell her to get away. She listened to him and escaped with her children. Luckily her current husband (Richard Stout) and other men in the area had a talk with the tribe and the battle was diverted. 
2 CONT Penelope lived to be 110 years old. When she married Richard he was 40 and she was 22. She had 502 descendents before she died. This story can be found in Hopewell Valley Heritage by Alice Blackwell Lewis, copywrite 1973, pg 8. Th
2 CONC ere is also mention of this in the Hopewell Museum. Which is the official Stout Museum.  All Stout's in America are related to this one Richard Stout. 
2 CONT Possible husband last name was Kent or Lent.
