1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Category: Gravesend, New Netherland
2 CONT {{New Netherland Settler}}
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT <blockquote>Richard's father Richard Stout was an early English settler under Dutch rule in  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravesend,_Brooklyn Gravesend, Brooklyn, Kings, western Long Island], ~1645-1657. Gravesend is now a neighb
2 CONC orhood in south-central Brooklyn, along the shore of Gravesend Bay and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney_Island Coney Island]. In 1609 Henry Hudson landed his ship the ''Half Moon'' there at the island known by the natives as ''
2 CONC Narrioch'' (Coney Island). In 1643 Gravesend then became one of the original towns founded in the Dutch colony of New Netherland when Governor Willem Kieft granted a landpatent to the Anabaptist Lady Deborah Moody, as a site where
2 CONC  that English sect could settle free from religious persecution. Clashes with the natives delayed the town for two more years, until  December 19, 1645.</blockquote>
2 CONT Richard Stout II, son of Richard I and Penelope Kent-Van Princin Stoutborn 1646 at Gravesend, <ref>''Historical and genealogical miscellany'', John E Stilwell, 1916, Vol IV, page 317 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04sti
2 CONC l#page/317/mode/1up</ref> and July 10 1717. <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV page 322 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/322/mode/1up</ref> He married (1) Francis Heath before June 1 1676 when Richard bought land i
2 CONC n Shrewsbury to settle there.However, Frances would not go unless a neighbor would move with them.  She had signed a land transaction with him in 1679-1680 and died after that date and before March 4 1691. <ref>John E Stillwell, V
2 CONC ol IV, page 318 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/318/mode/1up</ref> Land Richard acquired from Hananiah Gifford in March 4 1691 was conveyed to Ebenezer Cook September 26 1709 "reserving a place of ground thre
2 CONC e rods square, where the said Richard Stout's former wifewas buried " with both Richard and Mary's mark. <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 319 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/319/mode/1up</ref>  He married 
2 CONC (2) Mary Seymour about 1704 since they were witnesses to a marriage in 1704.<ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV,page 319 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/319/mode/1up</ref> Most likely children of Richard and France
2 CONC s Heath Stout based on theirlife events: John b. 1671 d 1699, <ref>''Stout and Allied Families'',Herald Franklin Stout, 1951, Vol 1 page 18 https://books.google.com/books?id=0BVWAAAAMAAJ&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=sailor+john</ref
2 CONC >  Lucy born 1666, <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 322 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/322/mode/1up</ref> Richard III, married 1706, <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 320 http://archive.org/stream/histo
2 CONC ricalgeneal04stil#page/320/mode/1up</ref>  Robert, bought land in 1709, <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV;, page 321 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/321/mode/1up</ref>  Joseph, received land from his father in 17
2 CONC 09, <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 321 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/321/mode/1up</ref> David, bought land 1712. <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 321 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil
2 CONC #page/321/mode/1up</ref> Most likely children of Richard and Mary Seymour Stout based on life events: Seymour, sold land given to him by his father before 1717 and his given name Seymour is maiden name of Mary, (custom of naming c
2 CONC hildren with wife's maiden name) <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 322 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/322/mode/1up/</ref> Penelope married 1731,<ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 322 http://archive.org/st
2 CONC ream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/322/mode/1up</ref> Rebecca, married 1726. <ref>John E Stillwell, Vol IV, page 323 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/323/mode/1up</ref>
2 CONT
