1 NAME Peter /Stout/
2 GIVN Peter
2 SURN Stout
2 _MARNM Stout
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1654
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gravesend
3 STAE Nieuw-Nederland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 APR 1704
2 ADDR
3 CITY Middletown
3 STAE New Jersey
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born at Gravesend, Long Island. Wealthy and respected citizen of Middletown, NJ; 1790 received lot at Hop River and 7 acres of meadowland at Conesconk from father. Died at Middletown, Long Island, NY. Will proved 1
2 CONC 4 April 1704: Lib. I, p 45, N. J. wills. Capt. James Bowne, exec.
2 CONT
2 CONT Part of family:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT John 1675
2 CONT
2 CONT ?Samuel
2 CONT
2 CONT ?Margaret
2 CONT
2 CONT ?Elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT information from (p. 4) Stout and Allied Families: Volume One, 1951, Harold F. Stout, The Eagle Press, Dover, Ohio
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category: Gravesend, New Netherland]]
2 CONT {{New Netherland Settler}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT <blockquote>Peter's father Richard Stout was an early English settler under Dutch rule in  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravesend,_BrooklynGravesend, Brooklyn, Kings, western Long Island], ~1645-1657. Gravesend is now a neighborh
2 CONC ood in south-central Brooklyn, along the shore ofGravesend Bay and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney_Island Coney Island]. In 1609 Henry Hudson landed his ship the ''Half Moon'' there atthe island known by the natives as ''Narri
2 CONC och'' (Coney Island). In 1643 Gravesend then became one of the original towns founded in the Dutch colony of New Netherland when Governor Willem Kieft granted a land patent to the Anabaptist Lady Deborah Moody, as a site where tha
2 CONC t English sect could settle free from religious persecution. Clashes with thenatives delayed the town for two more years, until  December 19, 1645.</blockquote>
2 CONT Peter Stout, son of Richard and Penelope Van Princin Stout, was born 1654 and died before June 9 1703. He married Mary Bullen born 1655 and died 1692. His will may be found here which is dated April 14 1704. <ref>''Calendar of New
2 CONC  Jersy Wills, Adminstrations, etc'', William Nelson,etal, 1901, Vol 1, page 445 https://archive.org/stream/calendarofnewjer00newj#page/445/mode/1up/search/peter+stout</ref> <ref>''Historical and genealogical miscellany'', John Sti
2 CONC lwell, 1916, pages 329-330 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/329/mode/1up</ref> born 1655 and died 1692.  More about Peter may be found here. <ref>''History of the Stout Family'', Nathan Stout, 1813, page 15 ht
2 CONC tp://files.usgwarchives.net/nj/monmouth/history/family/stout01.txt</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT They were the parents of these known children Mary, John, and Samuel.In Richard Stout's will Peter's wife is named as daughter-in-law, John, her son and Mary, Peter's daughter. <ref>''Pioneers of Old Hopewell'', Ralph Ege, 1915, p
2 CONC ages 280-281 https://play.google.com/books/reader?id=uQR_vjf2_EEC&printsec=frontcover&output=reader&hl=en&pg=GBS.PA286.w.1.1.0</ref> 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT <references />
2 CONT
2 CONT == Research notes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Disputed children ===Note:  There are other children, two Margarets and one Elizabeth namedin family trees which do not give adequate sources for them.  Birth and death dates have been entered for them which suggest they died 
2 CONC before their father's will was written.
