1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Category: New Castle County, Delaware Category: Middletown Township, New Jersey
2 CONT {{New Netherland Descendant}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT Benjamin Stout, son of Richard I and Penelope Kent Van Princin Stout, was probably their youngest son if one follows the order of birth given in the concessions and born 1669 and died before June 10 1734.<ref>''The Stout Family of
2 CONC  Delaware'', Thomas Hale Streets, 1915 page 22, 28https://archive.org/stream/stoutfamilydela00stregoog#page/n22/mode/2up/search/benjamin+</ref>  He married  (1) Mary Leonard <ref>Stout and Allied Familes, Herald F. Stout, 1951, pa
2 CONC ge 6 https://books.google.com/books?id=0BVWAAAAMAAJ&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=mary+leonard</ref>and (2)  Agnes who was a member of the Baptist Church in 1712.  <ref>''Historical and genealogical miscellany'', John Stilwell, 1916,
2 CONC  page 346http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/346/mode/1up</ref>   Prior to the second marriage Benjamin gave Charles and Benjamin were property in 1721 probably. More about this can be found here.  <ref>Thomas Ha
2 CONC le Streets, 1915, page 26 https://archive.org/stream/stoutfamilydela00stregoog#page/n26/mode/2up/search/benjamin+</ref>
2 CONT At some point Benjamin Stout traveled to Pennsylvania and then to New Castle, Delaware ending up in Appoquinimink hundred <ref>''The Historyof the Stout Family'', Nathan Stout, 1823, page 17 http://files.usgwarchives.net/nj/monmou
2 CONC th/history/family/stout01.txt</ref> <ref>Thomas Hale Streets, 1915, page 26 https://archive.org/stream/stoutfamilydela00stregoog#page/n26/mode/2up</ref> where he died. He did not have sufficient personal property to pay his debts.
2 CONC  Agnes Stout, his widow, and Jacob Stout, his son, petitioned the Orphans Court November 18 1735 for permission to sell the real estate.  This was approved and both Jacob Stout and Agnes Stout signed the conveyance and a copy of h
2 CONC is will is found here. <ref>Thomas Hale Streets, 1915, page 28 https://archive.org/stream/stoutfamilydela00stregoog#page/n28/mode/2up</ref> <ref>JohnStilwell, 1916, page 346 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/34
2 CONC 6/mode/1up</ref> 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Benjamin and Mary Leonard Stout were:
2 CONT *1.  Charles 
2 CONT *2. Benjamin Jr
2 CONT
2 CONT and the children of Benjamin Sr and Agnes Truax? Stout was:*3. Jacob<ref>Thomas Hale Streets, 1915, page 26 https://archive.org/stream/stoutfamilydela00stregoog#page/n26/mode/2up</ref>
2 CONT *4. Agnes m Alexander Blackshear. Agnes Stout Blackshear's great (5) granddaughter says on the Find A Grave memorial she created that Agnes' mother was Agnes Truax and offerssupportive information on all three memorials. <ref>Find
2 CONC  A Grave Memorial, Roy and Robyn Adair, creator and photographer http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=121415153</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references />
2 CONT Benjamin Stout did NOT die on 10 June 1734.  That is the date his will was proved in court.  A person has to have died BEFORE the will is proved and recorded in the court records.  His will was written 25 April 1734.  That means h
2 CONC e died sometime between the day he wrote his will (25 April 1734) and the will was proved in court (10 June 1734).  How long it takes for the administrator(s) to present the will to court may be a matter of days, weeks, months, or
2 CONC  even years.  Most generally it is between a few days to a month or so.  To correct in dating Benjamin's death, it should be recorded as "he died between 25 April 1734 and 10 June 1734" or more simply, "he died in 1734."* Referenc
2 CONC e: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/124870007/benjamin-stout Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 5 2022, 9:24:29 UTC''
