1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT {{1776| category = American Revolution}}{{NSSAR Patriot Ancestor | sar-number = P299055| rank = Private }}
2 CONT == Biography == John Stout, son of Zebulon and Charity Burrows (Burroughs) Stout, bornabout 1730 and died before December 27 1798 the date his will was proved. He  married Mabel Sexton, daughter of Charles Sexton Sr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children with their spouses as stated in his will were:*1. Zephaniah m Rhoda Stout, daughter of Nathaniel and Charity Furman Stout.  Zephaniah and Rhoda had two sons, Ebenezer, who became a lawyer, and one died in childhood an
2 CONC d after his death she married Burgess Allison.
2 CONT *2. Amos m __________Morgan and moved to the Lake Country*3. Elizabeth "Betsy" b 1750 m Nathaniel Hart, son of John and DeborahScudder Hart, May 2 1770.  Moved to Mason County, Kentucky in 1795.
2 CONT *4. Mabel m James Campbell
2 CONT *5. Kesiah m Lewis Gordon
2 CONT *6. Rachel m Jonathan Stout*7. Charity m John Parke.  <ref>''The History of the Stout Family'', Nathan Stout, 1823, pages 8-9 http://files.usgwarchives.net/nj/monmouth/history/family/stout01.txt</ref> <ref>''Historical and genalog
2 CONC ical miscellany'', John E Stillwell, 1916, Vol IV, page 337 http://archive.org/stream/historicalgeneal04stil#page/337/mode/1up/search/charity+burrows</ref> <ref>''The History of the Stout Family'', Nathan Stout,1823, page 8 http:/
2 CONC /files.usgwarchives.net/nj/monmouth/history/family/stout01.txt</ref> <ref>''Pioneers of Old Hopewell'', Ralph Ege, 1908,pages 251-256 https://archive.org/stream/pioneersoldhope00egegoog#page/n264/mode/1up/search/charles+sexton</re
2 CONC f> <ref>''Calendar of New Jersey Wills'', Elmer T Hutchinson, Vol IX (1796-1800), page 351 https://books.google.com/books?id=iMeVj8cUVE4C&pg=PA6&dq=Calendar+of+New+Jersey+Wills#v=onepage&q=john%20stout&f=false</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Military==
2 CONT *Revolutionary War Veteran
2 CONT *Military Date	1777-1780
2 CONT *Military Place	New Jersey, USA
2 CONT *State or Army Served	   New Jersey
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Research Notes==1. Elizabeth Stout Hart's father-in-law was the first signer of the Declaration of Independence with consequences by Nathaniel and his brother recounted on page 256 of Ralph Ege's work cited above.2. His will is 
2 CONC an interesting read in which he names all of his children, their spouses and Ebenezer, his grandson, whose mother Rhoda StoutAllison had died in January of 1798.  The will written December 26 1796 had a codicil written April 9 179
2 CONC 8 in which the legacy given in thewill is modified as distributed to his daughters.
2 CONT
