2 CONT 1820July 24, 1820, Washington reporter (newspaper) concerning the Washington Crossroads Light Artillery Company, an article as follows:
2 CONT 4 July 1820
2 CONT Transcribed from an article in
2 CONT The Washington Reporter on 24 July 1820
2 CONT From www.GenealogyBank.com
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 4th of July 1820, according to previous notice, the Washington Cross Roads Light Artillery Company met at Bricelands Cross Roads, and after performing a number of evolutions, they retired to a spring with a number of the ci
2 CONC tizens where Elijah Crafford was appointed president and Capt., James McFarren, vice president, and Alex Conn, secretary- after the Declaration of Independence was read the following resolutions were severally read and adopted:
2 CONT 1stResolved, That as our national and civil rights are privileges are secured to us both as a state and a nation better than to any other in our knowledge and as it is from the kind hand of a magnificent Providence that every good
2 CONC  comes; therefore we esteem it to be our highest privilege and our first duty to acknowledge God in all things and especially on this day to commemorate with solemn gratitude his divine goodness manifested in raising up and inspir
2 CONC ing with wisdom and courage, men who in the face of the most appalling danger dared to declare and did achieve our independence and liberty, and have formed the most equitable and righteous government in the world.
2 CONT 2ndResolved, That we commemorate with profound respect and veneration the Men!!! The Heroes!!! The Veterans!!! Who fought and bled to gain our liberty and independence and who have handed them down unsullied to us their posterity.
2 CONT 3rdResolved, Therefore that it is our duty and that we after their laudable example do hereby pledge ourselves, our posterity, our lives and our sacred honour to each other and to the state and nation that we will with our might d
2 CONC efend the same and hand them down to our posterity [illegible word] the same purity as the best legacy this world can afford.
2 CONT 4thResolved, That it is our privilege and we esteem it our duty as freemen citizens of the state of Pennsylvania, publicly to express our sentiments respecting our [illegible word] and state policy.
2 CONT 5thResolved, That we mark with deep sensations of regret and abhorrence everything which is done by any man or body of men that strikes at the liberty and independence of our beloved country.
2 CONT 6thResolved, That we view with approbation the general conduct of James Monroe, president of the U. S. and that he is worthy of our support at the ensuing election.
2 CONT 7thResolved, That we view with the deepest regret, the conduct of our national legislature last session in admitting two new slave states into the American Union, and in voting down the tariff bill by which they have done incalcul
2 CONC able injury to the United States.
2 CONT 8thResolved, That we view the loan office bill as it was recommended animpolitic measure.
2 CONT 9thResolved, That the proceedings of the day with the above resolutions be signed by the president and vice president and attested by the secretary, and published in the Washington Examiner and Reporter.
2 CONT Elijah Crafford, Pres’t
2 CONT Jas McFarren, Vice-Pres’
2 CONT Attest-Alex. Conn, Secretary.
2 CONT NMB6-2009
2 CONT
