2 CONT Elijah's Pension File
2 CONT 1832- In his pension application statement made to the Court of Common Pleas of Washington County,Pennsylvania in October of 1832, Elijah states his service in theBattle of Long Island, a battle where it is reported many losses by
2 CONC  death and where John Arndt was wounded. No mention of marriage orname of spouse was made in the pension application statement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pension Statement of Elijah Crafford
2 CONT Stateof Pennsylvania}}
2 CONT WashingtonCounty}}
2 CONT On this first day of October AD 1832 personally appeared in open court before the Hon H K Baird and his associates Judges of a Court of Common Please now sitting Elijah Crafford a resident of the Township of Hanover, County of Was
2 CONC hington, and State of Pennsylvania aged seventy six years who being first duly sworn according to law doth on his oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the Act of Congress pass
2 CONC ed June7th 1832.
2 CONT That he enlisted in what was called the flying camp in Capt. John Arndt’s Company and Col. Peter Keithlines [actually Kichlein’s] Battalion in the month of June A.D.1776 and in the beginning of July following he marched from Easto
2 CONC n Northampton County Pennsylvania in which County he then resided through the State of New Jersey to New Brunswick and from thence to New York. That on the 27thday of August he was in the Battle of Long Island and after the retrea
2 CONC t from the Island Capt. Arndt’s Company fell into Col Edward Hands Regiment they were marched to a place above New York called Dulingeies [should be Delancey’s] Mills where this applicant did Military duty for some time; from that
2 CONC  place he was marched to Amboy and from thence to Boundbrook where he was discharged by Capt John Arndt in the latter part of the Month of Dec’r of the same year,and that his discharge is lost.
2 CONT He hereby relinquishes every claim whatever to a pension or annuity except the present and declares that his name is not on the pension book of any agency or state.
2 CONT E Crafford [signature]
2 CONT Sworn the day and year aforesaid Thomas Officer Proth’y
2 CONT We Elisha Macurdy a clergyman residing in the Township of Hanover County of Washington and State of Pennsylvania and James McFarren residing in the same place hereby certify that we are well acquainted with Elijah Crafford who has
2 CONC  subscribed & sworn to the above declaration that we believe him to be seventy six years of age that he is respected [possibly reputed] and believed in the neighborhood where he resides to have been a soldier of the revolution and
2 CONC  that we concur in that opinion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elishaacurdy [signature]
2 CONT Ja’scFarren [signature]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sworn& subscribed the day and year aforesaid
2 CONT Thomas Officer Proth’y
2 CONT
2 CONT And the said court do hereby declare their opinion after the investigation of the matter and after putting the interrogatories prescribed by the war department that the above named applicant was a revolutionary soldier and served 
2 CONC as he states. And the court further certifies that it appears to them that Elisha Macurdy who has signed the preceding certificate is a clergyman resident in Hanover Township Washington County Pennsylvania and that James McFarren 
2 CONC who has also signed the same is a resident in said state, County and township and is a credible person and that their statement is entitled to credit.
2 CONT I Thomas Officer Clerk of the Court of Common Pleas in and for the County of Washington aforesaid. I hereby certify that the foregoing contains the original proceedings of said court in the matter of the application of Elijah Craw
2 CONC ford for a pension.
2 CONT In testimony whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal of office this first day of October 1832.
2 CONT Thomas Officer, Proth’y
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Stateof Pennsylvania}}
2 CONT Washington County}}
2 CONT On this 2nd day of Octo 1832 personally appeared in open Court before the Hon. Th H. Baird and his associates Robert Lyle who being sworn according to law deposeth and saith that was acquainted at camp with Elijah Crafford who has
2 CONC  subscribed and sworn to the foregoing declaration and that to his knowledge he served in the Revolutionary War as stated in said declaration.
2 CONT Sworn to & subscribed the day and year above written
2 CONT Robert Lyle [signature]
2 CONT Thomas Officer, Proth’y
2 CONT I Thomas Officer Prothonotary of the Court of Common Pleas in & for the County of Washington do hereby certify that Robert Lyle the above deponent is a person of good character & his statement is entitled to full credit and belief
2 CONC  Further that Jeremiah Emery before whom Elijah Crawford has sworn & subscribed the foregoing additional statement is a Justice of the Peace in & for the County of Washington & as such his acts and certificates are entitled to ful
2 CONC l credit & belief.
2 CONT Thomas Officer, Proth’y
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Personally appeared before me the under signer a Justice of the Peace in and for the County of Washington, Elijah Crawford who has subscribed &sworn to the above declaration & being duly sworn according tolaw doth say that by reas
2 CONC on of his age & infirmity & loss of memory Cannot swear precisely to the very length of his service but according to the best of his recollection he served not less than the period mentioned below and in the following grade.
2 CONT For six months I served as a Corporal in Capt John Arndt’s Company in Col Kerchlines Battalion.
2 CONT E Crafford
2 CONT Sworn and Subscribed before me the 25th day of December A.D. 1832
2 CONT [Jeremiah] Emery
2 CONT
2 CONT The Brief in the Case of Elijah Crafford, returned by the Examining Clerk of the Pension Board was found ‘defective’, ‘witness not certified credible’, and ‘witness certificate should precede opinion of the Court’….
2 CONT Thusly this pension was not certified until 10th January 1833 and was paid in arrears to the 4th of March 1831, the date of the passage of the Act of Congress. Certificate #13,346 was signed by Hon’ble I M L McRemon, House of Rep’
2 CONC s. [Somewhat illegible signature]
2 CONT
2 CONT ElijahCrafford, Pension Service File #2148 states his service as being fromJune 1776 through Dec 1776 as Corporal in John Arndt’s Company andCols Peter Keithline and Edward Hand. He engaged in the Battle ofLong Island, filed for p
2 CONC ension from Washington County, PA and that hedied on February 4, 1836, that date coming from the AgencyBook.County: Washington Co.Name: Elijah Crafford; Rank:CorporalAnnual Allowance: 22 00; Sums Received: 66 00Description ofservi
2 CONC ce: Pennsylvania state troopsWhen placed on the pension roll:January 10, 1833Commencement of pension: March 4, 1831Age: 77
2 CONT
