2 CONT 1619 Pinoak Pl Jonesboro Arkansas 72404-8038
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=1988-quest_1&id=I03958
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William Overton came to Virginia before 1659.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is generally believed and stated, although without absolute proof, that Gen. Robert Overton was the father of the Virginia immigrant. He was one of Oliver Cromwell's chief officers, and distinguished himself at the battle of Ma
2 CONC rston Moor. He accompanied Cromwell to Scotland in 1650, and commanded a brigade of Ironsides at the battle of Dunbar. He was a soldier and scholar and an intimate friend of Milton. He ended his years as a political prisoner in th
2 CONC e Tower of London. He married, in 1632, Anne, daughter of Jeremy Gardiner of Stratford Bow, Middlesex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT b. He is believed to have emigrated to Virginia shortly after his marriage in 1670 (another source states that they were married at Yorktown, on board the vessel in which Elizabeth Waters had come to Virginia). A book called Josep
2 CONC hus, which was in the possession of their daughter, Barbara (Overton) Winston, and which she passed, in her will, to her son, John Winston, and through several generations after that, was destroyed in a fire, but not before severa
2 CONC l written copies of its entries had been made by several different descendants. These were entries of William Overton, his date of birth and marriage, and the names and dates of birth of his children. Some records call his wife Ma
2 CONC ry, others Elizabeth, which appears to be the case also with one of their daughters. But the will of Ann Waters, dated 7 Sep 1697, of St. Sepulchre, London, England, specifically calls her daughter Elizabeth Overton "now in Virgin
2 CONC ia". Also named as being in Virginia is her brother, John Waters. A deed is recorded in the Virginia land books for 4,600 acres of land on the south side of Pamunky River on Falling Creek, 23 April 1681, to William Overton, for tr
2 CONC ansporting 92 persons to the colony, including Elizabeth Overton; this would appear to indicate that he had indeed married Elizabeth in England. An additional grant, dated 29 Oct 1690, was made to William Overton for 837 acres lyi
2 CONC ng in St. Peter's Parish, New Kent Co. (now Hanover) for transporting 17 persons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From "History and Genealogies", by W.H. Miller, published by Press of Transylvania Co., Lexington KY, 1907:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Temperance Overton, (the wife of William Harris) came to this country with three brothers and settled in Virginia. She was a daughter of William Overton, and Mary Waters, William Overton was a Colonel under Oliver Cromwell, and c
2 CONC ommanded one wing of the army at the battle of Dunbar; for some cause he was cast into the Tower of London by Cromwell, and died there." 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/OVERTON/2000-03/0952818215
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Hylbom Family Ancestry Project:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hylbom.com/family/paternal-lines/paternal-ni-to-po/overton-6106/
2 CONT
2 CONT The names of William Overton and (Mary) Elizabeth Waters‘ children have been compiled from “the Josephus Book,” and from Anderson, Early Descendants of William Overton and Elizabeth Waters; from Boddie, Historical Southern Familie
2 CONC s, Vol. V; and from “The Overton Family” by W. E. Dickinson in Richmond-Times Dispatch, 5 and 12 Sept 1915.  One account, published by Media Research Bureau before 1939, named nine children: “Mary, Barbara, James, John, William, A
2 CONC nn, Elizabeth, Temperance, and probably Samuel.”  However, most accounts contain only the six from Josephus (although gaps in the birthdates would allow for other children who did not survive).
2 CONT
2 CONT William‘s life in America was spent in the county of New Kent, where all records have been destroyed by fires.  Part of his land later fell into Mathews and King William counties, and his offspring resided on land which became Han
2 CONC over and Louisa counties.  Some descendants went to North Carolina, thence to Kentucky, and some remained in Kentucky for several generations before moving westward to Texas and other states.  Still another group migrated to Tenne
2 CONC ssee and Louisiana, where several became prominent in state affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT The record of the family of William Overton and Elizabeth (Mary) Waters, all born in New Kent County, Virginia is as follows (compiled from Anderson, Early Descendants; from “Overton of Virginia, North Carolina, Tennessee and Loui
2 CONC siana,” in Boddie’s Historical Southern Families, Vol. V; and from “The Overton Family” in Richmond-Times Dispatch 5 Sep 1915:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (the Anderson family records call her Mary), born 28 Jun 1673 may have married Robert Anderson.
2 CONT
2 CONT William, born 6 or 14 Aug 1675 married Margaret (Peggy) Garland.  He died 18 Jun 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT Temperance Overton, born 2 Mar 1679.  In about 1695 she married William Harris.  She died 19 Feb 1710, according to her grave marker at “Cedar Hill” in Hanover County, Virginia.  William Harris was born in Virginia.  After Tempera
2 CONC nce died, he married (2nd) Mary Butler [Ellis].
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel, born 14 Aug 1686 married Miss Carr[17]; he died before 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT James, born 14 Aug 1688 and died 18 Jun 1749.  In 1706 he married Elizabeth Garland (widow Truhart); she was born in 1690 and died 19 Nov 1739 [Carr Bible].  Margaret and Elizabeth Garland were sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara, born 5 Feb 1690 married James or John Winston and died in 1766.
2 CONT
2 CONT
