1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Harris family moved from Virginia to Pulaski Co. Kentucky between 1804 1nd 1805.  Mother Mary Anne Harris died in 1821 and sister Sarah's husband, Achilles, became guardian for the minor Harris children.  1821 
2 CONC - October PC KY - Court records, PC KY (4&5) 1816-1823 / 1820-1823 pages 171, 197, 198, 199  Property settlement - these proceedings were all done in Circuit Court in Chancery sitting  "This indenture of bargain and sale made on t
2 CONC he _day of October 1821 between Aaron Sargent, Commissioner for Lawrence Vanhook and Mary Ann, his wife, late Mary Ann Harris, Achilles Jasper and Sally, his wife, late Sally Harris, Rhoda Harris, Tyre Harris, Jemima Harris, Elemu
2 CONC el Harris and Benjamin Harris, heirs and legal representatives of Benjamin Harris deceased."  <http://kyharris.webs.com/timeline.htm>
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that Dabney Harris had a step brother Benjamin also born in 1766 in Albemarlie Co., VA but he had a different family. And since this Benjamin Harris had a farm next to Dabney, I assume it is his son as Find A Grave has 
2 CONC it:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=141039512
2 CONT
2 CONT This Harris genealogy begins with Benjamin Harris Sr. from about 1766/69 - 1815, he is the son of Dabney and Mary Harris Sr and grandson of Christopher and Mary Dabney/Harris of Albemarle Co., VA, then of Madison Co., KY. It is fa
2 CONC mily tradition that his wife ANNA was of Indian heritage. Our HARRIS branch migration pattern starts out in Washington Co., VA, and then during the winter of 1804 or spring of 1805 the family removed into Pulaski Co., KY. Some of 
2 CONC the HARRIS children remained in Jessamine, Garrard and Pulaski Co., KY after the death of their father in 1815, others moved on to MO, IN, IL, OK, OR, and areas in-between. Surnames related to Benj & Anna's children and grandchild
2 CONC ren are: JASPER, DEWEESE, SMITH, VANHOOK, BURKET, HALL, WILSON, BROWN, and CASH to name a few. We know that there was a very close relationship of our "Benjamin Harris Sr." with his brothers, Dabney Harris Jr. and Christopher Harr
2 CONC is. We know that our Harris Family was Baptist, per the "Old Fishing Creek Baptist Church" minutes where Ben and Anna were received by letter and Dabney Jr was received by experience. Ben's two sons, Tyre and Benjamin Jr., were Ba
2 CONC ptist ministers and both removed to Indiana, along with Dabney Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.webring.org/l/rd?ring=harris;id=1;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww%2Ekyharris%2Ewebs%2Ecom%2F
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://familysearch.org/tree/person/details/L6HB-6PS FamilySearch Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 27 2019, 3:53:03 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-40001-119475418/benjamin-harris-sr-in-familysearch-family-tree FamilySearch Family Tree] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 18 2023, 11:13:20
2 CONC  UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Harris Sr. (Harris DNA Group #6) was the s/o Dabney & Mary unk/Harris and grandson Of Christopher & Mary Dabney/Harris. He married Anna Unk in about 1795 in North Carolina. Benjamin 1st appears in Washington, VA in 1796 i
2 CONC n the lower district, he bought land 3 April 1799 - Grant No. 41 - Land Office Treasury Warrant #14964; pg 153. On Sep 15, 1804 Benjamin & Anna sold this land to Benjamin Wallis. Benjamin & Anna removed to Pulaski, KY as shown by 
2 CONC entry on May 9, 1805 where Benjamin posts surety for the marriage of Aaron Sargent. In 1805 Benjamin pays taxes on 58 acres located on Pitman Creek and has 2 horses. In August 1809 Pulaski, KY court minutes he was appointed to a g
2 CONC roup of road maintainers that included Read Jones, Isaac Ingram, Absolom Dodson. Benjamin’s neighbors while he lived in the Pitman Creek area were: William Waldens, Sr, Read Jones, Isaac Ingrams, Absolem Dodson, Parish Garners, He
2 CONC nry Garners, and Henry Francis. On Nov 28 1809 Benjamin was paid $3.00 for serving as a jail guard for 4 days. In 1810 Benjamin’s father Dabney Harris dies in Washington, VA & Benjamin’s younger brother Dabney Jr removes to Pulask
2 CONC i, KY to live with him as evidenced on the 1810 census. In 1811 Benjamin has moved to the Coldweather Creek area of Pulaski to 78 acres he purchased from Samuel Allen, that he paid in full Dec 17, 1814; Pulaski Deed Book 2: 1806-1
2 CONC 814: page 442. On 1814 August 27 – “Old” Fishing Creek Baptist Church minutes – pg. 9 Brother Benjamin Harris and Sister Anna his wife received by letter. 1815 May 26 - PCKY - Deeds 2: 483 - Benjamin Harris of Pulaski Co., KY, to 
2 CONC James Hainey of the same county for $20.00, 5 acres more/less in the bottom of Coldwater Creek, being in part of the said tract Harris now lives on, recorded on May 26, 1815. Christopher Harris brother of Benjamin removes from Was
2 CONC hington, VA along with his wife, children and mother Mary to Pulaski, KY and buys land near Benjamin on Pointer Creek.  June 25 1815 - “Old” Fishing Creek Baptist Church minutes – pg. 10   Bro. Benjamin Harris deseast – 18 July 18
2 CONC 15: 
2 CONT *Benjamin is buried at the Old Fishing Creek Cemetery.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/141039512/benjamin_harris Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 30 2024, 6
2 CONC :13:00 UTC''
