2 CONT !Richard Gentry, along with Christopher Harris and the famous Daniel Boone and others, is commemorated on a historical monument marking the site of Fort Boonesborough, 1775-1783. It reads: "In Memory of the Pioneers of Kentucky. E
2 CONC rected by the Boonesborough Chapter, D.A.R., 1907."
2 CONT
2 CONT !Madison County, KY, Deed Book G, pages 186-87, (Film # 183287) Harris Heirs to McMillen: "This indenture made the 2 day of December one thousand eight hundred & nine between Richard Gentry and Jane [his] wife, Christopher Harris,
2 CONC  John Harris, Thomas Burgan guardian to Polly Harris, infants of Benjamin Harris Decd, William Harris, Margaret Harris, John Bennet, and Isabel his wife, Samuel Harris, Barnabas Harris, & Overton Harris heirs & assigns of Christop
2 CONC her Harris decd of the one part of the County of Madison and State of Kentucky and Samuel McWilliams [sic] [&] James Guthrie of the state aforesaid of the other part, Witnesseth that the said Samuel McWilliams [struck out] McMilli
2 CONC n having...a certain tract or parcel of land lying in Bourbon County on Hinstons fork of licking containing twelve hundred acres...."
2 CONT
2 CONT !Richard Gentry was a soldier in the American Revolution. In source #1, above, it is stated that he "was proud of the fact that he had been a soldier of the Revolution--an American patriot. He religiously celebrated the Fourth of 
2 CONC July as a holiday for all his family as well as his slaves...He loved to relate that he was one of the guards that marched off the British prisoners after the surrender of Cornwallis at Yorktown...how his heart swelled with pride 
2 CONC while the ragged, barefooted Colonial troops marched off, as their prisoners, the splendidly uniformed British Regulars."
2 CONT
2 CONT !The book goes on to state, "He was for many years a member of the Baptist Church, but left it with most of his family in 1833, to join the new church of Alexander Campbell. He continued to prosper until he owned over 1000 acres o
2 CONC f fine blue grass land...He traded largely in mules, cattle and hogs, which he collected and sent to market on foot, driven by some of his sons, either to Cincinatti, Louisville, Richmond, Va., or Charleston, S.C."
2 CONT
2 CONT !It continues, "He was the father of 19 children, 16 sons and 3 daughters, and all but one lived to be grown and married; 12 by his first wife, Jane Harris, and 7 by his second wife, Nancy Guthrie, whom he married when he was 58 y
2 CONC ears old. She was a daughter of Nathaniel Guthrie, who came to Kentucky from Amherst Co, Va., in 1805, where she was born April 12, 1799, and married Richard Gentry Oct. 12, 1821. He died Feb. 12, 1843, and she married March 25, 1
2 CONC 854, Michael Farris, who died April 1, 1857. She applied for a pension March 23, 1858, as the former widow of Richard Gentry, which was allowed. She died at Sedalia, Mo., Dec. 16, 1881." [NOTE: Richard Gentry himself applied for a
2 CONC  soldier's pension in Madison County, KY, on 13 Aug 1832. His record contains his signature as well as narratives of the various campaigns in which he was involved. It states that "the applicant was again called out in the militia
2 CONC  from Albemarle & served till after the surrender of Cornwallis -- & then marched with the prisoners to Fredericksburg -- where he was discharged."]
2 CONT
2 CONT !Personal description of Richard Gentry: He "had light hair and blue eyes, and was of a florid complexion; he was over six feet in height, and weighed about 220 pounds. He was a great hunter, and most all of his sons loved the hou
2 CONC nds and the chase."
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL: Although Richard Gentry was originally buried on his farm in Kentucky, his remains were moved to Jefferson Barracks National Cemetery in St. Louis on 20 June 1958. He is one of only a few Revolutionary War veterans buried
2 CONC  there. (Source: Web site of Jefferson Barracks National Cemetery.)
2 CONT
