1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT SOURCES: 1) "The Gentry Family in America" by Richard Gentry (b. 1846); 2) Some information from FamilySearch and RootsWeb; 3) Registration form for National Register of Historic Places filed in 1996 on the William B. Hunt House, 
2 CONC 8939 W. Terrapin Hills Rd., Columbia, MO 65203. This 1860s house was built around David Gentry's two-room, 1-1/2 story log cabin, the walls of which are still visible in the current house's central hall and southwest parlor. The o
2 CONC lder structure dates to about 1832, when David bought the 320 acres on which he built it from the estate of Joel Shaw (Deed Book D, pp. 38-40). David's sons James and Fayette farmed this land in later years and sold the property i
2 CONC n 1852 sometime after David's death (Deed Book U, p. 655).
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM SOURCE NO. 1, ABOVE: "He moved from Kentucky to Missouri and lived for some years between Columbia and Rocheport in Boone Co. He removed to Monroe Co., Mo., about 1835, and settled a farm near Middle Grove. He died there abou
2 CONC t 1836, and his wife a few years later. His children sold the farm of about 400 acres to a Mr. Owenby. David Gentry served in the War of 1812 under Gen. Harrison on the Northern Lakes."
2 CONT
2 CONT DISCREPANCY: David Gentry, 50-60 years old, is listed on the 1840 census for Boone County, MO, with a female (a younger wife?) of 20-30 years old and an infant female in the household. Although several sources (as the item, above)
2 CONC  refer to his death in 1836, this census listing coupled with the fact that documentation exists in Monroe County, MO, court records of a suit for divorce filed by his wife, Susan, in 1836 indicate otherwise. Susan charged him wit
2 CONC h abandonment.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: David Gentry is found on a list of members of the Columbia (Missouri) Temperance Society of 1831 and later.
