2 CONT MH:S500010  Jerry Stambaugh  Stambaugh Web Site  <p>MyHeritage family tree</p><p>Family site: Stambaugh Web Site</p>Family tree: 337878451-1  337878451-1  Discovery 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT MH:SC500105  Rhodes Gentry  Rhodes Gentry  15 APR 2017  Added via a Person Discovery  3 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT MH:S500024  julia peterson  peterson Web Site  <p>MyHeritage family tree</p><p>Family site: peterson Web Site</p>Family tree: 386332002-1  386332002-1  Discovery 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT MH:SC500165  10 MAY 2017  Personal photo of Rhodes Gentry Added via a Photo Discovery™  3 
2 CONT Husband to Nancy Culbertson Gentry (2nd wife), children from this union: Amanda Rhodes Gentry James (1843-1913), Allie V. Gentry Farrell (1841-1911), Alexander Gentry (1845-?) and Samuel Rhodes Gentry (1839-1864). Son of Richard G
2 CONC entry who fought in the Revolutionary War and is buried at Jefferson Barracks Nat. Cem. His first wife was Allie Moore (1808-1835), there were four children from this union.  James Christy Gentry (1830-1896), Nancy Jane Gentry (18
2 CONC 28-1896), William Overton Gentry (1833-1901) and Mary Ann Gentry Davis(1835-1918). 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIO ON HIS FATHER: His father, Richard a Revoluntionary War veteran has two burial places--it seems he was exhumed and buried in the Jefferson National Cemetery in St. Louis, becoming the only Revolutionary War veteran buried ther
2 CONC e. The son of David Gentry (son of Nicholas, son of Nicholas) and Mary Estes (dau of Richard Estes and Mary Yancey/Yancy). He was a Revolutionary soldier, enlisting from Albemarle Cty., Virginia, and serving first for two months a
2 CONC s a private under Capt. William Dalton, at the age of 17. He was present at the capture of Cornwallis at Yorktown. Married (1) Jane Harris (in 1784)(dau of Christopher Harris and Agnes McCord); (2) Nancy Guthrie (in 1821)(dau of N
2 CONC athaniel Guthrie and Nancy Jane Johns); had 19 children (seven with second wife), 16 of whom were sons. He was 'wealthy in lands and slaves' after the 1786 move from Virginia to Kentucky via the Cumberland Gap. His wife is said to
2 CONC  have ridden the trip on horseback carrying her son Reuben in her arms. They settled at Boone's Fort, then established a plantation in Madison County. He boiled down salt water to salt and became wealthy in Kentucky, where salt wa
2 CONC s like money. Died on his estate in Madison County.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/71027386/rhodes_r_gentry Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 6 2024, 5:52:0
2 CONC 5 UTC''
