1 NAME Elizabeth /Spencer/
2 GIVN Elizabeth
2 SURN Spencer
2 _MARNM Hescott
1 SEX F
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth Anne (Hescott) Brown (daughter of Robert Hescott & Elizabeth Spencer) did not exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elizabeth (Spencer) Hescott''' (fl. late 17th century) was a colonial resident of Virginia. Her historical identity is preserved primarily through her marriage to Robert Hescott and her role as a maternal ancestor to several pr
2 CONC ominent Virginia families, including the Thompsons and the Browns of Albemarle.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family and Heritage ===
2 CONT Elizabeth married '''Robert Hescott''' in the late 17th century. While specific dates for her birth and death remain unverified due to the fragmented nature of early New Kent County records, she is traditionally placed within the 
2 CONC social and geographic context of the Virginia Tidewater.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her known child is:
2 CONT * '''Jane Hescott''' (c. 1687 – bef. 1734) – Married '''Robert Thompson, Jr.''' of St. Peter's Parish, New Kent County. Through Jane, Elizabeth is the maternal grandmother of Sarah (Thompson) Brown, the matriarch of the Brown's Co
2 CONC ve family.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes and Conflations ===
2 CONT The reconstruction of Elizabeth Spencer’s life requires careful forensic auditing to avoid several common digital genealogical errors:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Maternal Lineage Conflation:''' In many digital repositories, Elizabeth Spencer is frequently conflated with her daughter or granddaughters. Specifically, the Hescott surname is often mistakenly attributed as the maiden name 
2 CONC of the first wife of Benjamin Brown, Sr. Archival evidence—most notably the Reuben Dabney Brown records—clarifies that the first Mrs. Brown was a '''Wilson''', and the Hescott name belongs exclusively to the maternal line of the s
2 CONC econd wife, Sarah Thompson.
2 CONT * '''Surname Origins:''' The Spencer family was well-established in the Virginia colony during the 17th century (notably in Westmoreland and Surry Counties). While Elizabeth is often linked to high-status Spencer lineages in secon
2 CONC dary pedigrees, primary documentation (such as a will or deed of gift) linking her to a specific Spencer patriarch has not been recovered in the surviving administrative records of New Kent or Hanover Counties.
2 CONT * '''Geographic Accuracy:''' Her life was centered in the region that formed '''New Kent County'''. Many online profiles incorrectly list her death in Albemarle County; however, Albemarle was not settled by her descendants until d
2 CONC ecades after her likely death, and there is no record of her migrating to the Blue Ridge frontier.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT * '''St. Peter's Parish Register:''' New Kent County, Virginia.
2 CONT * '''Chamberlayne, C.G.:''' ''The Vestry Book and Register of St. Peter's Parish, New Kent and James City Counties, Virginia, 1684-1786.''
2 CONT * '''Brown Family Bible:''' Records of the Benjamin Brown family, including maternal Thompson/Hescott lines.
2 CONT * '''Forensic Identity Audit:''' Resolution of the Hescott/Wilson identity conflict in the Brown's Cove Master Profiles.
