1 NAME Robert /Hescott/
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN Hescott
2 _MARNM Hescott
1 SEX M
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth Anne (Hescott) Brown (daughter of Robert Hescott & Elizabeth Spencer) did not exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert Hescott''' (fl. late 17th – early 18th century) was a colonial Virginian whose identity is primarily established through the maternal lineage of the Thompson and Brown families of Hanover and Albemarle Counties. He is mo
2 CONC st frequently identified in archival reconstructions as the father of Jane Hescott, who married Robert Thompson, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family and Marriage ===
2 CONT Robert Hescott married '''Elizabeth Spencer'''. Due to the loss of many early colonial records (particularly "burnt" county records), specific dates for their marriage and vital events are often estimated based on the birth of the
2 CONC ir children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their primary documented issue includes:
2 CONT * '''Jane Hescott''' (c. 1687 – c. 1734) – Married '''Robert Thompson, Jr.''' in St. Peter's Parish, New Kent County. She is the mother of Sarah Thompson, the second wife of Benjamin Brown, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Geographic Presence ===
2 CONT The Hescott family was associated with the Tidewater and Central Piedmont regions of Virginia. Records for their descendants are concentrated in '''St. Peter's Parish (New Kent County)''' and later '''Hanover County'''. The migrat
2 CONC ion patterns of his children and grandchildren suggest Robert Hescott was part of the early wave of planters who expanded into the inland counties during the late 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes ===
2 CONT Forensic auditing of the Hescott name reveals that it is relatively rare in colonial Virginia records, which has led to several digital conflations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The "Elizabeth Anne" Error:''' Many digital trees mistakenly claim Robert Hescott had a daughter named "Elizabeth Anne" who was the first wife of Benjamin Brown, Sr. Archival evidence—including the Brown Family Bible—refutes 
2 CONC this, identifying the first wife as a '''Wilson'''. The confusion likely stems from the fact that Robert's daughter (Jane) was the mother of Benjamin's *second* wife.
2 CONT * '''Parentage and Origins:''' While some secondary pedigrees suggest an English origin (specifically Bristol or Gloucestershire), primary ship passenger lists or headright grants explicitly linking this Robert Hescott to a specif
2 CONC ic English parish have not been definitively verified.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT * '''St. Peter's Parish Register:''' New Kent County, Virginia (records of Thompson/Hescott associations).
2 CONT * '''Brown Family Bible:''' Hanover and Albemarle Counties, Virginia (contextualizing the maternal lineage).
2 CONT * '''Chamberlayne, C.G.:''' ''The Vestry Book and Register of St. Peter's Parish, New Kent and James City Counties, Virginia, 1684-1786.''
2 CONT * '''Genealogical Forensic Audit:''' Cross-referencing the Thompson-Brown-Dabney lineages to isolate the Hescott maiden name from conflated Wilson/Brown profiles.
