0 @N6000000002605198476@ NOTE Penelope was one of the survivors of a shipwrecked vessel sailing from the old world to the New.  She was picked up on the beach of Long Island by some merciful Indians and nursed back to health; her wounds were stitched by withes
1 CONC  of a tree and fishbone for a needle.  A reward which had been offered for any survivors that were brought in alive probably saved Penelope's life.  Her husband was among the lost passengers in this shipwreck which occurred off th
1 CONC e coast of Sandy Hook in 1640.
1 CONT
1 CONT Penlope met Richard Stout at New Amsterdam in 1644 and they moved to Gravesend, Long Island. After the Dutch surrendered New Amsterdam to the English in 1644 Richard along with five other men received a land grant in East Jersey f
1 CONC rom the Duke of York.  They founded the village of Middleton.  Richard became a member of the New Jersey Legislature (Assembly) in 1671.
0 @N6000000001586473566@ NOTE - Story about arrival extracted from the Historical collections of the state of New Jersey by John H Barker and Henry Howe published in 1847 and copied by hand by John Hyde Stout in 1893.  - Johnathan the ancestor of the Stouts ca
1 CONC me here from Middletown in this state in the year 1706; his family was one of the first three which settled on the tract now called Hopewell. The place was then a wilderness and full of Indians. The following account of the Stouts
1 CONC  is taken from Benedict's History of the Baptists. The family of the Stouts are so remarkable for their number, origin, and character, in both church and state, that their history deserved to be conspicuously recorded; and no plac
1 CONC e can be so proper as that of Hopewell, where the bulk of the family resides.  We have already saw that Johnathan Stout and family were the seed of the Hopewell church, and the beginning of the Hopewell settlement; and that of the
1 CONC  fifteen which constituted the church, nine were Stouts. The church was constituted at the house of a Stout, and the meetings were held chiefly at the dwellings of the Stouts for forty one years, viz, from the beginning of the set
1 CONC tlement to the building of the Meeting House before described.  Mr. heart was of the opinion (1790) that from first to last, half of the members have been and were of that name; for in looking over the church book (saith he) I fin
1 CONC d that near two hundred of the name have been added, besides about as many more of the blood of the Stouts, who had lost the name by marriages.  The present (1790) two deacons and four elders are Stouts, the late David and Zebulon
1 CONC  Stout, were two of its main pillars, the first lived to see his offspring multiplied into an hundred and seventeen souls.  The origin of this Baptist family is no less remarkable, for they all sprang from one woman, and she as go
1 CONC od as dead; her history is in the mouths of most of her posterity, and is a follows; She as born at Amsterdam about the year 1602; her father's name was Van Princess, she and her first husband (whose name is not known) sailed for 
1 CONC New York (then New Amsterdam) about the year 1620; the vessel was stranded at Sandy Hook; the crew got ashore, and marched towards the said New York, but Penelope (for that was her name) husband was hurt in the wreck, and could no
1 CONC t march with them;  therefore he and his wife tarried in the woods; they had not been long in the place before the Indians killed them both (as they thought) and stripped them to the skin; however Penelope came to, though her skul
1 CONC l was fractured, and left shoulder so hacked, that she could neveruse that one like the other; she was also cut across the abdomen, so that her bowels appeared; these she kept in with her hand; she continued in this situation for 
1 CONC seven days, taking shelter in a hollow tree, and eating the excrescence of it; the seventh day she saw a deer passing by with arrows sticking in it, and soon after two Indians appeared, whom she was glad to see, in hope they would
1 CONC  put an end to her misery, accordingly, one made towards her to knock her on the head; but the other who was an elderly man, prevented him; and throwing his matchcoat about her; carried her to his wigwam, and cured her of her woun
1 CONC ds and bruises.  After that he took her to New York, and made a present of her to her countrymen; by an Indian present, expecting ten times that value in return.  It was in New York, that one Richard Stout married her; he was a na
1 CONC tive of England, and of a good family.  She was now in her twenty second year and he in his fortieth. She bore him seven sons and three daughters, viz. Johnathan (founder of Hopewell), Joseph, Richard, James, Peter, David, Benjami
1 CONC n, Mary, Sarah, and Alice, the daughters married into the families of the Bounds, Pikes, Throckmontons, and Skeltons, and so lost the name of Stout.  The sons married into the families of Bulleu, Crawford, Ashton, Traux Le, and ha
1 CONC d many children.  The mother lived to the age of one hundred and ten, and saw her offspring multiplied into five hundred and two in about eighty eight years. - Copied from the history of New Jersey in 1893 by John Hyde Stout one o
1 CONC f the descendants of Richard Stout and Penelope Van Princess. -
0 @N6000000002217903034@ NOTE About 1642, Penelope and her 1st husband a Dutchman named Von Printzen boarded a ship in Amsterdam, Holland, and headed for New Amsterdam, now New York, in the new world.  The ship was met with unfavorable weather and was driven a
1 CONC shore at Sandy Hook, NJ.  The passengers managed to reach the beach with great difficulty.  Mr. Von Printzen was ill on most of the voyage and decided that he would remain on the beach until he felt better.  His wife, Penelope, st
1 CONC ayed with him, as the others made their way to the settlement of New Amsterdam.  The story goes that Indians found the couple, killed Mr. Von Printzen and severely wounded Penelope leaving her for dead.  She managed to creep to a 
1 CONC hollow tree where she remained several days before she was found by two Indians.  An argument took place between the older Indian who wanted to save the white woman and the younger Indian who wanted to kill her.  The old Indian pr
1 CONC evailed, threw her over his shoulder and took her to his teepee where he nursed her back to health.
1 CONT
1 CONT Samuel Smith in his book "The Colonial History of Nova Caesaria or New Jersey" originally published in 1765, states, "After some time the Dutch in New Amsterdam hearing of a white woman among the Indians concluded who it must be a
1 CONC nd some of them came to her relief."
