0 @N6000000008630596270@ NOTE Source:  "Descendants of Charles Coppedge," compiled by Carol Ansley (cjansley@worldnet.att.net), provided by Glenn E Young (younggandw@juno.com) to Paula Miller dated 7 April, 2003
0 @N6000000001177312320@ NOTE Ancestry World Tree,  Debra, dgwill@att.net, Williams & McMillin Families, 2007:
1 CONT
1 CONT DUKE WILLIAMS moved into Henderson County TN  late in his life and is buried in the Lexington Cemetery, Henderson Co., TN as indicated above. The burial location of his wife, EDE HARRIS WILLIAMS, is not known. [Henderson Co formed
1 CONC  1822; Decatur Co formed from it in 1845; Carroll Co formed 1870s; Chester Co formed in 1882; Madison Co formed in 1880s]
1 CONT
1 CONT Among DUKE WILLIAMS’ children, with EDE HARRIS WILLIAMS, his wife, was a daughter, whose tombstone reads: To the memory of our Mother, LYDIA HARRIS TAYLOR, born in Caswell Co., N.C. Oct.23, 1800; Died at Spring Creek, Tenn. Nov.16
1 CONC , 1831. On the right side of this tombstone is inscribed: Daughter of EDE HARRIS and DUKE WILLIAMS. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The SOUTHERN STATESMAN, Jackson, Nov. 19, 1831 mentions her death, as the wife of JESSE TAYLOR and had died Nov.16, leaving a widower and three children. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The only other tombstone in this old graveyard is that of LUCY BROWNING wife of Major JESSE TAYLOR, 1831-1887. The WEST TENNESSEE WHIG, Jackson, April 27, 1887, mentions the death of LUCY, wife of Major JESSE TAYLOR, died in Jacks
1 CONC on, April 25, 1887; burial at Spring Creek. These Taylors kept a girls’ school at Spring Creek in the 1870s."Colonel Taylor’s cemetery" is located in northeast Madison County; about 1.1 mile north of the crossroads in the village 
1 CONC of Spring Creek, at a point on U.S. Highway 70,turn west into a private driveway, drive past the frame house and at about .1 mile begin to walk to a high point in a field due west about .4 mile from U.S. Highway 70 to this cemeter
1 CONC y, terraced off from the surrounding ground. In Madison County deed book 4, page 85 this cemetery is called "Colonel Taylor’s graveyard," referring to Colonel SAMUEL TAYLOR, postmaster at Forked Deer Post Office, the first such of
1 CONC fice in the Spring Creek area and later he was Jackson’s first postmaster. Colonel Taylor died of tuberculosis, November 6,1825 in Jackson and was buried in the town’s oldest cemetery and his remains were later moved to what is no
1 CONC w Riverside Cemetery. Members of his family continued to bury at the family graveyard near Spring Creek.
