1 CONT .WILLIAMS, MARMADUKE, b. April 6, 1772, in Caswell co., N. C.; was a lawyer, and in congress from N. C. from 1803 to 1809; in 1810 removed to Madison co., Ala., and thence in 1818 to Tuscaloosa; was [p.429] repeatedly in the leg.,
1 CONC  and a delegate in the convention that formed the state constitution; was a candidate for gov. but not elected; in 1826 was appointed a com'r to adjust the unsettled accounts between Ala. and Miss. growing out of their territorial
1 CONC  connection; in 1832 elected judge of the county court, and held till April, 1842, when he resigned, having reached the age of 70; d. at Tuscaloosa, Ala., Oct. 29, 1850. Source: Ancestry.com: American Biographical Library - (9). S
1 CONC alt Lake City, UT: Ancestry Incorporated, 1996WILLIAMS, Marmaduke, jurist, was born in Caswell county, N.C., April 6, 1774; son of Nathaniel (q.v.) and Mary Ann (Williamson) Williams of Rockingham county, N.C. He received a good e
1 CONC ducation, became a lawyer, and made his first appearance in public life in 1802, when he represented for one term the county of Caswell in the North Carolina state senate. He succeeded his brother, Robert Williams (q.v.) as repres
1 CONC entative in congress, and was twice reelected, serving throughout the 8th, 9th and 10th congresses, from Oct. 17, 1803, to March 3, 1809. In 1810 he left North Carolina and located in Huntsville, Madison county, then in the Missis
1 CONC sippi Territory. In 1818, two years after the first settlement of "The Falls of the Black Warrior" (Tuscaloosa), Ala., he removed there and in the spring of 1819 was elected a member of the constitutional convention of Alabama fro
1 CONC m Tuscaloosa county. He was an active and useful member of the convention, and was the first member to move the expediency of the formation of a constitution and state government for Alabama. At the first state election under the 
1 CONC constitution he was defeated for governor of the state by William W. Bibb, then the territorial governor. He rendered conspicuous service in the Alabama legislature, of which he was a member in the sessions of 1821, 1822, 1825, 18
1 CONC 26, 1829, 1830, 1832, 1833, 1837, 1838, 1839. He was secretary of the board of trustees of the University of Alabama, from its incorporation in 1821 to 1840; and was judge of the Tuscaloosa county court, 1833-42. In 1826 he served
1 CONC  as commissioner to adjust the unsettled territorial accounts with Mississippi. On Dec. 25, 1798, he married Mrs. Agnes Payne Harris (first cousin to Dolly Payne Madison) daughter of Robert and Anne (Burton) Payne, of Pittsylvania
1 CONC  county, Va. She died Aug. 28, 1850, and husband and wife are buried in Tuscaloosa [p.420] Thomas M. Owen (q.v.) of Montgomery Ala., is a grandson. Judge Marmaduke Williams died in Tuscaloosa, Ala., Oct. 29, 1850. Source: Ancestry
1 CONC .com. Biographies of Notable Americans, 1904 [database online]. Orem, UT: MyFamily.com, Inc., 1997Cousin of John Williams, of Tennessee, Lewis Williams, and Robert Williams.
