1 CONT Great Register of Voters, Tulare County, California, 1892, page
1 CONT 3, #230:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bell, Isaac Thomas, age 48, 5'9", light complexion, blue eyes,
1 CONT light hair, born in Tennessee, resides Visalia.
1 CONT
1 CONT History of Central California Counties of Fresno, Tulare, and
1 CONT Kern, Lewis Publishing, Chicago, 1892, pages 637-638:
1 CONT
1 CONT Isaac T. Bell, one of the business men of Visalia, Tulare
1 CONT County, California, was born in Sumner County, Tennessee, July
1 CONT 17, 1844. His grandfather, Absalom Bell, emigrated from Scotland
1 CONT to this country and settled in Maryland. To him and his wife
1 CONT three children were born, two sons and one daughter. One of
1 CONT these sons, Tyree H. Bell, born in Tennessee in 1815, is the
1 CONT father of Isaac T. He married Mary Ann Walton, a native of
1 CONT Tennessee and a relative of the distinguished Sir Isaac Walton
1 CONT of England. To them nine children were born, seven of whom are
1 CONT living - all in California.
1 CONT
1 CONT The subject of our sketch was educated in one of the primitive
1 CONT log schoolhouses of old Tennessee and remained on the farm until
1 CONT lhe was sixteen years old, when he became a soldier on the
1 CONT Confederate side of the great civil war. He entered the service
1 CONT as a private in Company F, Seventh Tennessee Cavalry under
1 CONT General W H Jackson, and afterward under General N B Forest. He
1 CONT was promoted to aid-de-camp to General Tyree H Bell, his father,
1 CONT and served in that capacity till the close of the war. At the
1 CONT battle of Guntown, Mississippi, he was twice wounded.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the war Mr. Bell became a traveling salesman for a St.
1 CONT Louis firm, and continued thus employed untile 1868. About this
1 CONT time he was married to Miss Elizabeth Smith, a native of his own
1 CONT State. He then engaged in the mercantile business in Newbern and
1 CONT Lexington until 1874, when he was elected Circuit Clerk of the
1 CONT County of Henderson, and filled that office until 1878. From
1 CONT that time until 1883 he was again employed as a salesman. In the
1 CONT latter year he came to California, settled on the plains in
1 CONT Fresno County and engaged in farming. In 1886 he removed to
1 CONT Visalia and was clerk in the United States Land Office during
1 CONT the administration of President Cleveland, his father being
1 CONT receiver during that time.  Mr. Bell is now a member of the firm
1 CONT of Jeffuds & Bell, and is doing an extensive real-estate and
1 CONT land office business, both members of the firm being experienced
1 CONT in their line of work and having extensive tracts of land for
1 CONT sale.
1 CONT
1 CONT To Mr. and Mrs. Bell five children have been born, one of whom
1 CONT is deceased. those living are James W., William J., John Tyree,
1 CONT and Annie May.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Bell has been a member of the I.O.O.F. for twenty-four years
1 CONT and has passed all the chairs in the different branches of that
1 CONT order. He represented his lodge four years in Tennessee, and
1 CONT also represented the encampment in the Grand Lodge. He and his
1 CONT wife are members of the Methodist Episcopal Church South. He is
1 CONT a steward in the church and had been a superintendent of the
1 CONT Sunday school for fourteen years. By all who know him he is
1 CONT regarded as a useful, influential and enterprising citizen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Visalia Morning Delta, Thursday, June 4, 1914:
1 CONT
1 CONT DEATH CLAIMS WELL KNOWN VISALIA MAN
1 CONT
1 CONT I.T. Bell succumbs to Long Illness at Home in Visalia
1 CONT
1 CONT I. T. Bell, well known pioneer of this county passed away at his
1 CONT home on Goshen Avenue in this city Wednesday afternoon at 3
1 CONT o'clock. The deceased was past 77 years of age and leaves a host
1 CONT of friends who will mourn his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. I. T. Bell, wife of the deceased, William J. Bell of San
1 CONT Francisco, James
1 CONT
1 CONT Bell of Los Angeles and John Bell, sons of the deceased were
1 CONT present at the time of his death. Mrs. S. C. Dunlap, a daughter,
1 CONT is at present touring in the east and word could not be sent her
1 CONT as her address was unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT The funeral has been set fro Friday morning at 10 a.m. and will
1 CONT take place form the Bell residence on Goshen avenue. The
1 CONT services are in charge of Reverend Fenton of the Methodist
1 CONT Episcopal Church South.
1 CONT
1 CONT The deceased was a member of the Odd Fellows lodge and was in
1 CONT high standing of the Grand Encampment of the I.O.O.F. of which
1 CONT he held the office of Grand Senior Warden.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tulare County Death Records, Book 14, page 12, Cerftificate 22:
1 CONT
1 CONT Isaac Thomas Bell, date of death, 3 June 1914; date of birth 17
1 CONT July 1844; age 69 years, 10 months, 17 days; occupation, land
1 CONT lawer; place of birth, Tennessee; resided in Visalia, 28 years;
1 CONT resided in California, 31 years; informant, William J. Bell of
1 CONT San Francisco; cause of death, ascites of col. tumor of
1 CONT abdominal muscles; date of burial, 5 June 1914; blace of burial,
1 CONT Visalia City Cemetery, lot 17, block 13, Section A, grave south
1 CONT west 1.
0 @N6000000008630583115@ NOTE 1870 Census, Dyer County, Tennessee, 6th District, Newbern,
1 CONT pages 104-105, dwelling & family 328: Ruben Harrel, age 25,
1 CONT farmer, $3000 real estate, $6000 personal, b Tenn; Susan, age
1 CONT 22, keeping house, b. Tenn; Maggie age 3, b. Tenn; Maud, age 5
1 CONT months, b. Tenn.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1880 Census, Fresno County, California, ED 5, page 22, dwelling
1 CONT 133, family 140:  Reuben G Harrell, age 34, head Farmer, b.
1 CONT Tenn, mother b. Va; Susan B, age 32, wife, b. Tenn. father b.
1 CONT Ky; Maggie B., age 12, dau, at school, b. Tenn; Willie M. age
1 CONT 10, son, at school, b. Tenn; Myrtle, age 8, dau. at school, b.
1 CONT Tenn; Katie, age 1, b. Calif.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1900 Census, Fresno County, California, ED 5, page 10, 1261 O
1 CONT Street, Fresno, dwelling 227, family 268:   Reuben G Harrell,
1 CONT head, born Dec 1846, age 53, married 32 years, b. Tenn, US Land
1 CONT Agt.; Susan B, wife, born April 1849, age 50, married 32 years,
1 CONT mother of 6, 4 living, b. Tenn; Margaret B, dau, born Sept 1869,
1 CONT age 30, single, b. Tenn, clerk; Myrtle E, dau, born June 1872,
1 CONT age 27, single, b. Tenn, copyist; Kathryn M. dau, born Feb 1880,
1 CONT age 20, single; Tyree H Bell, father in law, born Sept. 1815,
1 CONT widowed, born in Ky, father born Md, Mother b. NC.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1920 Census, Fresno County, California, ED 43, page 2A, dwelling
1 CONT 28, family 29,  346 Van Ness, Fresno: Reuben G Harrell, head,
1 CONT age 74, married, lawyer, general practice; Susan B, wife, age
1 CONT 72, married; Myrtle E, dau, age 40, single, Deputy County Supt.
1 CONT Schls; Carrie Bell, niece, age 35, single, Bookkeeper, lumber
1 CONT Company. (she is the daughter of Josiah Walton Bell)
1 CONT
1 CONT History of Fresno County, With Biographical Sketches, Paul E
1 CONT Vandor, Historic Record Company, Los Angeles, 1919, pages 663-4:
1 CONT
1 CONT REUBEN B. HARRELL. - The old pioneers of Fresno County can best
1 CONT appreciate its gradual transition from a sterile spot on the
1 CONT landscape to its present floral wealth and luxuriance of plants,
1 CONT trees, and vines, which today greet the eye on every hand.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reuben G. Harrell is one of the old pioneers and recalls
1 CONT shooting doves on the present site of the city of Fresno when
1 CONT the county was in its infancy. He is a native of Gallatin,
1 CONT Tenn., born December 20, 1845. Reared on a Southern plantation
1 CONT in ante-bellum days, he received a common school education, and
1 CONT as a lad of fifteen, at the outbreak of our great civil strife
1 CONT in 1861, enlisted, serving one year as a scout on the
1 CONT Confecerate side. In 1862 he entered the regular service in the
1 CONT Forest Command of Cavalry, Army of the Tennessee, under Gen. T.
1 CONT H. Bell. For two years he was assistant adjutant and took part
1 CONT with the Army of the Tennessee in all the big battles,
1 CONT surrendering at Gainsville, Alabama, May 10, 1865, to Gen. R. N.
1 CONT Canby. after his return home he was employed in a store until
1 CONT 1875, then, hearing much about the resources of California and
1 CONT the attendent results for ambitious and energetic young men, he
1 CONT sought his fortune in the Golden State, arriving in Fresno the
1 CONT fall of 1875. from 1875 to 1882 he was engaged in the occupation
1 CONT of farming and stock-raising, and from 1882 to 1885 was in a
1 CONT general merchandise store in Fresno. For eight years he served
1 CONT as deputy county assessor for Fresno County, and since 1885 has
1 CONT practiced law in the United States Land Courts, connected with
1 CONT the United States Department of the Interior. He has been of
1 CONT great assistance to homesteaders locating in the Valley.
1 CONT
1 CONT December 20, 1866, he was united in marriage with Miss Susie B.
1 CONT Bell, a native of Tennessee and daughter of Gen. T. H. Bell, the
1 CONT commander of Mr. Harrell's old regiment. Their union was blessed
1 CONT by the birth of seven children, four of whom are living:
1 CONT Margaret, the wife of Dr. D. C. Farnham of San Francisco;  Maud,
1 CONT the wife of the attorney, D. E. Perkins of Visalia, is the
1 CONT mother of two daughters; Catherine, married Dr. E. M. Doyle of
1 CONT Sacrament; and Myrtle E., who is at home, is an artist of note,
1 CONT and is now chief deputy county school superintendent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Harrell have enjoyed more than fifty years of
1 CONT domestic happiness, and on December 20, 1916, surrounded by
1 CONT their children, friends and neighbors, celebrated their golden
1 CONT wedding anniversary. Mr. Harrel is a notary public and is
1 CONT trustee of the Southern Methodist Church. He is held in high
1 CONT esteem by his fellow citizens.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fresno Morning Republican, May 23, 1923:
1 CONT
1 CONT PIONEER TO BE BURIED TODAY
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. Harrell's Funeral to be in Visalia
1 CONT
1 CONT VISALIA, May 22 - Funeral services for Mrs. Susan Bell Harrell,
1 CONT 75, who died yesterday in Fresno following a brief illness will
1 CONT be conducted from St. Paul's Episcopal church here tomorrow
1 CONT morning at 10 o'clock, Rev. R. A. Griesser, rector, officiating.
1 CONT Mrs. Harrell was one of the real pioneers of Visalia. She was
1 CONT the mother of Mrs. D. E. Perkins of this city, Miss Myrtle
1 CONT Harrell, Fresno, Mrs. D. C. Farnham, San Francisco, Mrs. Ernst
1 CONT Boyle, Sacramento, and a sister to Mrs. C. A. Ledsinger, Los
1 CONT Angeles, and J. W. Bell, Los Angeles. She was born in Tennessee
1 CONT and with her husband, R. G. Harrell, moved to Fresno County in
1 CONT 1875 before the city of Fresno had started. Later they came to
1 CONT Visalia.
0 @N6000000008630587846@ NOTE living in Sumner County on Nashville Pike in 1930 ref Kleber
1 CONT obit
0 @N6000000008630587815@ NOTE Sumner County News, Thursday, October 16, 1930., page 1
1 CONT
1 CONT Kleber M. Bell
1 CONT
1 CONT Kleber Miller Bell died at 4:30 o'clock a. m. Wednesday, october
1 CONT 15th, 1930, at the home of his sister, Mrs. George Doubleday, on
1 CONT the Nashville Pike. He had been ill for several months.
1 CONT
1 CONT The deceased was born in this county on September 19th, 1847,
1 CONT the son of Thomas and Sophia Green Bell, pioneer citizens of
1 CONT this section.  Early in life he removed to Fresno, California,
1 CONT where he remained until about twenty years ago when he returned
1 CONT to Sumner county. He had since resided here.  Mr. Bell was an
1 CONT excellent citizen, quiet and unassuming, honest and upright in
1 CONT all his business dealings. He was never married.
1 CONT
1 CONT There survive him four brothers and three sisters, as follows:
1 CONT Judge B.D. Bell of gallatin, Rev. J.W. Bell of Verona,
1 CONT Mississippi, Tyrell (sic) L. Bell of Dallas Texas, and Thomas H.
1 CONT Bell of Oklahoma City; Mrs. F. H. Dunklin and Mrs. George
1 CONT Doubleday of this county, and Mrs. R. E. Douglass of Nashville.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Bell was a member of the Methodist Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Funeral services will be conducted at the residence of Mrs.
1 CONT Doubleday at 2 o'clock this afternoon (Thursday) by Rev. W. A.
1 CONT Adams. Burial will follow at lthe old Bell burying ground on
1 CONT Station Camp Creek near Douglass Chapel.
